ADDART({id:"senegalcountry",t:"Senegal",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌅",w:"Senegal",r:["sahel","slavetrade","africacolon","mali","soccer","moroccocountry"],
p:[["Africa’s western tip","Senegal includes the westernmost point of mainland Africa."],["Teranga","Senegalese people are known for teranga, a deep tradition of hospitality."],["Peaceful politics","Senegal is one of the few African countries that has never had a military coup."]],
l:"Senegal is a country on the Atlantic coast of West Africa, with about 18.5 million people. Its capital, Dakar, lies on the Cap-Vert peninsula, the westernmost point of mainland Africa. Senegal almost surrounds the small country of the Gambia. Once home to the Jolof Empire and a center of French colonial rule in West Africa, it became independent in 1960 under the poet-president Léopold Sédar Senghor. Senegal is known for its stable democracy, its music, its Sufi Muslim brotherhoods and the national value of teranga, or hospitality.",
s:[["Land","Senegal is mostly flat, with dry Sahel grasslands in the north and greener savanna and forests in the south, in the region of Casamance. The Senegal River forms its northern border with Mauritania, and the Gambia River flows through the Gambia, which reaches deep into Senegal’s territory."],["Early history","The region was part of the empires of Ghana and Mali, and from the 1300s the Jolof Empire of the Wolof people ruled much of it. Islam spread through trade and scholars. Portuguese sailors arrived in the 1400s, followed by Dutch, British and French traders."],["The colonial era","The French founded Saint-Louis in 1659, and Gorée Island off Dakar became a trading post linked to the Atlantic slave trade. In 1902 Dakar became the capital of French West Africa. Senegalese soldiers, the tirailleurs sénégalais, fought for France in both world wars."],["Independence","Senegal became independent in 1960, first briefly in the Mali Federation and then on its own. Léopold Sédar Senghor, a celebrated poet of the Négritude movement, was president for 20 years. Senegal has held regular elections, and power has passed peacefully between parties, most recently in 2024."]],
k:[["Capital","Dakar"],["Population","About 18.5 million (2024)"],["Area","About 197,000 km²"],["Highest point","Near Népen Diakha, about 648 m"],["Currency","West African CFA franc"]],
d:[["1350s","The Jolof Empire is founded"],["1659","The French found Saint-Louis"],["1902","Dakar becomes the capital of French West Africa"],["1960","Senegal becomes independent; Senghor is president"],["2000","Power passes peacefully to an opposition party for the first time"],["2002","Senegal reaches the World Cup quarter-finals"],["2024","Senegal begins producing oil"]],
s2:[["Gorée Island","Gorée, a small island off Dakar, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a place of remembrance of the Atlantic slave trade. Its House of Slaves, with its Door of No Return facing the sea, is visited by people from around the world, including many of African descent seeking to honor their ancestors."],["Faith","About 95 percent of Senegalese are Muslim, most belonging to Sufi brotherhoods such as the Mourides, founded by Amadou Bamba, and the Tijaniyya. Each year millions of pilgrims travel to the holy city of Touba for the Grand Magal. There is also a Christian minority, and Senegal is known for religious tolerance."],["Music and arts","Senegal is famous for mbalax, a lively music mixing Wolof drumming with modern styles, made world famous by the singer Youssou N’Dour. Griots preserve history in song, and Dakar hosts a major biennial of contemporary African art."],["Food","Senegal’s national dish, thiéboudienne, of fish, rice and vegetables cooked in tomato sauce, is recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage. Yassa, chicken or fish with onions and lemon, and mafé, a peanut stew, are also popular, and sweet bissap drink is made from hibiscus flowers."],["Sport","Football is hugely popular. In 2002 Senegal beat France, the defending champions, in its first World Cup match and reached the quarter-finals, and in 2022 it won the Africa Cup of Nations for the first time. Traditional Senegalese wrestling, laamb, draws huge crowds."],["Economy","Senegal’s economy relies on fishing, peanuts, phosphates, tourism and services. Large offshore oil and gas fields began production in 2024, raising hopes for growth and debates over how to share the benefits. Many young Senegalese seek work abroad, and money they send home supports families."]],
sp:["The African Renaissance Monument","On a hill overlooking Dakar stands the African Renaissance Monument, a bronze statue of a man, a woman and a child gazing out to sea. Completed in 2010 to mark 50 years of independence, it is about 49 meters tall, taller than the Statue of Liberty without its pedestal. It was intended as a symbol of Africa rising from centuries of slavery and colonialism, but it also caused controversy over its cost, its North Korean builders and its style. Today it is one of Dakar’s most visited landmarks."],
g:[["Teranga","The Wolof word for hospitality"],["Griot","A West African storyteller and musician"],["Sufi brotherhood","A Muslim religious order following a spiritual leader"],["Mbalax","A popular Senegalese dance music"],["Thiéboudienne","Senegal’s national dish of fish and rice"]],
f:["Dakar is on the westernmost point of mainland Africa.","Senegal has never had a military coup.","Thiéboudienne is recognized by UNESCO."],
fun:"Senegal’s national dish, thiéboudienne, is on UNESCO’s list of intangible cultural heritage."});

ADDART({id:"ugandacountry",t:"Uganda",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦍",w:"Uganda",r:["nile","lakes","kenyacountry","tanzaniacountry","drccountry","africacolon"],
p:[["The Pearl of Africa","Uganda is famous for its green landscapes, lakes and mountains."],["Source of the Nile","The White Nile flows out of Lake Victoria in Uganda."],["Great apes","About half of the world’s mountain gorillas live in Uganda."]],
l:"Uganda is a landlocked country in East Africa, crossed by the equator, with about 50 million people. Its capital is Kampala. Uganda shares Lake Victoria, Africa’s largest lake, with Kenya and Tanzania, and the White Nile begins its long journey north there. Its forests shelter about half of the world’s mountain gorillas. Home to ancient kingdoms such as Buganda, Uganda became a British protectorate and gained independence in 1962. After years of dictatorship and conflict in the 1970s and 1980s, it has grown more stable, and it hosts one of the largest refugee populations in Africa.",
s:[["Land","Uganda lies on a high plateau, with a mild climate despite being on the equator. Its lakes include Victoria, Albert, Edward and Kyoga, and the Rwenzori Mountains, the Mountains of the Moon, rise on the border with the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with glaciers near their snowy peaks."],["Kingdoms","Before colonial rule, the region was home to powerful kingdoms, including Buganda, Bunyoro, Toro and Ankole. The Kingdom of Buganda, ruled by a king called the kabaka, was well organized, with roads and a parliament of chiefs. Traditional kingdoms were restored in 1993 as cultural institutions."],["Colonial rule and independence","Britain made Uganda a protectorate in 1894, governing largely through local rulers, especially Buganda. Uganda became independent on 9 October 1962, with Milton Obote as prime minister. Tensions between the central government and the kingdoms soon led to conflict."],["Years of turmoil","In 1971 the army officer Idi Amin seized power. His rule was marked by brutality, with estimates of 100,000 to 500,000 people killed, and in 1972 he expelled tens of thousands of Ugandans of South Asian descent. He was overthrown in 1979 with help from Tanzania. More conflict followed until Yoweri Museveni’s National Resistance Army took power in 1986."]],
k:[["Capital","Kampala"],["Population","About 50 million (2024)"],["Area","About 242,000 km²"],["Highest point","Margherita Peak, 5,109 m"],["Currency","Ugandan shilling"]],
d:[["1862","John Hanning Speke reaches the source of the White Nile"],["1894","Uganda becomes a British protectorate"],["1962","Uganda becomes independent"],["1971","Idi Amin seizes power"],["1979","Amin is overthrown"],["1986","Yoweri Museveni’s forces take power"],["2006","Oil is discovered near Lake Albert"]],
s2:[["Mountain gorillas","Bwindi Impenetrable National Park and Mgahinga Gorilla National Park are home to about half of the world’s mountain gorillas. Strict protection and gorilla tourism, which brings income to local communities, have helped their numbers grow. Uganda also has chimpanzees, tree-climbing lions and many birds."],["The source of the Nile","In 1862 the British explorer John Hanning Speke identified the place where the Nile flows out of Lake Victoria near Jinja as the river’s source. Local people had long known the place. Jinja is now a center for whitewater rafting on the Nile."],["Politics today","Yoweri Museveni has led Uganda since 1986. Supporters credit his government with restoring peace and stability, economic growth and a strong early response to HIV and AIDS. Critics point to the removal of presidential term and age limits, pressure on opposition politicians and the press, and long rule by one leader."],["Refugees","Uganda hosts well over a million refugees, mainly from South Sudan and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Its policies, which allow refugees to work, move freely and farm land, have been praised internationally, though resources are stretched."],["Food and culture","Matooke, green cooking bananas, steamed and mashed, is a staple. A popular street snack, the rolex, is an omelet rolled in a chapati. Uganda has dozens of ethnic groups and languages; English and Swahili are official, and Luganda is widely spoken."],["Faith and sport","Most Ugandans are Christian, with a significant Muslim minority. Each 3 June, huge crowds gather at Namugongo to honor the Uganda Martyrs, young Christians killed in 1885 to 1887. In athletics, Joshua Cheptegei won Olympic gold in the 10,000 meters in 2024 and set world records."]],
sp:["The Pearl of Africa","When the young Winston Churchill traveled through Uganda in 1907, he was so impressed by its beauty, fertile land and rich wildlife that he wrote in his book My African Journey that Uganda was the pearl of Africa. The phrase stuck, and Ugandans proudly use it today in tourism and national pride. Its green hills, crater lakes, forests and the snowy Rwenzori peaks continue to amaze visitors."],
g:[["Protectorate","A territory controlled and protected by another country"],["Kabaka","The king of Buganda"],["Matooke","Cooked green bananas, a Ugandan staple"],["Refugee","A person forced to flee their country"],["Equator","An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth"]],
f:["The White Nile begins at Lake Victoria in Uganda.","About half of the world’s mountain gorillas live in Uganda.","Uganda hosts over a million refugees."],
fun:"The rolex, a popular Ugandan snack, is an omelet rolled in a chapati."});

ADDART({id:"angolacountry",t:"Angola",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦌",w:"Angola",r:["africacolon","slavetrade","brazilcountry","drccountry","southafricacountry","coldwar"],
p:[["A large country","Angola is one of the largest countries in Africa, on the Atlantic coast."],["Oil and diamonds","It is rich in oil and diamonds."],["Peace after war","A long civil war ended in 2002."]],
l:"Angola is a large country in southern Africa, on the Atlantic coast, with about 38 million people. Its capital is Luanda. Once home to the kingdoms of Kongo and Ndongo, Angola was a Portuguese colony for centuries and a major source of enslaved Africans taken to Brazil. It became independent in 1975, but a civil war, part of the Cold War, lasted until 2002. Angola is one of Africa’s largest oil producers and a major source of diamonds, though many of its people remain poor. Portuguese is its official language.",
s:[["Land","Angola has a narrow coastal plain rising to a high central plateau. The north is covered with tropical forest, while the south is drier, merging into the Namib Desert. The enclave of Cabinda, rich in oil, is separated from the rest of the country by a strip of the Democratic Republic of the Congo."],["Kingdoms","The Kingdom of Kongo, with its capital at Mbanza Kongo, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, ruled the north from the 1300s. To the south, the Kingdom of Ndongo was led in the 1600s by Queen Njinga, who resisted Portuguese conquest for decades through war and diplomacy."],["The colonial era","The Portuguese navigator Diogo Cão reached the Congo River in 1483, and Luanda was founded in 1576. For three centuries, Angola was a center of the Atlantic slave trade, with millions of people shipped across the ocean, mostly to Brazil. Portugal later expanded its control inland."],["Independence and war","An armed struggle for independence began in 1961. After Portugal’s 1974 revolution, Angola became independent on 11 November 1975. Rival movements, the MPLA and UNITA, then fought a civil war, supported by the Soviet Union and Cuba on one side and the United States and South Africa on the other. Hundreds of thousands died before peace came in 2002."]],
k:[["Capital","Luanda"],["Population","About 38 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1,247,000 km²"],["Highest point","Morro do Moco, 2,620 m"],["Currency","Kwanza"]],
d:[["1483","Portuguese navigator Diogo Cão arrives"],["1576","Luanda is founded"],["1624","Njinga becomes ruler of Ndongo"],["1961","The war for independence begins"],["1975","Angola becomes independent"],["2002","The civil war ends"],["2017","A new president succeeds José Eduardo dos Santos after 38 years"]],
s2:[["Queen Njinga","Njinga Mbande, queen of Ndongo and Matamba, was a brilliant diplomat and military leader who resisted Portuguese expansion in the 1600s. A famous story says that when a Portuguese governor offered her no chair at a meeting, she had a servant kneel to serve as her seat. Today she is a national hero and a symbol of resistance."],["Landmines","The civil war left millions of landmines across Angola, injuring and killing many people. In 1997 Diana, Princess of Wales, walked through a minefield being cleared in Huambo, drawing world attention to the issue and helping lead to an international treaty banning landmines. Clearance continues today."],["Oil and the economy","Angola is one of Africa’s largest oil producers, and oil makes up most of its exports. It was a member of OPEC from 2007 until 2024. Diamonds are also important. Oil wealth built gleaming towers in Luanda, but inequality is high, and governments have tried to diversify the economy and tackle corruption."],["Music and dance","Angola is the birthplace of semba, a rhythmic music and dance that influenced Brazilian samba, and of kizomba, a slow, romantic partner dance now popular worldwide. Kuduro, an energetic dance music, emerged in Luanda in the 1990s."],["Nature","Angola has spectacular landscapes, including the Kalandula Falls, the Tundavala cliffs and the deserts of Namibe. The giant sable antelope, or palanca negra, found only in Angola and once thought extinct during the war, is a national symbol."],["Links with Brazil and Portugal","Angola shares the Portuguese language with Brazil, Portugal, Mozambique and other countries. Its historic ties with Brazil, through the slave trade, are reflected in shared music, food, religion and words."]],
sp:["The giant sable antelope","The giant sable antelope, with its long, curved horns, lives only in central Angola. During the long civil war, no one could study it, and many feared it had been wiped out by hunting. In 2004, scientists using camera traps in Cangandala National Park photographed a small surviving herd. Conservationists have since protected and bred the animals. Called palanca negra in Portuguese, it appears on Angola’s national football team’s nickname, the Palancas Negras, and is a powerful symbol of the country’s survival."],
g:[["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Civil war","A war between groups within the same country"],["Landmine","An explosive device hidden in the ground"],["OPEC","An organization of oil-exporting countries"],["Semba","A traditional Angolan music and dance"]],
f:["Angola became independent in 1975.","Its civil war ended in 2002.","Kizomba dance comes from Angola."],
fun:"Angola’s national football team is nicknamed the Giant Sable Antelopes."});

ADDART({id:"zimbabwecountry",t:"Zimbabwe",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🪨",w:"Zimbabwe",r:["zimbabwe","waterfalls","southafricacountry","africacolon","mandela","inflation"],
p:[["Houses of stone","Zimbabwe is named after Great Zimbabwe, an ancient city of stone."],["A mighty waterfall","It shares one of the world’s greatest waterfalls with Zambia."],["Many languages","Zimbabwe recognizes 16 official languages."]],
l:"Zimbabwe is a landlocked country in southern Africa, between the Zambezi and Limpopo rivers, with about 16.6 million people. Its capital is Harare. It takes its name from Great Zimbabwe, a stone city built from the 1000s. Colonized by the British as Rhodesia, it was ruled by a white minority government until a liberation war led to independence in 1980. Under Robert Mugabe, it faced political repression, land seizures and economic collapse, including record hyperinflation. Zimbabwe is known for Victoria Falls, its wildlife, stone sculpture and high literacy.",
s:[["Land","Most of Zimbabwe lies on a high plateau with a pleasant climate. The Zambezi River forms its northern border, with Victoria Falls and Lake Kariba, one of the world’s largest artificial lakes. Eastern Highlands rise to Mount Nyangani. National parks such as Hwange have large herds of elephants."],["Great Zimbabwe","From about the 1000s to the 1400s, the Shona people built Great Zimbabwe, a city of massive stone walls built without mortar. It was the center of a trading kingdom that exchanged gold and ivory for goods from as far as China and Persia. Its name means houses of stone."],["Rhodesia","In 1890 Cecil Rhodes’s British South Africa Company took control of the region, which was named Rhodesia. African land was taken for white settlers. In 1965, Ian Smith’s white minority government declared independence from Britain to avoid majority rule, a move not recognized internationally."],["Liberation and independence","African nationalist movements fought a liberation war through the 1970s. After talks in London in 1979, elections were held, and Zimbabwe became independent on 18 April 1980, with Robert Mugabe as prime minister and later president."]],
k:[["Capital","Harare"],["Population","About 16.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 391,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Nyangani, 2,592 m"],["Currency","Zimbabwe Gold (ZiG) and US dollar"]],
d:[["1000s","Building of Great Zimbabwe begins"],["1855","David Livingstone sees Victoria Falls"],["1890","The British South Africa Company takes control"],["1965","Rhodesia declares independence under white minority rule"],["1980","Zimbabwe becomes independent"],["2008","Hyperinflation reaches record levels"],["2017","Robert Mugabe resigns after 37 years in power"]],
s2:[["Victoria Falls","On the Zambezi River, shared with Zambia, Victoria Falls is one of the largest waterfalls in the world, about 1.7 kilometers wide. Its local name, Mosi-oa-Tunya, means the smoke that thunders, after its towering spray. The Scottish missionary David Livingstone named it after Queen Victoria in 1855."],["The Mugabe years","Robert Mugabe led Zimbabwe for 37 years. Early on, his government expanded schools and health care, giving Zimbabwe one of Africa’s highest literacy rates. But in the 1980s, the Gukurahundi massacres in Matabeleland killed thousands, and later years brought repression of opponents and disputed elections. In 2017 the army intervened, and Mugabe resigned."],["Land reform and hyperinflation","From 2000, the government seized many white-owned commercial farms to redistribute land to Black Zimbabweans. Supporters saw it as correcting colonial injustice; critics noted that it was chaotic, often benefited the politically connected and caused farm output to collapse. Inflation soared, reaching billions of percent in 2008, and the country abandoned its own currency for years."],["Stone sculpture","Zimbabwe is famous for stone sculpture, carved from serpentine and other stones. Shona sculptors gained international fame from the 1950s, and their works are displayed in museums worldwide. The soapstone Zimbabwe Bird, found at Great Zimbabwe, is the national emblem."],["Music","The mbira, a thumb piano with metal keys on a wooden board, is central to Shona music and ceremonies and is recognized by UNESCO. Thomas Mapfumo created chimurenga music, blending mbira rhythms with electric guitars, which became the soundtrack of the liberation struggle."],["People and languages","The Shona are the largest group, followed by the Ndebele. Zimbabwe’s constitution recognizes 16 official languages, among the most of any country, including English, Shona, Ndebele and Zimbabwean Sign Language. Many Zimbabweans work abroad, especially in South Africa and Britain."]],
sp:["The hundred-trillion-dollar note","In 2008, prices in Zimbabwe were doubling roughly every day. Shoppers carried piles of banknotes in bags, and prices on shelves changed several times daily. The central bank printed ever-larger notes, and in 2009 it issued a 100-trillion-dollar note, with 14 zeros, one of the highest denominations ever printed. Even it could buy little more than a loaf of bread or a bus ticket. Soon after, Zimbabwe stopped using its own currency and adopted the US dollar and other foreign currencies. Today the old notes are sold as souvenirs."],
g:[["Hyperinflation","Extremely rapid rise in prices"],["Mbira","A thumb piano with metal keys"],["Liberation war","A war fought to free a country from colonial or minority rule"],["Mortar","A paste used to bind bricks or stones"],["Plateau","A high, flat area of land"]],
f:["Zimbabwe means houses of stone.","Victoria Falls is shared with Zambia.","Zimbabwe has 16 official languages."],
fun:"Zimbabwe once printed a 100-trillion-dollar banknote."});

ADDART({id:"ecuadorcountry",t:"Ecuador",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐢",w:"Ecuador",r:["galapagos","andes","amazon","inca","perucountry","colombiacountry"],
p:[["On the equator","Ecuador is named after the equator, which passes through it."],["Four worlds","It has Pacific coast, Andes mountains, Amazon rainforest and the Galápagos Islands."],["Rights for nature","Ecuador was the first country to give nature legal rights in its constitution."]],
l:"Ecuador is a country on the northwest coast of South America, named for the equator, which crosses it. About 18 million people live there, and its capital, Quito, high in the Andes, is one of the highest capital cities in the world. Ecuador packs remarkable variety into a small area: Pacific beaches, snow-capped volcanoes, Amazon rainforest and the Galápagos Islands, which inspired Charles Darwin. Once part of the Inca Empire and Spanish colonial rule, it became independent in 1822. It is the world’s largest exporter of bananas and uses the US dollar.",
s:[["Land","Ecuador has three mainland regions: the Costa, the Pacific coastal lowlands; the Sierra, the Andes highlands with volcanoes such as Cotopaxi and Chimborazo; and the Oriente, the Amazon rainforest in the east. The Galápagos Islands lie about 1,000 kilometers offshore."],["Ancient peoples","Ecuador has some of the oldest cultures in the Americas. The Valdivia culture on the coast made pottery more than 5,000 years ago. Later peoples built towns and traded across the Andes. In the late 1400s, the Inca conquered the region, and the Inca ruler Atahualpa had strong ties to Quito."],["Spanish rule and independence","Spanish conquistadors conquered the region in the 1530s, founding Quito in 1534 on an Inca city. Quito became a center of art and religion. On 24 May 1822, Antonio José de Sucre won the Battle of Pichincha near Quito, securing independence. Ecuador was part of Gran Colombia until 1830, when it became a separate republic."],["Modern Ecuador","Ecuador has had periods of political instability, military rule and economic crisis. After a severe crisis, it adopted the US dollar as its currency in 2000. Oil, bananas, shrimp, flowers and cacao are major exports. In recent years, rising crime linked to drug trafficking has become a serious challenge."]],
k:[["Capital","Quito"],["Population","About 18.1 million (2024)"],["Area","About 280,000 km²"],["Highest point","Chimborazo, 6,263 m"],["Currency","US dollar"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BCE","The Valdivia culture makes pottery"],["1534","The Spanish found Quito"],["1822","The Battle of Pichincha secures independence"],["1830","Ecuador becomes a separate republic"],["1835","Charles Darwin visits the Galápagos"],["2000","Ecuador adopts the US dollar"],["2008","A new constitution grants rights to nature"]],
s2:[["The Galápagos Islands","The Galápagos, a group of volcanic islands, are home to giant tortoises, marine iguanas, blue-footed boobies and finches found nowhere else. Charles Darwin’s visit in 1835 helped inspire his theory of evolution. The islands became Ecuador’s first national park in 1959 and one of the first UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 1978."],["Chimborazo","Because the Earth bulges at the equator, the summit of Chimborazo, an inactive volcano, is the point on Earth’s surface farthest from the center of the Earth, even farther than the summit of Everest, although Everest is higher above sea level."],["Rights of nature","In 2008 Ecuador became the first country to recognize the rights of nature in its constitution, giving ecosystems such as rivers and forests the right to exist and regenerate. Courts have used this to protect forests and rivers. In 2023, voters chose to stop oil drilling in part of the Yasuní National Park in the Amazon."],["Panama hats","The famous Panama hat actually comes from Ecuador. Woven from toquilla straw, especially around the town of Montecristi, the hats were shipped through Panama and worn by workers building the Panama Canal, giving them their name. The weaving tradition is recognized by UNESCO."],["Peoples","Most Ecuadorians are mestizo, of mixed Indigenous and European descent. Indigenous peoples, such as the Kichwa of the highlands and the Shuar and Waorani of the Amazon, have strong movements defending their lands and cultures. Afro-Ecuadorians live mainly in the province of Esmeraldas."],["The middle of the world","North of Quito stands the Ciudad Mitad del Mundo, a monument marking the equator, where visitors stand with one foot in each hemisphere. Modern GPS shows the actual equator is about 240 meters away, and a nearby museum claims to mark the true line."]],
sp:["The farthest point from Earth’s center","Mount Everest is the highest mountain above sea level, at 8,849 meters. But the Earth is not a perfect sphere: it bulges at the equator because of its rotation. Chimborazo in Ecuador, at 6,263 meters, sits almost on the equator, on top of this bulge. As a result, its summit is about 6,384 kilometers from the Earth’s center, roughly 2 kilometers farther than the summit of Everest. Climbers on Chimborazo’s peak are, in a sense, the people closest to the stars."],
g:[["Equator","An imaginary line around the middle of the Earth"],["Dollarization","Using a foreign currency, such as the US dollar, as a country’s own"],["Mestizo","A person of mixed Indigenous and European ancestry"],["Volcano","An opening in the Earth’s crust through which lava erupts"],["Rights of nature","The legal idea that nature has rights"]],
f:["Ecuador is named after the equator.","Ecuador uses the US dollar.","Panama hats come from Ecuador."],
fun:"The top of Chimborazo is the farthest point on Earth from the planet’s center."});

ADDART({id:"boliviacountry",t:"Bolivia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦙",w:"Bolivia",r:["andes","inca","perucountry","chilecountry","brazilcountry","spanish"],
p:[["High in the Andes","Bolivia is one of the highest and most rugged countries in the world."],["Many peoples","Most Bolivians are Indigenous or of mixed Indigenous descent."],["A mirror of salt","The Salar de Uyuni is the largest salt flat on Earth."]],
l:"Bolivia is a landlocked country in central South America, with about 12.4 million people. Its constitutional capital is Sucre, while the government sits in La Paz, the highest seat of government in the world. Bolivia stretches from the high Andes and Altiplano plateau to the lowland Amazon rainforest. It has a large Indigenous population, especially Quechua and Aymara, and its constitution recognizes 36 Indigenous languages alongside Spanish. Home to the ancient city of Tiwanaku and the silver mountain of Potosí, it became independent in 1825 and is named after Simón Bolívar.",
s:[["Land","Western Bolivia is dominated by the Andes and the Altiplano, a high plateau at about 3,700 meters, with Lake Titicaca and the Salar de Uyuni. To the east, the land drops to the Yungas valleys and then to tropical lowlands and the Amazon rainforest, which cover most of the country."],["Tiwanaku and the Inca","Tiwanaku, near Lake Titicaca, was the center of a powerful civilization from about 500 to 1000 CE, known for its stone gateways and monuments. Later, the region became part of the Inca Empire. Quechua and Aymara cultures remain strong today."],["Silver and Spain","The Spanish conquered the region in the 1530s. In 1545 they found vast silver deposits in the mountain of Cerro Rico at Potosí, which became one of the largest and richest cities in the world. Indigenous people were forced to work in the mines under a harsh labor system called the mita, and many died."],["Independence","Bolivia became independent on 6 August 1825 and was named after the liberator Simón Bolívar. In the War of the Pacific from 1879 to 1884, it lost its coastline to Chile, becoming landlocked. It also lost territory to Brazil and, in the Chaco War of 1932 to 1935, to Paraguay."]],
k:[["Capitals","Sucre (constitutional), La Paz (seat of government)"],["Population","About 12.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1,099,000 km²"],["Highest point","Nevado Sajama, 6,542 m"],["Currency","Boliviano"]],
d:[["c. 500 CE","Tiwanaku rises near Lake Titicaca"],["1545","Silver is found at Potosí"],["1825","Bolivia becomes independent"],["1884","Bolivia loses its coastline to Chile"],["1932","The Chaco War with Paraguay begins"],["1952","The Bolivian National Revolution"],["2006","Evo Morales becomes the first Indigenous president"]],
s2:[["Salar de Uyuni","The Salar de Uyuni, covering more than 10,000 square kilometers, is the world’s largest salt flat, the remains of ancient lakes. After rain, a thin layer of water turns it into a giant mirror reflecting the sky. Beneath it lie some of the world’s largest lithium resources, valuable for batteries."],["Lake Titicaca","Lake Titicaca, shared with Peru, lies at about 3,800 meters and is often called the highest navigable lake in the world. According to Inca legend, the first Inca rulers emerged from its waters. Aymara communities live on its islands and shores."],["Politics","In 2006 Evo Morales, an Aymara former coca farmers’ leader, became Bolivia’s first Indigenous president. A new constitution in 2009 renamed the country the Plurinational State of Bolivia. Supporters credit his government with reducing poverty and empowering Indigenous people; critics accused it of weakening democratic institutions. In 2019 he resigned after protests over a disputed election, an event that supporters describe as a coup and opponents as a response to electoral fraud."],["La Paz","La Paz sits in a deep canyon at about 3,600 meters, with the city of El Alto on the plateau above. Since 2014, a network of cable cars called Mi Teleférico, the largest urban cable car system in the world, has carried commuters over the steep city."],["Culture","Bolivia is famous for festivals such as the Carnival of Oruro, recognized by UNESCO, with spectacular devil dances. Indigenous women known as cholitas, in bowler hats and layered skirts, have become symbols of pride, and some even compete as wrestlers. Llamas and alpacas are raised in the highlands."],["A navy without a sea","Despite being landlocked, Bolivia keeps a navy that patrols Lake Titicaca and its rivers. Every 23 March, Bolivia marks the Day of the Sea, remembering the coastline it lost to Chile. In 2018 the International Court of Justice ruled that Chile was not obliged to negotiate giving Bolivia sea access."]],
sp:["The mountain that ate men","The Cerro Rico, or rich mountain, towering over the city of Potosí, contained one of the greatest silver deposits ever found. From the 1500s, its silver flowed to Spain and around the world, helping create the first truly global trade, as silver coins reached China and the Philippines through the Manila galleons. But the price was terrible: many Indigenous and enslaved African workers died in its mines, and it became known as the mountain that eats men. Miners still work its tunnels today, and the city of Potosí is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],
g:[["Altiplano","A high plateau in the Andes"],["Landlocked","Having no coastline"],["Plurinational","Made up of many nations or peoples"],["Mita","A forced labor system used in colonial mines"],["Lithium","A light metal used in batteries"]],
f:["Bolivia is named after Simón Bolívar.","The Salar de Uyuni is the largest salt flat on Earth.","Bolivia has a navy despite having no coast."],
fun:"Silver from Bolivia’s Potosí reached the Philippines on the Manila galleons."});

ADDART({id:"jamaicacountry",t:"Jamaica",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎵",w:"Jamaica",r:["reggae","slavetrade","athletics","ukcountry","cubacountry","coffee"],
p:[["Island of music","Jamaica gave the world reggae, ska and dancehall music."],["Fastest people","Jamaican sprinters, such as Usain Bolt, are among the fastest people ever."],["Out of many, one people","Jamaica’s motto celebrates its mixed heritage."]],
l:"Jamaica is an island country in the Caribbean Sea, south of Cuba, with about 2.8 million people. Its capital is Kingston. First home to the Taíno people, the island was colonized by Spain and then Britain, which built sugar plantations worked by enslaved Africans. Jamaica became independent in 1962. Though small, it has had a huge influence on world culture through reggae music and Bob Marley, the Rastafari movement and its world-beating sprinters. Its national motto is Out of Many, One People.",
s:[["Land","Jamaica is the third-largest island in the Caribbean. The Blue Mountains rise in the east, famous for their coffee, and limestone hills and caves cover much of the interior. Beaches and coral reefs line the coast, especially on the north and west."],["The Taíno and Spain","The Taíno people called the island Xaymaca, meaning land of wood and water. Christopher Columbus arrived in 1494, and Spain colonized the island from 1509. The Taíno population was devastated by disease and forced labor."],["British rule and slavery","England captured Jamaica in 1655. It became one of the richest sugar colonies in the world, worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans. Enslaved people resisted constantly. The Maroons, who escaped to the mountains, fought the British and won treaties in 1739. Slavery was abolished in 1834, with full freedom in 1838."],["Independence","After the Morant Bay Rebellion of 1865 and long struggles for rights, Jamaica gained self-government and independence on 6 August 1962. It remains a member of the Commonwealth with the British monarch as head of state, though there is debate about becoming a republic."]],
k:[["Capital","Kingston"],["Population","About 2.8 million (2024)"],["Area","About 11,000 km²"],["Highest point","Blue Mountain Peak, 2,256 m"],["Currency","Jamaican dollar"]],
d:[["1494","Christopher Columbus arrives"],["1655","England captures Jamaica from Spain"],["1692","An earthquake destroys Port Royal"],["1739","The Maroons sign treaties with the British"],["1838","Enslaved people gain full freedom"],["1962","Jamaica becomes independent"],["2009","Usain Bolt sets the 100-meter world record"]],
s2:[["Reggae","Jamaican music developed from mento to ska in the late 1950s, then rocksteady and, by the late 1960s, reggae, with its offbeat rhythm. Bob Marley and the Wailers made reggae famous worldwide with songs about love, unity and justice. Reggae was recognized by UNESCO in 2018. Dancehall music and sound systems influenced hip hop."],["Rastafari","The Rastafari movement emerged in Jamaica in the 1930s, honoring the Ethiopian emperor Haile Selassie and emphasizing African identity and repatriation. Known for dreadlocks and the colors red, gold and green, it spread worldwide through reggae music."],["Sprinting","Jamaica is a sprinting powerhouse. Usain Bolt set world records of 9.58 seconds in the 100 meters and 19.19 seconds in the 200 meters in 2009 and won eight Olympic gold medals. Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce and Elaine Thompson-Herah are among the greatest female sprinters ever."],["Food","Jamaica’s national dish is ackee and saltfish. Jerk, meat marinated in a spicy blend of allspice and Scotch bonnet peppers and cooked slowly over a fire, is famous worldwide. Patties, rice and peas, and Blue Mountain coffee are also loved."],["National heroes","Jamaica honors seven national heroes, including Nanny of the Maroons, an 18th-century leader of resistance; Sam Sharpe and Paul Bogle, leaders of rebellions; Marcus Garvey, who inspired Black pride movements worldwide; and Norman Manley and Alexander Bustamante, founders of modern Jamaica."],["Hurricanes","Jamaica lies in the path of Atlantic hurricanes. In October 2025, Hurricane Melissa, one of the strongest Atlantic hurricanes on record, struck the island, causing widespread destruction, especially in the west, and a major recovery effort followed."]],
sp:["The bobsled team","In 1988, Jamaica, a tropical island with no snow, sent a bobsled team to the Winter Olympics in Calgary, Canada. The team, made up of sprinters and army members, had trained on wheeled sleds. They crashed during their final run but walked their sled across the finish line to cheers. Their story inspired the 1993 film Cool Runnings. Jamaican bobsledders have continued to compete in Winter Olympics since then."],
g:[["Reggae","A Jamaican music with a strong offbeat rhythm"],["Maroons","Escaped enslaved people who formed free communities"],["Rastafari","A religious and cultural movement born in Jamaica"],["Jerk","A spicy Jamaican method of seasoning and cooking meat"],["Commonwealth","An association of countries, most once part of the British Empire"]],
f:["Jamaica became independent in 1962.","Usain Bolt holds the 100-meter world record.","Reggae was recognized by UNESCO in 2018."],
fun:"Jamaica sent a bobsled team to the 1988 Winter Olympics."});

