ADDART({id:"portugalcountry",t:"Portugal",c:"Countries and Places",i:"⛵",w:"Portugal",r:["explor","spaincountry","brazilcountry","angolacountry","magellan","soccer","vascodagama"],
p:[["Explorers’ homeland","Portuguese sailors led Europe’s Age of Discovery."],["A global language","Portuguese is spoken by more than 250 million people on four continents."],["Carnation Revolution","A peaceful revolution in 1974 ended decades of dictatorship."]],
l:"Portugal is a country on the western edge of Europe, on the Iberian Peninsula, with about 10.7 million people, including the Atlantic islands of the Azores and Madeira. Its capital is Lisbon. One of Europe’s oldest nations, with borders largely unchanged since the 1200s, Portugal led the Age of Discovery in the 1400s and 1500s, building a vast empire from Brazil to Africa and Asia. After a long dictatorship ended in the peaceful Carnation Revolution of 1974, it became a democracy and joined the European Union. Portuguese is spoken worldwide.",
s:[["Land","Mainland Portugal has mountains and forests in the north and rolling plains, cork oak forests and sunny beaches in the south, in the Algarve. The Tagus and Douro rivers cross the country. The Azores, with Portugal’s highest mountain, Mount Pico, and Madeira are volcanic islands in the Atlantic."],["Birth of a kingdom","After centuries of Roman, Visigothic and Muslim rule, Afonso Henriques declared himself king, and Portugal was recognized as a kingdom in 1143. Its alliance with England, dating from 1373, is the oldest alliance in the world still in force."],["The Age of Discovery","Encouraged by Prince Henry the Navigator, Portuguese sailors explored the coast of Africa. Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488, Vasco da Gama reached India by sea in 1498 and Pedro Álvares Cabral reached Brazil in 1500. Portugal set up trading posts from Africa to Goa, Malacca, Macau and Japan, and it took part heavily in the Atlantic slave trade."],["Dictatorship and democracy","Portugal became a republic in 1910. From 1933, António de Oliveira Salazar led the authoritarian Estado Novo regime, which fought long wars against independence movements in its African colonies. On 25 April 1974, army officers overthrew the regime almost without bloodshed. Portugal granted independence to its colonies and joined the European Community in 1986."]],
k:[["Capital","Lisbon"],["Population","About 10.7 million (2024)"],["Area","About 92,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Pico (Azores), 2,351 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["1143","Portugal is recognized as a kingdom"],["1498","Vasco da Gama reaches India by sea"],["1755","A great earthquake destroys Lisbon"],["1910","Portugal becomes a republic"],["1974","The Carnation Revolution"],["1986","Portugal joins the European Community"],["2016","Portugal wins the European football championship"]],
s2:[["The Lisbon earthquake","On 1 November 1755, a massive earthquake, followed by a tsunami and fires, destroyed much of Lisbon and killed tens of thousands of people. The disaster shook European thinkers’ ideas about God and nature, and the city was rebuilt with some of the first earthquake-resistant buildings."],["The Portuguese language","Portuguese is the official language of Portugal, Brazil, Angola, Mozambique, Cape Verde, Guinea-Bissau, São Tomé and Príncipe, Timor-Leste and Equatorial Guinea, and is spoken in Macau. With well over 250 million speakers, it is one of the most spoken languages in the world. José Saramago won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1998."],["Fado","Fado is a soulful music of Lisbon and Coimbra, sung with Portuguese guitars, often expressing saudade, a feeling of longing for something lost. Amália Rodrigues made it famous worldwide. Fado was recognized by UNESCO in 2011."],["Tiles and food","Portugal is famous for azulejos, painted ceramic tiles, often blue and white, that decorate churches, stations and houses. Its food includes bacalhau, dried salted cod, said to have hundreds of recipes, grilled sardines and the custard tart pastel de nata. Port wine comes from the Douro valley."],["Cork","Portugal produces about half of the world’s cork, harvested from the bark of cork oak trees every nine years without cutting down the trees. Cork is used for bottle stoppers, floors, shoes and even spacecraft heat shields."],["Links with Asia","Portuguese traders were the first Europeans to reach Japan and established Macau, which they returned to China in 1999. Portuguese words entered Asian languages, such as pan for bread in Japanese. Ferdinand Magellan, who led the first voyage to reach the Philippines in 1521, was Portuguese, though he sailed for Spain."]],
sp:["The Carnation Revolution","Shortly after midnight on 25 April 1974, a Portuguese radio station played a banned song, Grândola, Vila Morena. It was a secret signal for young army officers to begin a coup against the dictatorship. Crowds poured into the streets of Lisbon to support the soldiers, and a woman named Celeste Caeiro, carrying red carnations from a restaurant, handed them to soldiers, who placed them in their gun barrels. The revolution, which ended almost without bloodshed, became known as the Carnation Revolution, and 25 April is now Freedom Day in Portugal."],
g:[["Iberian Peninsula","The peninsula in southwestern Europe shared by Spain and Portugal"],["Fado","A traditional, soulful Portuguese song"],["Saudade","A Portuguese word for deep longing"],["Azulejo","A painted ceramic tile"],["Estado Novo","The authoritarian regime that ruled Portugal from 1933 to 1974"]],
f:["Portugal’s alliance with England dates from 1373.","Portugal produces about half the world’s cork.","Fado was recognized by UNESCO in 2011."],
fun:"Soldiers put carnations in their gun barrels during Portugal’s 1974 revolution."});

ADDART({id:"norwaycountry",t:"Norway",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏔️",w:"Norway",r:["viking","swedencountry","iceage","arctic","amundsen","ev"],
p:[["Land of fjords","Norway’s coast is cut by deep, narrow inlets called fjords."],["Midnight sun","In the far north, the sun does not set in summer."],["Oil wealth","Norway saves its oil money in one of the world’s largest funds."]],
l:"Norway is a country in northern Europe, on the western side of the Scandinavian Peninsula, with about 5.6 million people. Its capital is Oslo. Famous for its dramatic fjords, mountains, midnight sun and northern lights, Norway was the homeland of many Vikings. After centuries of union with Denmark and then Sweden, it became fully independent in 1905. Since oil was discovered in the North Sea in 1969, Norway has become one of the richest countries in the world, saving its oil income in a huge national fund. The Nobel Peace Prize is awarded in Oslo.",
s:[["Land","Norway is long and narrow, with a coastline so indented that, including fjords and islands, it stretches about 100,000 kilometers. Mountains cover much of the country. The far north lies above the Arctic Circle, where the sun stays up all night in summer and barely rises in winter. Norway also governs the Arctic islands of Svalbard."],["The Vikings","From the late 700s, Norwegian Vikings sailed to raid, trade and settle, reaching the British Isles, Iceland, Greenland and even North America. Around 872, Harald Fairhair is said to have united Norway into one kingdom. Christianity spread in the 1000s."],["Unions and independence","From 1380 Norway was united with Denmark for over four centuries. In 1814 Norway adopted its own constitution on 17 May, now its national day, but was forced into union with Sweden. In 1905 Norway peacefully became fully independent. During World War II, Germany occupied Norway from 1940 to 1945."],["Oil and the welfare state","Oil was discovered in the North Sea in 1969. Norway created a national oil company and, in 1990, a fund to save oil revenues for future generations. Norway has a strong welfare state, with free education and health care, and regularly ranks among the best countries in the world for quality of life."]],
k:[["Capital","Oslo"],["Population","About 5.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 324,000 km² (mainland)"],["Highest point","Galdhøpiggen, 2,469 m"],["Currency","Norwegian krone"]],
d:[["793","Vikings raid Lindisfarne, England"],["c. 872","Harald Fairhair unites Norway"],["1814","Norway adopts its constitution"],["1905","Norway becomes fully independent"],["1940","Germany invades Norway"],["1969","Oil is discovered in the North Sea"],["1994","Voters reject joining the European Union"]],
s2:[["Fjords","Fjords are deep, narrow sea inlets carved by glaciers during the ice ages and later flooded by the sea. The Geirangerfjord and Nærøyfjord are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The Sognefjord, more than 200 kilometers long, is the longest in Norway."],["The oil fund","Norway’s Government Pension Fund Global, built from oil and gas income, is the world’s largest sovereign wealth fund, worth well over a trillion US dollars, owning small shares in thousands of companies worldwide. Strict rules limit how much the government can spend each year, and the fund avoids investing in some companies on ethical grounds."],["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of northern Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia, traditionally known for reindeer herding, fishing and joik singing. Norway once tried to force them to give up their language and culture. Since 1989 the Sámi have had their own elected parliament in Norway."],["Electric cars","Norway leads the world in electric cars. Thanks to tax breaks and other incentives, the great majority of new cars sold in Norway in recent years have been fully electric, a share far higher than in any other country."],["Explorers and sport","Norway produced famous polar explorers, such as Fridtjof Nansen and Roald Amundsen, who reached the South Pole first in 1911. Skiing was invented in Scandinavia thousands of years ago, and Norway has won more Winter Olympic medals than any other country."],["Outside the EU","Norway is not a member of the European Union; its voters rejected membership in 1972 and 1994. However, it belongs to the European Economic Area, follows many EU rules and is a founding member of NATO."]],
sp:["A Christmas tree for London","Every year since 1947, the city of Oslo has given a large Norway spruce to the people of London, as thanks for Britain’s support during World War II, when Norway’s king and government lived in exile in London. The tree, often over 20 meters tall, is cut in a forest near Oslo, shipped across the North Sea and set up in Trafalgar Square, where it is decorated with lights in the traditional Norwegian way. Its lighting ceremony is a beloved London tradition."],
g:[["Fjord","A long, narrow sea inlet between steep cliffs"],["Midnight sun","The sun visible at midnight in summer near the poles"],["Sovereign wealth fund","A state-owned investment fund"],["Sámi","The Indigenous people of the far north of Europe"],["Arctic Circle","An imaginary line around the far north of the Earth"]],
f:["Norway became fully independent in 1905.","The Nobel Peace Prize is awarded in Oslo.","Most new cars sold in Norway are electric."],
fun:"Oslo has sent a Christmas tree to London every year since 1947."});

ADDART({id:"switzerlandcountry",t:"Switzerland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"⌚",w:"Switzerland",r:["alps","dunant","un","germanycountry","francecountry","italycountry"],
p:[["Mountain nation","The Alps cover much of Switzerland."],["Four languages","Switzerland has four national languages: German, French, Italian and Romansh."],["Neutral country","Switzerland has stayed neutral in wars for over 200 years."]],
l:"Switzerland is a small, landlocked country in central Europe, with about 9 million people. Bern is its federal city and seat of government, while Zurich and Geneva are its largest economic and international centers. Famous for the Alps, lakes, chocolate, cheese, watches and banks, Switzerland has four national languages and a unique system of direct democracy in which citizens vote on many laws. It has been neutral in international wars since 1815 and hosts many international organizations, including the Red Cross and the United Nations in Geneva.",
s:[["Land","The Alps cover about 60 percent of Switzerland, with peaks such as the Matterhorn and the Jungfrau. The Swiss Plateau between the Alps and the Jura Mountains holds most of the people and cities. Large lakes include Lake Geneva and Lake Constance, and great rivers, such as the Rhine and the Rhône, begin in Swiss glaciers."],["Founding","According to tradition, in 1291 three Alpine communities, Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden, swore an oath of alliance to defend their freedom, founding the Swiss Confederation. Legends tell of William Tell, an archer forced to shoot an apple off his son’s head. Over centuries, other regions joined."],["Neutrality","After centuries as fierce soldiers, the Swiss turned to neutrality. Their permanent neutrality was recognized by European powers at the Congress of Vienna in 1815. Switzerland stayed out of both world wars, though its dealings with Nazi Germany, including accepting looted gold, were later criticized."],["A modern federation","A constitution in 1848 created a federal state of cantons, which have considerable self-government. A seven-member Federal Council governs together, and the presidency rotates every year. Switzerland joined the United Nations only in 2002 and is not a member of the European Union."]],
k:[["Federal city","Bern"],["Population","About 9 million (2024)"],["Area","About 41,000 km²"],["Highest point","Dufourspitze, 4,634 m"],["Currency","Swiss franc"]],
d:[["1291","The Swiss Confederation is founded, according to tradition"],["1815","Swiss neutrality is recognized"],["1848","A federal constitution is adopted"],["1863","The Red Cross is founded in Geneva"],["1971","Women gain the right to vote in federal elections"],["2002","Switzerland joins the United Nations"],["2016","The Gotthard Base Tunnel opens"]],
s2:[["Direct democracy","Swiss citizens vote several times a year in national referendums on laws and constitutional changes. If enough citizens sign a petition, they can force a vote on a new proposal. Topics range from taxes and immigration to bans on certain products. Women gained the vote in federal elections only in 1971."],["Languages","About 60 percent of Swiss people speak German, mostly Swiss German dialects, about a quarter speak French, around 8 percent Italian and less than 1 percent Romansh, a language descended from Latin. Many Swiss speak several languages, and the country’s name on coins and stamps is the Latin Helvetia."],["Geneva and the world","Geneva is home to the Red Cross, founded in 1863 by Henry Dunant, the European headquarters of the United Nations, the World Health Organization and many other organizations. The Geneva Conventions, rules for protecting people in war, are named after the city. CERN, the particle physics laboratory, is near Geneva."],["Watches, chocolate and cheese","Switzerland is famous for precision watches, made in Geneva since the 1500s and in the Jura Mountains since the 1700s, for milk chocolate, invented by a Swiss chocolatier in 1875, and for cheeses such as Emmental and Gruyère. Fondue, melted cheese shared from a pot, is a national dish."],["Tunnels and trains","Switzerland has one of the world’s best railway networks. The Gotthard Base Tunnel, opened in 2016, runs 57 kilometers under the Alps and is the longest railway tunnel in the world. Mountain railways climb to high peaks such as the Jungfraujoch."],["Glaciers","Switzerland has the most glaciers in the Alps, including the Aletsch Glacier, the largest. Rising temperatures are making them melt rapidly, and they have lost a large share of their ice in recent decades, threatening water supplies and causing landslides."]],
sp:["The Swiss Army knife","In 1891, the Swiss army needed a folding pocket knife for its soldiers that could cut food, open cans and take apart their rifles. Swiss craftsman Karl Elsener supplied the knives and soon designed an improved officer’s knife with more tools. His company, Victorinox, named after his mother, Victoria, and later another company made the famous red-handled knives. With blades, scissors, screwdrivers and many other tools, the Swiss Army knife became a worldwide symbol of handy design and even traveled into space with astronauts."],
g:[["Canton","A state of the Swiss federation"],["Neutrality","Not taking sides in wars"],["Referendum","A public vote on a law or proposal"],["Romansh","A language descended from Latin spoken in eastern Switzerland"],["Glacier","A slowly moving mass of ice"]],
f:["Switzerland has four national languages.","Swiss women gained the federal vote in 1971.","The Gotthard Base Tunnel is the world’s longest railway tunnel."],
fun:"The Swiss Army knife was designed in 1891 for Swiss soldiers."});

ADDART({id:"vanuatucountry",t:"Vanuatu",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌋",w:"Vanuatu",r:["pacific","volc","fijicountry","solomoncountry","climchange","pngcountry"],
p:[["Islands of fire","Vanuatu is a chain of volcanic islands in the South Pacific."],["Many languages","With over 100 languages, it has more languages per person than any other country."],["Leaps of faith","Vanuatu’s land divers inspired the modern bungee jump."]],
l:"Vanuatu is an island country in the South Pacific Ocean, made up of about 80 islands stretching in a Y shape, with about 330,000 people. Its capital is Port Vila, on the island of Efate. Settled about 3,000 years ago by the Lapita people, ancestors of many Pacific Islanders, it later became the New Hebrides, jointly ruled by Britain and France, before independence in 1980. Vanuatu has active volcanoes, coral reefs and the highest number of languages per person in the world. It faces cyclones, earthquakes and rising seas and has become a leading voice on climate change.",
s:[["Land","Vanuatu’s islands are volcanic, with rugged mountains, rainforests and coral reefs. Several volcanoes are active, including Mount Yasur on Tanna, one of the most accessible active volcanoes in the world, and Ambae and Ambrym. The country sits on the Pacific Ring of Fire."],["First settlers","The Lapita people, skilled seafarers known for their decorated pottery, reached Vanuatu about 3,000 years ago. A cemetery at Teouma on Efate holds some of the oldest Lapita remains. Later waves of people brought new cultures, creating great diversity."],["Europeans and blackbirding","The Spanish explorer Pedro Fernández de Quirós reached the islands in 1606, and James Cook named them the New Hebrides in 1774. In the 1800s, many ni-Vanuatu were taken, often by force or deception, to work on plantations in Australia and Fiji, a practice called blackbirding."],["The Condominium and independence","From 1906 Britain and France ruled the islands jointly in an unusual arrangement called the Condominium, with two governments, two police forces and two school systems. Vanuatu became independent on 30 July 1980."]],
k:[["Capital","Port Vila"],["Population","About 330,000 (2024)"],["Area","About 12,200 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Tabwemasana, 1,879 m"],["Currency","Vatu"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","Lapita settlers arrive"],["1606","Pedro Fernández de Quirós reaches the islands"],["1774","James Cook names them the New Hebrides"],["1906","Britain and France begin joint rule"],["1980","Vanuatu becomes independent"],["2015","Cyclone Pam devastates the islands"],["2025","The International Court of Justice gives a climate opinion requested by Vanuatu"]],
s2:[["Languages","Vanuatu has more than 100 Indigenous languages for its small population, the highest density of languages in the world. The national language is Bislama, a creole based mainly on English, and English and French are also official. Many people speak several languages."],["Land diving","On Pentecost Island, men perform the nagol, or land diving, jumping from wooden towers up to about 30 meters high with vines tied to their ankles. The ritual is linked to the yam harvest. Visitors who saw it inspired the modern sport of bungee jumping."],["Kastom","Many ni-Vanuatu live in villages following kastom, traditional ways of life, including chiefs, ceremonies, gardening and exchange. Sand drawing, making patterns in sand with one continuous line to tell stories and send messages, is recognized by UNESCO."],["Cyclones and earthquakes","In 2015 Cyclone Pam, one of the strongest storms ever recorded in the South Pacific, destroyed homes and crops across Vanuatu. In 2023 two severe cyclones struck within days, and in December 2024 a strong earthquake damaged Port Vila."],["Climate leadership","Vanuatu has led efforts to address climate change internationally. It led a campaign for the United Nations to ask the International Court of Justice for an advisory opinion on countries’ climate obligations. In July 2025 the court said that states have legal obligations to protect the climate system."],["Cargo cults","On Tanna, the John Frum movement began in the 1930s and grew during World War II, when islanders saw the vast supplies of American troops. Followers believed a figure named John Frum would bring wealth. The movement continues as a religion and political party today."]],
sp:["Mount Yasur, the lighthouse of the Pacific","On the island of Tanna, Mount Yasur has been erupting almost continuously for hundreds of years. Every few minutes, it throws glowing lava bombs into the air with loud explosions. Visitors can walk up to the rim of its crater to watch, a thrilling but dangerous experience. According to tradition, Captain James Cook was drawn to Tanna in 1774 by the glow of the volcano, and sailors called it the lighthouse of the Pacific."],
g:[["Lapita","An ancient Pacific people known for decorated pottery"],["Bislama","Vanuatu’s national creole language"],["Condominium","Joint rule of a territory by two countries"],["Blackbirding","Forcing or tricking Pacific Islanders into labor abroad"],["Kastom","Traditional customs in Vanuatu"]],
f:["Vanuatu has over 100 languages.","Land diving inspired bungee jumping.","Vanuatu became independent in 1980."],
fun:"Mount Yasur has been erupting almost continuously for centuries."});

ADDART({id:"solomoncountry",t:"Solomon Islands",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐚",w:"Solomon Islands",r:["pacific","ww2","pngcountry","vanuatucountry","seasnakes","reef"],
p:[["Hundreds of islands","Solomon Islands is made up of six main islands and hundreds of smaller ones."],["A turning point","The Battle of Guadalcanal in World War II was a turning point in the Pacific."],["Traditional money","Traditional money made of shells is still used in ceremonies."]],
l:"Solomon Islands is an island country in the southwestern Pacific Ocean, east of Papua New Guinea, made up of six main islands and nearly a thousand smaller ones, with about 820,000 people. Its capital, Honiara, is on Guadalcanal, scene of one of the most important battles of World War II. Most Solomon Islanders are Melanesian, and the country has about 70 languages. A British protectorate from 1893, it became independent in 1978. It has rich coral reefs and rainforests, and most people live in villages, fishing and farming.",
s:[["Land","The main islands, including Guadalcanal, Malaita, Isabel, Makira, New Georgia and Choiseul, are mountainous and covered with rainforest. Many smaller islands are low coral atolls. The Marovo Lagoon is one of the largest saltwater lagoons in the world."],["First peoples","People reached the Solomon Islands at least 30,000 years ago, among the earliest sea crossings in human history. Later, about 3,000 years ago, Lapita seafarers arrived. Polynesian peoples settled outer islands such as Rennell and Bellona."],["Europeans","The Spanish explorer Álvaro de Mendaña reached the islands in 1568 and named them after the biblical King Solomon, hoping they held gold. In the 1800s, many islanders were taken to work on plantations in Australia and Fiji. Britain declared a protectorate in 1893."],["War and independence","In 1942 Japan occupied the islands, and from August 1942 to February 1943, American and Allied forces fought Japanese forces for Guadalcanal in a brutal campaign that became a turning point in the Pacific War. Solomon Islanders served as scouts and coastwatchers. The country became independent on 7 July 1978."]],
k:[["Capital","Honiara"],["Population","About 820,000 (2024)"],["Area","About 28,900 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Popomanaseu, about 2,335 m"],["Currency","Solomon Islands dollar"]],
d:[["1568","Álvaro de Mendaña names the islands"],["1893","Britain declares a protectorate"],["1942","The Guadalcanal campaign begins"],["1978","Solomon Islands becomes independent"],["1998","A period of ethnic conflict begins"],["2003","A regional peacekeeping mission arrives"],["2019","Solomon Islands switches diplomatic recognition from Taiwan to China"]],
s2:[["The Tensions","From 1998 to 2003, conflict between groups from Guadalcanal and Malaita, known as the Tensions, caused violence and displaced thousands. In 2003 a regional mission led by Australia and New Zealand, RAMSI, restored order and stayed until 2017."],["Languages and culture","Solomon Islands has about 70 languages. English is official, but Solomon Islands Pijin is the common language. Customs called kastom guide land ownership, ceremonies and family life, and wantok, loyalty to people who speak one’s language, is very important."],["Shell money","On Malaita, strings of tiny shell beads, polished and dyed, have long been used as money for important exchanges such as bride price and settling disputes. Shell money is still made and used in ceremonies today."],["East Rennell","East Rennell, the southern part of Rennell Island, contains Lake Tegano, the largest lake in the insular Pacific, home to a rare freshwater sea snake and many unique species. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site but is threatened by logging and mining."],["International relations","In 2019 the government switched diplomatic recognition from Taiwan to China, and in 2022 signed a security agreement with China, drawing attention from Australia, the United States and others concerned about influence in the Pacific. The government said it was pursuing friendship with all countries."],["Climate and the sea","Solomon Islanders depend on the sea for food and travel. Rising sea levels have already swallowed several small reef islands, and coastal villages face erosion and flooding, making climate change a major concern."]],
sp:["The rescue of John F. Kennedy","In August 1943, during the fighting for the New Georgia islands, after the Guadalcanal campaign, a Japanese destroyer rammed and sank an American patrol boat, PT-109, commanded by a young lieutenant, John F. Kennedy. Kennedy and his surviving crew swam to a small island. Two Solomon Islander scouts, Biuku Gasa and Eroni Kumana, found them. Kennedy carved a message on a coconut husk, which the scouts paddled by canoe through dangerous waters to Allied forces, leading to the crew’s rescue. Kennedy, who later became US president, kept the coconut on his desk in the White House."],
g:[["Melanesian","Belonging to the peoples of the western Pacific islands"],["Pijin","The common creole language of Solomon Islands"],["Coastwatcher","A person who secretly reported enemy movements in World War II"],["Wantok","A Pijin word for people who share one’s language"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea by reefs or islands"]],
f:["Honiara is on Guadalcanal.","The country has about 70 languages.","Shell money is still used in ceremonies."],
fun:"Two Solomon Islanders saved future US president John F. Kennedy using a message carved on a coconut."});

ADDART({id:"kiribaticountry",t:"Kiribati",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌴",w:"Kiribati",r:["pacific","coral","climchange","ww2","fijicountry","timezones","tuvalucountry"],
p:[["Spread across the ocean","Kiribati’s 33 islands are scattered across a vast area of the Pacific."],["First to the new day","Part of Kiribati is the first place on Earth to enter each new day."],["Low and threatened","Most of its islands are only a few meters above sea level."]],
l:"Kiribati, pronounced KIR-ee-bas, is an island country in the central Pacific Ocean, made up of 33 coral atolls and islands spread across more than 3 million square kilometers of ocean, straddling the equator. About 135,000 people live there, most on Tarawa, where the capital, South Tarawa, lies. Formerly the Gilbert Islands, a British colony, it became independent in 1979. Kiribati’s Line Islands are the first place on Earth to greet each new day. Because most of its land is only a few meters above sea level, Kiribati is among the countries most threatened by rising seas.",
s:[["Land and sea","Kiribati has three island groups, the Gilbert, Phoenix and Line Islands. Most are low coral atolls, rings of narrow islands around lagoons. Kiritimati, or Christmas Island, is the largest coral atoll in the world by land area. Banaba, a raised coral island, is the highest point."],["People","The people, called I-Kiribati, are Micronesian and speak Gilbertese, or Te taetae ni Kiribati. Communities are centered on the maneaba, a large meeting house where elders make decisions. Fishing, coconut growing and handicrafts are traditional ways of life."],["Colonial history","Micronesian peoples settled the islands over 2,000 years ago. Britain made the Gilbert and Ellice Islands a protectorate in 1892 and later a colony. Phosphate mining on Banaba from 1900 stripped much of the island, and in 1945 many Banabans were moved to Rabi Island in Fiji."],["War and independence","In November 1943, American forces fought Japanese troops in the bloody Battle of Tarawa. Britain and the United States later tested nuclear weapons near Kiritimati and Malden Island from 1957 to 1962. The Ellice Islands separated as Tuvalu, and Kiribati became independent on 12 July 1979."]],
k:[["Capital","South Tarawa"],["Population","About 135,000 (2024)"],["Land area","About 811 km²"],["Highest point","Banaba, about 81 m"],["Currency","Australian dollar"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","Micronesian peoples begin settling the islands"],["1892","Britain declares a protectorate"],["1943","The Battle of Tarawa"],["1957","Nuclear weapons tests begin near Kiritimati"],["1979","Kiribati becomes independent"],["1995","Kiribati moves the international date line"],["2014","Kiribati buys land in Fiji for food security"]],
s2:[["The date line","The international date line once ran through Kiribati, so the eastern and western islands were on different days. In 1995 the government moved the line east, putting the whole country on the same day. Its Line Islands now use the time zone UTC+14, the earliest on Earth, and Caroline Island, renamed Millennium Island, was among the first places to see the dawn of the year 2000."],["Rising seas","Most of Kiribati is less than about 3 meters above sea level. Rising seas, stronger waves and storm surges flood homes, erode shores and make fresh groundwater salty. Leaders have warned the world that the country could become uninhabitable, and in 2014 Kiribati bought land in Fiji to grow food for its people if needed."],["Phoenix Islands Protected Area","In 2008 Kiribati created the Phoenix Islands Protected Area, one of the largest marine protected areas in the world, covering over 400,000 square kilometers of ocean and coral reefs. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2010."],["Dance and culture","Kiribati dance imitates the movements of the frigatebird, with outstretched arms, sharp head movements and precise footwork. The frigatebird also appears on the national flag. Traditional navigation, canoe building and string figures are part of the culture."],["Diplomacy","Kiribati has a seat at the United Nations and has spoken out on climate change. In 2019 it switched diplomatic recognition from Taiwan to China, drawing attention to competition for influence in the Pacific."],["Life on an atoll","On narrow atolls, with little soil and fresh water, life depends on fish, coconuts, breadfruit and a root crop called babai, a giant swamp taro grown in pits. South Tarawa has become very crowded as people move from outer islands, causing problems with water, sanitation and housing."]],
sp:["The first sunrise of the millennium","As the year 2000 approached, several Pacific countries competed to be the first to see the new millennium. Kiribati had moved the date line in 1995, placing its eastern Line Islands in the world’s earliest time zone. It renamed uninhabited Caroline Island as Millennium Island and invited a television crew and dancers to celebrate there. On 1 January 2000, the island was one of the first places on Earth to see the dawn of the new millennium, broadcast around the world."],
g:[["Atoll","A ring of coral islands around a lagoon"],["I-Kiribati","The people of Kiribati"],["Maneaba","A traditional meeting house"],["Date line","An imaginary line where the calendar date changes"],["Storm surge","A rise in sea level caused by a storm"]],
f:["Kiribati is pronounced KIR-ee-bas.","Kiritimati is the largest coral atoll by land area.","Kiribati’s Line Islands are first to greet each new day."],
fun:"Kiribati moved the international date line so the whole country could share the same day."});

