ADDART({id:"sahel",t:"The Sahel",c:"Geography",i:"🌾",w:"Sahel",r:["sahara","desert","mali","senegalcountry","nigeriacountry","climchange"],
p:[["The shore of the desert","The Sahel is a belt of dry grassland along the southern edge of the Sahara."],["Ancient empires","Great empires such as Ghana, Mali and Songhai rose in the Sahel."],["A green wall","African countries are working to restore land across the region."]],
l:"The Sahel is a semi-arid belt of land stretching about 5,000 kilometers across Africa, from the Atlantic Ocean in Senegal and Mauritania to the Red Sea in Sudan and Eritrea, between the Sahara Desert to the north and the wetter savannas to the south. Its name comes from the Arabic word for shore, as it was seen as the shore of the great sea of sand. The Sahel was home to some of Africa’s greatest empires and trading cities, such as Timbuktu. Today its fast-growing population faces droughts, land degradation, conflict and the effects of climate change, but also inspiring efforts to regreen the land.",
s:[["Land and climate","The Sahel receives about 200 to 600 millimeters of rain a year, falling mostly in a short rainy season from June to September. The rest of the year is hot and dry. Grasses, thorny acacia trees and baobabs grow there. Rainfall varies greatly from year to year, making life uncertain for farmers and herders."],["Countries","The Sahel crosses parts of Senegal, Mauritania, Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, Nigeria, Chad, Sudan and Eritrea, and touches Cameroon and others. Great rivers, such as the Niger and the Senegal, and Lake Chad have long supported farming and fishing."],["Peoples and ways of life","Many peoples live in the Sahel, including the Fulani, Tuareg, Hausa, Wolof, Songhai and Kanuri. Some are farmers growing millet, sorghum and peanuts; others are herders moving their cattle, camels, sheep and goats with the seasons in search of pasture and water."],["Empires and trade","From about the 700s, empires grew rich on trans-Saharan trade in gold, salt and other goods. The Ghana Empire was followed by the Mali Empire, whose ruler Mansa Musa made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, and then the Songhai Empire. Cities such as Timbuktu, Gao and Djenné became centers of trade and Islamic learning."]],
k:[["Length","About 5,000 km"],["Rainfall","About 200–600 mm a year"],["Name means","Shore (Arabic)"],["Main crops","Millet, sorghum, peanuts"],["Major restoration effort","The Great Green Wall"]],
d:[["700s","The Ghana Empire grows rich on trade"],["1324","Mansa Musa of Mali makes his pilgrimage to Mecca"],["1591","The Songhai Empire falls to a Moroccan army"],["1960","Many Sahel countries become independent"],["1968–1974","A severe drought and famine strike the Sahel"],["2007","The Great Green Wall initiative is launched"],["2012","Conflict breaks out in northern Mali"]],
s2:[["Droughts","From the late 1960s to the 1980s, the Sahel suffered severe droughts. Crops failed and herds died, and famine killed many thousands of people and animals. The disasters brought international attention, aid and research into the causes, which include natural variations in ocean temperatures as well as human pressures on the land."],["Lake Chad","Lake Chad, shared by Chad, Cameroon, Niger and Nigeria, shrank dramatically from the 1960s to the 1980s because of drought and increased water use, losing most of its surface area. It supports millions of people through fishing, farming and herding, and its decline has added to hardship and conflict in the region."],["The Great Green Wall","In 2007 the African Union launched the Great Green Wall, a plan to restore degraded land across the Sahel, originally imagined as a wall of trees from Senegal to Djibouti. It has grown into a broader program of planting, protecting natural regrowth and improving farming. Progress has been uneven, but some areas have become greener."],["Farmers who regreened the land","In Niger and Burkina Faso, farmers have restored millions of hectares by protecting tree shoots that sprout from old roots, a method called farmer-managed natural regeneration, and by digging small pits and stone lines to catch rainwater. These low-cost methods have increased crop yields and tree cover."],["Conflict and politics","Since 2012, armed groups, including some linked to international jihadist movements, have spread violence in Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger and neighboring countries, killing thousands and forcing millions from their homes. Military coups took place in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger between 2020 and 2023. These governments formed the Alliance of Sahel States and left the West African regional bloc ECOWAS in 2025. Views differ widely on the causes and solutions."],["Mud architecture","Sahel builders have long made beautiful buildings from mud brick. The Great Mosque of Djenné in Mali, rebuilt in 1907, is the largest mud-brick building in the world. Every year, the townspeople gather to replaster it with fresh mud in a joyful festival."]],
sp:["Replastering the Great Mosque of Djenné","Each year, before the rainy season, thousands of people in the town of Djenné in Mali take part in the Crepissage, a festival to repair their great mud mosque. For days beforehand, mud is mixed in pits and left to ferment. Then, on the chosen day, men and boys race up ladders and wooden beams that stick out from the walls, while women and girls carry water and mud. Working together, to music and cheers, they smooth a fresh layer of mud over the whole building, protecting it for another year."],
g:[["Semi-arid","Having little rain, but more than a desert"],["Savanna","A grassland with scattered trees"],["Herder","A person who looks after grazing animals"],["Land degradation","The decline of land’s ability to support plants and farming"],["Trans-Saharan trade","Trade carried across the Sahara Desert by camel caravans"]],
f:["Sahel means shore in Arabic.","The Great Mosque of Djenné is the largest mud-brick building.","The Great Green Wall began in 2007."],
fun:"Each year, the people of Djenné replaster their huge mud mosque together in a festival."});

ADDART({id:"patagonia",t:"Patagonia",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Patagonia",r:["andes","argentinacountry","chilecountry","penguin","darwin","iceage"],
p:[["The end of the world","Patagonia is the wild southern tip of South America."],["Ice and wind","It has huge glaciers, jagged peaks and fierce winds."],["Giant dinosaurs","Some of the largest dinosaurs ever found lived in Patagonia."]],
l:"Patagonia is a vast region at the southern end of South America, shared by Argentina and Chile. It includes the southern Andes, with glaciers, ice fields and jagged granite peaks, the windswept steppes of the Argentine plains and the islands of Tierra del Fuego. Sparsely populated, Patagonia is home to Indigenous peoples such as the Mapuche and Tehuelche, descendants of European settlers, including a Welsh-speaking community, and wildlife such as guanacos, condors and penguins. It is famous for its national parks and for fossils of giant dinosaurs.",
s:[["Land","Patagonia is usually said to begin around the Colorado River in Argentina and the region of the Biobío in Chile and extends south to Cape Horn. The Chilean side is wet, with forests, fjords and glaciers, while the Argentine side is dry, flat steppe swept by strong westerly winds. Together they cover about a million square kilometers."],["Ice fields","The Southern Patagonian Ice Field is the largest expanse of ice in the Southern Hemisphere outside Antarctica. Its glaciers include the Perito Moreno Glacier in Argentina, which regularly advances across a lake and then collapses spectacularly, unlike most glaciers, which are retreating."],["Wildlife","Guanacos, wild relatives of the llama, roam the plains with rheas, large flightless birds. Andean condors soar over the mountains, pumas hunt in the parks and colonies of Magellanic penguins nest on the Atlantic coast, while southern right whales and orcas visit the shores."],["First peoples","People have lived in Patagonia for at least 12,000 years. The Cueva de las Manos in Argentina has hundreds of painted handprints, some about 9,000 years old. Peoples such as the Tehuelche and Mapuche of the mainland and the Selk’nam and Yaghan of Tierra del Fuego adapted to its harsh climate."]],
k:[["Countries","Argentina and Chile"],["Area","About 1 million km²"],["Famous parks","Torres del Paine, Los Glaciares"],["Southernmost city","Ushuaia, Argentina"],["Name","From the Patagones, a name given by Magellan’s crew"]],
d:[["c. 7300 BCE","The oldest handprints at Cueva de las Manos are painted"],["1520","Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition reaches Patagonia"],["1834","Charles Darwin explores Patagonia on HMS Beagle"],["1865","Welsh settlers arrive in Chubut"],["1878","Argentina’s Conquest of the Desert campaign begins"],["1959","Torres del Paine National Park is created"],["2017","Patagotitan, a giant dinosaur, is named"]],
s2:[["Magellan and the giants","In 1520, sailors on Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition met tall Tehuelche people on the coast and called them Patagones, possibly after a giant in a Spanish romance novel, giving the region its name. European stories of a land of giants persisted for centuries, although the Tehuelche were simply tall."],["The Welsh in Patagonia","In 1865 about 150 Welsh settlers arrived on the ship Mimosa, seeking a place where they could keep their language and culture. They founded towns in the Chubut valley, such as Trelew and Gaiman. Today some of their descendants still speak Welsh and serve Welsh tea and cakes."],["Conquest and loss","In the late 1800s, Argentina and Chile extended their control over Patagonia by force. Argentina’s Conquest of the Desert campaign from 1878 killed and displaced many Indigenous people, and in Tierra del Fuego the Selk’nam were nearly wiped out by violence and disease. Indigenous communities continue to seek recognition and land rights."],["Dinosaur land","Patagonia’s rocks have revealed some of the largest dinosaurs ever discovered, such as Argentinosaurus and Patagotitan, giant long-necked plant-eaters weighing as much as 60 metric tons or more, and the huge meat-eater Giganotosaurus."],["Torres del Paine","Torres del Paine National Park in Chile is famous for its granite towers, turquoise lakes, glaciers and guanacos. With Los Glaciares National Park and Mount Fitz Roy in Argentina, it attracts hikers and climbers from around the world."],["Tierra del Fuego","At Patagonia’s southern tip lies Tierra del Fuego, the Land of Fire, named by Magellan’s crew after the fires of Indigenous people they saw on shore. The Argentine city of Ushuaia, often called the southernmost city in the world, is a starting point for ships to Antarctica."]],
sp:["Darwin in Patagonia","When Charles Darwin explored Patagonia in 1833 and 1834 during the voyage of HMS Beagle, he found fossils of giant extinct mammals, such as ground sloths and glyptodonts, huge armored relatives of armadillos. He noticed that they resembled smaller living animals of the same region. This puzzle, along with his observations of rheas and of the Galápagos Islands, helped lead him to his theory of evolution. He also described the vast, empty plains of Patagonia as haunting his memory."],
g:[["Steppe","A large, dry, treeless grassland"],["Ice field","A large area of connected glaciers"],["Guanaco","A wild South American relative of the llama"],["Rhea","A large, flightless South American bird"],["Glacier","A slowly moving mass of ice"]],
f:["Patagonia is shared by Argentina and Chile.","Welsh is still spoken by some Patagonians.","Giant dinosaurs such as Patagotitan were found there."],
fun:"Some people in Patagonia still speak Welsh, brought by settlers in 1865."});

ADDART({id:"polynesia",t:"Polynesia",c:"Geography",i:"🛶",w:"Polynesia",r:["pacific","hawaii","nzcountry","samoacountry","tongacountry","explor"],
p:[["Many islands","Polynesia is a huge triangle of islands in the Pacific Ocean."],["Master navigators","Polynesians crossed thousands of kilometers of ocean using stars, waves and birds."],["Shared roots","Polynesian peoples share related languages and cultures."]],
l:"Polynesia, meaning many islands, is a vast region of the central and southern Pacific Ocean, forming a triangle with Hawai‘i, New Zealand and Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, at its corners. It includes more than a thousand islands, among them Samoa, Tonga, Tahiti, Tuvalu and the Cook Islands. Polynesians are descendants of seafarers who, without maps or instruments, explored and settled the largest ocean on Earth, reaching New Zealand around 1250 to 1300 CE. Their related languages and cultures, including the haka, tattooing and the ideas of mana and tapu, are known worldwide.",
s:[["The Polynesian Triangle","Polynesia covers an area larger than Russia, mostly ocean. It includes independent countries such as Samoa, Tonga and Tuvalu, self-governing islands such as the Cook Islands and Niue, territories such as French Polynesia, American Samoa and Wallis and Futuna, the US state of Hawai‘i, Chile’s Rapa Nui and New Zealand."],["Origins","Polynesians descend from Austronesian-speaking peoples whose ancestors spread from Taiwan through the Philippines and Indonesia thousands of years ago. The Lapita people reached Tonga and Samoa about 2,800 years ago, and Polynesian culture developed there."],["The great voyages","After a long pause, Polynesians sailed east from about 1000 CE, settling the Society Islands, the Marquesas, Hawai‘i and Rapa Nui, and finally New Zealand around 1250 to 1300 CE. They traveled in large double-hulled canoes carrying families, plants and animals such as taro, breadfruit, pigs and chickens."],["Wayfinding","Polynesian navigators found their way without instruments, reading the rising and setting points of stars, the patterns of ocean swells, the flight of birds, clouds and the color of the water. This knowledge was passed down through generations."]],
k:[["Name means","Many islands"],["Corners","Hawai‘i, New Zealand, Rapa Nui"],["Settlement of New Zealand","About 1250–1300 CE"],["Language family","Austronesian"],["Famous canoe revival","Hōkūle‘a (1976)"]],
d:[["c. 800 BCE","Lapita settlers reach Tonga and Samoa"],["c. 1000 CE","Polynesians begin settling eastern Polynesia"],["c. 1200 CE","Polynesians and Native Americans make contact"],["c. 1250–1300","Polynesians settle New Zealand"],["1768–1779","James Cook’s voyages through Polynesia"],["1976","The canoe Hōkūle‘a sails from Hawai‘i to Tahiti"],["2023","Tuvalu and Australia agree on a treaty including climate migration"]],
s2:[["Languages","Polynesian languages, such as Māori, Hawaiian, Samoan, Tongan and Tahitian, are closely related and belong to the Austronesian family, which also includes Filipino, Malay and Malagasy. Words such as taboo, from tapu, and tattoo, from tatau, came into English from Polynesian languages."],["Mana and tapu","Mana is spiritual power, authority and prestige, while tapu marks something as sacred or forbidden. These ideas shaped Polynesian societies, guiding the roles of chiefs, priests and ordinary people and how people treated land, sea and each other."],["Moai of Rapa Nui","On Rapa Nui, the easternmost corner of the triangle, people carved nearly a thousand giant stone statues called moai, representing ancestors, between about 1250 and 1500 CE. How they were moved has long fascinated people; experiments suggest they were walked upright by teams rocking them with ropes."],["Contact with the Americas","Sweet potatoes, native to South America, were grown in Polynesia long before Europeans arrived, suggesting contact. A 2020 genetic study found evidence that Polynesians and Native Americans met around 1200 CE."],["Hōkūle‘a","In 1976, the Hawaiian voyaging canoe Hōkūle‘a sailed about 4,000 kilometers from Hawai‘i to Tahiti, guided by the Micronesian master navigator Mau Piailug without instruments. The voyage proved that Polynesians could have settled the Pacific by deliberate navigation and sparked a revival of traditional voyaging and pride across the Pacific."],["Colonial rule and today","From the 1700s, European explorers, missionaries, traders and colonial powers transformed Polynesia. Tonga was the only Polynesian kingdom never formally colonized, though it was a British protected state. France tested nuclear weapons in French Polynesia from 1966 to 1996. Today low-lying nations such as Tuvalu face rising seas from climate change."]],
sp:["Reading the ocean","Polynesian navigators were trained from childhood to read the ocean. They memorized the points on the horizon where dozens of stars rise and set, creating a star compass. They could feel different swells passing under the canoe, even at night, and notice how islands far over the horizon bend these swells. Clouds that gather over islands, the greenish reflection of lagoons on clouds and the flight paths of seabirds returning to land at dusk all pointed the way. With this knowledge, they found tiny islands in the vastness of the Pacific."],
g:[["Polynesia","The many islands of the central and southern Pacific"],["Wayfinding","Traditional navigation without instruments"],["Mana","Spiritual power and prestige"],["Tapu","Sacred or forbidden; the origin of the word taboo"],["Moai","Giant stone statues of Rapa Nui"]],
f:["Polynesia means many islands.","New Zealand was settled around 1250–1300 CE.","The words taboo and tattoo come from Polynesian languages."],
fun:"The English words taboo and tattoo come from Polynesian languages."});

ADDART({id:"centralasia",t:"Central Asia",c:"Geography",i:"🐎",w:"Central Asia",r:["silk","mongol","ibnsina","islam","russiacountry","chinacountry"],
p:[["The heart of Asia","Central Asia lies between China, Russia, Iran and Afghanistan."],["The Silk Road","Great Silk Road cities such as Samarkand and Bukhara grew rich on trade."],["Nomads and horses","Nomadic herders on horseback have lived on its steppes for thousands of years."]],
l:"Central Asia is a region in the heart of the Asian continent, usually defined as the five countries of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan, with about 80 million people. It stretches from the Caspian Sea to China, with vast steppes and deserts in the west and north and high mountains, such as the Tian Shan and the Pamirs, in the southeast. For centuries the Silk Road ran through its oasis cities, such as Samarkand and Bukhara, connecting China with Persia and Europe. Ruled by many empires and later by Russia and the Soviet Union, the five countries became independent in 1991.",
s:[["Land","Central Asia is far from any ocean. The Kazakh Steppe, a vast grassland, covers the north, while the Karakum and Kyzylkum deserts stretch across Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan. In the southeast, the Pamirs, called the roof of the world, and the Tian Shan rise above 7,000 meters. Rivers such as the Amu Darya and Syr Darya once flowed into the Aral Sea."],["Nomads and cities","For thousands of years, nomadic peoples herded horses, sheep and camels on the steppes, living in portable felt tents called yurts. Settled peoples farmed in river valleys and oases, building cities that became centers of trade and learning."],["Empires","Central Asia was part of the Persian Empire and conquered by Alexander the Great. Arab armies brought Islam in the 700s. In the 1200s the Mongols under Genghis Khan devastated the region. In the late 1300s, Timur, also known as Tamerlane, built an empire with Samarkand as its glittering capital."],["Russian and Soviet rule","Russia conquered Central Asia in the 1800s. Under the Soviet Union, the region was divided into five republics. The Soviets built schools and industry but also forced nomads onto collective farms, causing a terrible famine in Kazakhstan in the early 1930s. The five republics became independent when the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991."]],
k:[["Countries","Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan"],["Population","About 80 million"],["Famous cities","Samarkand, Bukhara, Khiva, Almaty, Tashkent"],["Main languages","Kazakh, Kyrgyz, Uzbek, Turkmen, Tajik, Russian"],["Independence","1991"]],
d:[["329 BCE","Alexander the Great reaches Samarkand"],["700s","Arab armies bring Islam to the region"],["1220","Genghis Khan’s Mongols conquer the region"],["1370","Timur makes Samarkand his capital"],["1961","Yuri Gagarin is launched into space from Baikonur, Kazakhstan"],["1991","The five countries become independent"],["2005","A dam helps the North Aral Sea begin to recover"]],
s2:[["Scholars of the golden age","Central Asia produced some of the greatest scholars of the Islamic golden age. Al-Khwarizmi, from Khwarazm, gave his name to the word algorithm, and his book gave us algebra. Ibn Sina, born near Bukhara, wrote a medical encyclopedia used for centuries, and al-Biruni measured the Earth with remarkable accuracy."],["Samarkand","Samarkand, in Uzbekistan, is one of the oldest cities in Central Asia. Its Registan square, framed by three huge madrasas covered in blue tiles, is one of the most famous sights in the Islamic world. In the 1420s, Timur’s grandson Ulugh Beg built an observatory there and made a precise catalog of stars."],["The Aral Sea","The Aral Sea, once the fourth-largest lake in the world, shrank dramatically after Soviet planners diverted its rivers in the 1960s to irrigate cotton fields. Fishing towns were left far from the water, and dust storms carried salt and chemicals. Since a dam was built in 2005, the northern part in Kazakhstan has partly recovered."],["Space and nuclear tests","The Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan, from which Sputnik and the first human in space were launched, is still used by Russia. The Soviet Union carried out hundreds of nuclear tests at Semipalatinsk in Kazakhstan from 1949 to 1989, harming the health of local people. The site was closed in 1991."],["Nomadic traditions","Central Asian peoples keep traditions such as hunting with golden eagles, horse games like kok-boru, in which riders compete for a goat carcass, and living in yurts in summer pastures. The Kyrgyz Epic of Manas is one of the longest epic poems in the world."],["Today","The five countries have different paths: Kazakhstan is rich in oil and uranium, Uzbekistan is the most populous, Turkmenistan has huge gas reserves, and Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan are mountainous, relying on hydropower and on money sent home by workers abroad. Most people are Muslim, and Russian remains widely spoken."]],
sp:["The Door to Hell","In the Karakum Desert of Turkmenistan lies the Darvaza gas crater, a pit about 70 meters wide that has been burning for decades. According to the most common account, in 1971 Soviet geologists drilling for gas accidentally caused the ground to collapse, and the crater began leaking methane gas. To stop the gas from spreading, they set it on fire, expecting it to burn out in a few weeks. It is still burning, glowing orange at night, and is nicknamed the Door to Hell. Turkmenistan’s government has discussed putting it out."],
g:[["Steppe","A vast, dry, treeless grassland"],["Yurt","A round, portable felt tent used by nomads"],["Oasis","A fertile place with water in a desert"],["Madrasa","An Islamic school or college"],["Nomad","A person who moves from place to place with herds"]],
f:["Central Asia includes five countries ending in -stan.","The word algorithm comes from al-Khwarizmi.","The first human in space was launched from Kazakhstan."],
fun:"A gas crater in Turkmenistan has been burning since about 1971."});

ADDART({id:"lagos",t:"Lagos",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏙️",w:"Lagos",r:["nigeriacountry","cinema","urban","slavetrade","brazilcountry","africacolon"],
p:[["Africa’s megacity","Lagos is one of the largest and fastest-growing cities in Africa."],["City of lagoons","It is built on islands and the mainland around a large lagoon."],["Culture capital","Lagos is a center of Nollywood films and Afrobeats music."]],
l:"Lagos is the largest city in Nigeria and one of the largest cities in Africa, home to an estimated 15 million or more people in its metropolitan area. Built around the Lagos Lagoon on islands and the mainland along the Atlantic coast, it was Nigeria’s capital until 1991 and remains its business, financial and cultural center. Lagos is famous for its energy, traffic, markets, music, films and technology start-ups, and also for the challenges of rapid growth, such as housing, flooding and transport.",
s:[["Origins","The area was settled by the Awori, a Yoruba people, and the city is called Èkó in Yoruba. It became a kingdom ruled by an oba, a traditional king, influenced by the Kingdom of Benin. Portuguese traders named it Lagos, meaning lakes, after a town in Portugal."],["Colonial era","Lagos became a major port in the Atlantic slave trade in the 1700s and 1800s. Britain annexed Lagos in 1861, partly to end the slave trade, and it became the capital of the colony and later of Nigeria, which became independent in 1960. In 1991 the capital moved to Abuja."],["The city today","Lagos is divided between the islands, Lagos Island, Ikoyi and Victoria Island, where many banks, offices and wealthy neighborhoods are, and the vast mainland, where most people live. Long bridges, such as the Third Mainland Bridge, connect them."],["Economy","Lagos is Nigeria’s commercial heart, with its main port, airport, stock exchange and many company headquarters. Markets such as Balogun are packed with traders. The Lekki area has a free trade zone, a deep-sea port and one of the world’s largest oil refineries."]],
k:[["Country","Nigeria"],["Metropolitan population","An estimated 15 million or more"],["Yoruba name","Èkó"],["Capital of Nigeria until","1991"],["Known for","Business, Nollywood, Afrobeats"]],
d:[["1400s","The Awori Yoruba settle the area"],["1472","Portuguese sailors arrive"],["1861","Britain annexes Lagos"],["1960","Lagos becomes capital of independent Nigeria"],["1977","Lagos hosts the FESTAC ’77 arts festival"],["1991","The capital moves to Abuja"],["2023","The first line of the Lagos light rail opens"]],
s2:[["Music","Lagos is the birthplace of Afrobeat, created in the 1970s by Fela Kuti, who blended jazz, funk and Yoruba music with political lyrics at his club, the Shrine. Today Lagos is the center of Afrobeats, a newer pop style whose stars, such as Burna Boy, Wizkid and Tems, have huge global audiences."],["Nollywood","Nigeria’s film industry, Nollywood, centered in Lagos, produces more films each year than almost any other country. It began in the 1990s with low-budget movies sold on video and now includes cinema releases and streaming hits watched across Africa and the world."],["Technology","Lagos has become a leading African technology hub, with start-ups in online payments, banking and delivery. The area around Yaba is nicknamed Yabacon Valley. Companies founded in Lagos have attracted large international investment."],["Traffic and transport","Lagos is famous for its traffic jams, called go-slows. Yellow minibuses called danfos and motorcycle taxis carry millions. New bus rapid transit lines, light rail lines and ferries on the lagoon aim to ease congestion."],["Challenges","Lagos has grown extremely fast, and many residents live in informal settlements without reliable water, electricity or sanitation. Makoko, a community of homes on stilts over the lagoon, has become a symbol of both hardship and resourcefulness. Rising seas and heavy rains cause frequent flooding."],["The Brazilian Quarter","In the 1800s, formerly enslaved Africans and their descendants returned from Brazil and Cuba to Lagos. They settled in an area called the Brazilian Quarter, building houses and churches in Afro-Brazilian style and bringing foods, festivals and Portuguese surnames that survive today."]],
sp:["Eko Atlantic","Along Lagos’s Atlantic shore, a new city district called Eko Atlantic is being built on land reclaimed from the sea. Since the late 2000s, millions of metric tons of sand have been dredged from the ocean floor and protected by a sea wall made of thousands of concrete blocks, called the Great Wall of Lagos. Supporters see it as a modern business district protecting the coast from erosion; critics argue it serves mainly the wealthy and may worsen flooding elsewhere. It shows both the ambition and the inequality of the growing megacity."],
g:[["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea"],["Oba","A traditional Yoruba king"],["Afrobeats","A popular West African music style"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing, often without services"]],
f:["Lagos was Nigeria’s capital until 1991.","Its Yoruba name is Èkó.","Fela Kuti created Afrobeat in Lagos."],
fun:"Lagos is being extended into the sea with a new district built on reclaimed land."});

ADDART({id:"saopaulo",t:"São Paulo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌆",w:"São Paulo",r:["brazilcountry","rio","coffee","japancountry","soccer","urban"],
p:[["Giant of the south","São Paulo is the largest city in South America."],["City of immigrants","People from Italy, Japan, Lebanon and many other countries made it their home."],["Brazil’s engine","It is Brazil’s richest city and business center."]],
l:"São Paulo is the largest city in Brazil and in South America, with about 11.5 million people in the city and more than 20 million in its metropolitan area. Founded by Jesuit missionaries in 1554, it grew rich from coffee in the 1800s and attracted millions of immigrants, including the largest Japanese community outside Japan. Today São Paulo is Brazil’s financial and industrial center, a huge city of skyscrapers, museums, restaurants and cultural life, but also of great inequality.",
s:[["Founding","São Paulo was founded on 25 January 1554 by Jesuit priests, including José de Anchieta and Manuel da Nóbrega, as a mission to convert Indigenous people. For centuries it was a small town. From there, bands of explorers called bandeirantes set out to search for gold and to capture Indigenous people as slaves, extending Portuguese control inland."],["Coffee and immigrants","In the 1800s, coffee plantations made São Paulo state rich. After slavery was abolished in Brazil in 1888, the government encouraged immigration. Italians, Portuguese, Spaniards, Japanese, Germans, Lebanese, Syrians and others arrived in large numbers. The city grew rapidly into an industrial center."],["A world city","São Paulo is home to Brazil’s stock exchange, banks and many company headquarters. Avenida Paulista, lined with skyscrapers, is its symbolic heart, closed to cars on Sundays for walking and cycling. The city has dozens of universities, theaters and some of the best restaurants in Latin America."],["Inequality","Wealthy neighborhoods with luxury apartments stand near favelas, informal communities where many families lack secure housing and services. The contrast between rich and poor is one of the city’s biggest challenges."]],
k:[["Country","Brazil"],["City population","About 11.5 million (2022 census)"],["Metropolitan population","More than 20 million"],["Founded","25 January 1554"],["Known for","Business, immigration, food, culture"]],
d:[["1554","Jesuit priests found São Paulo"],["1888","Slavery is abolished in Brazil, and immigration grows"],["1908","The first Japanese immigrants arrive"],["1922","The Week of Modern Art launches Brazilian modernism"],["1951","The first São Paulo Art Biennial is held"],["1968","The São Paulo Museum of Art building opens"],["2007","The Clean City Law bans outdoor billboards"]],
s2:[["Little Tokyo","In 1908 the ship Kasato Maru brought the first Japanese immigrants to Brazil to work on coffee farms. Today Brazil has the largest population of Japanese descent outside Japan, and the São Paulo district of Liberdade, with its red lanterns, shops and festivals, is a center of Japanese and East Asian culture."],["Art and architecture","The São Paulo Museum of Art, or MASP, designed by the Italian-Brazilian architect Lina Bo Bardi, seems to float above the street on four red pillars. Ibirapuera Park, with buildings by Oscar Niemeyer, hosts the São Paulo Art Biennial, one of the oldest and most important art exhibitions in the world."],["Food","Thanks to its immigrants, São Paulo has an enormous variety of food. It is famous for pizza, brought by Italians, and its own pizza day; for Japanese restaurants; and for Lebanese dishes such as esfihas and kibbeh, alongside Brazilian favorites like feijoada and pastel, a fried pastry sold at street markets."],["Sport","Football is a passion in São Paulo, with rival clubs such as Corinthians, Palmeiras and São Paulo FC, and Santos, Pelé’s club, nearby. The Interlagos circuit hosts Formula One races, and the city was home to the racing driver Ayrton Senna."],["The Clean City Law","In 2007 São Paulo banned outdoor billboards, large shop signs and advertising on buses with its Clean City Law. Thousands of billboards came down, revealing buildings and, critics noted, also exposing run-down areas and hidden favelas. Many residents came to love the cleaner skyline."],["Getting around","With heavy traffic, São Paulo relies on its metro, opened in 1974, trains and buses. Wealthy residents and businesspeople famously use helicopters, and the city has one of the largest numbers of helicopters of any city in the world."]],
sp:["The Week of Modern Art","In February 1922, a group of young artists, writers and musicians held a Week of Modern Art at São Paulo’s Municipal Theatre. They presented bold paintings, poems and music that broke with European traditions and celebrated Brazil’s mixed Indigenous, African and European roots. Some audiences booed and threw things. Afterwards the writer Oswald de Andrade proposed that Brazilian culture should devour foreign influences and transform them into something new. The event launched Brazilian modernism and shaped Brazilian art for decades."],
g:[["Metropolitan area","A city together with its surrounding suburbs and towns"],["Favela","An informal neighborhood in a Brazilian city"],["Bandeirante","A colonial explorer from São Paulo"],["Biennial","An event held every two years"],["Immigrant","A person who moves to live in another country"]],
f:["São Paulo is the largest city in South America.","It has the largest Japanese community outside Japan.","São Paulo banned billboards in 2007."],
fun:"São Paulo banned outdoor billboards in 2007."});

ADDART({id:"mexicocity",t:"Mexico City",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌮",w:"Mexico City",r:["mexicocountry","aztec","kahlo","quake","olymp","soccer"],
p:[["City on a lake","Mexico City was built on the ruins of the Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan, on a lake."],["High and huge","It is one of the largest cities in the world, at over 2,200 meters above sea level."],["Sinking city","Parts of the city sink many centimeters each year."]],
l:"Mexico City is the capital and largest city of Mexico, with more than 20 million people in its metropolitan area, making it one of the largest cities in the world and the largest Spanish-speaking city. It lies in a high valley about 2,240 meters above sea level, on the site of Tenochtitlan, the capital of the Aztec Empire, founded on an island in Lake Texcoco in 1325. After the Spanish conquest in 1521, it became the capital of New Spain. Today it is famous for its history, museums, murals, food and lively culture, and also for earthquakes, sinking ground and once-severe air pollution.",
s:[["Tenochtitlan","According to legend, the Mexica, or Aztecs, founded Tenochtitlan in 1325 where they saw an eagle perched on a cactus eating a snake, an image now on Mexico’s flag. The city grew into one of the largest in the world, with causeways, canals, floating gardens and great temples."],["Conquest and New Spain","In 1521 Spanish conquistadors led by Hernán Cortés, with many Indigenous allies, conquered Tenochtitlan after a long siege. The Spanish built Mexico City on its ruins, draining much of the lake. It became the capital of New Spain, a vast colony that also governed the Philippines for over two centuries."],["Modern capital","After independence in 1821, Mexico City remained the capital. It grew enormously in the 1900s as people moved from the countryside, spreading across the valley. It hosted the 1968 Summer Olympics and the 1970 and 1986 football World Cups, and matches of the 2026 World Cup."],["The historic center","The Zócalo, one of the largest public squares in the world, is surrounded by the Metropolitan Cathedral, the National Palace with Diego Rivera’s murals and the ruins of the Templo Mayor, the great Aztec temple. The historic center is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
k:[["Country","Mexico"],["Metropolitan population","More than 20 million"],["Elevation","About 2,240 m"],["Founded","1325, as Tenochtitlan"],["Known for","Aztec history, museums, murals, food"]],
d:[["1325","The Mexica found Tenochtitlan"],["1521","Tenochtitlan falls to the Spanish and their allies"],["1821","Mexico becomes independent"],["1968","Mexico City hosts the Summer Olympics"],["1978","Workers discover the Templo Mayor ruins"],["1985","A powerful earthquake strikes"],["2026","The city hosts the opening match of the World Cup"]],
s2:[["The sinking city","Because the city was built on soft lake clay and pumps huge amounts of water from underground, parts of Mexico City are sinking, in some places by tens of centimeters a year. Buildings tilt, pipes break and the cathedral has been carefully reinforced. Ironically, the city also faces water shortages and floods."],["Earthquakes","Mexico City’s soft lakebed soil amplifies earthquake shaking. On 19 September 1985, an earthquake killed thousands of people and destroyed many buildings. On the same date in 2017, another strong quake killed hundreds. The city now has strict building codes and an earthquake warning system with sirens."],["Xochimilco","In the south of the city, canals and chinampas, artificial islands used for farming since Aztec times, survive at Xochimilco. Families and visitors ride colorful boats called trajineras, accompanied by mariachi musicians. Xochimilco is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Art and museums","Mexico City has one of the largest numbers of museums of any city in the world. The National Museum of Anthropology holds the famous Aztec Sun Stone. Murals by Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros decorate public buildings, and Frida Kahlo’s Blue House in Coyoacán is a beloved museum."],["Food","Mexico City is a paradise for street food: tacos al pastor, tamales, quesadillas, tortas and elotes, corn on the cob. Mexican cuisine is recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage. Markets such as La Merced sell chilies, fruits and spices from across the country."],["Cleaner air","In the 1990s, Mexico City was considered one of the most polluted cities in the world, as smog trapped in the mountain valley. Policies such as restrictions on driving, cleaner fuels, moving factories and expanding public transport, including the metro and Metrobús, greatly improved air quality, though pollution remains a concern."]],
sp:["The stone of the moon goddess","On 21 February 1978, electrical workers digging in central Mexico City struck a huge carved stone. It turned out to be a giant disk showing Coyolxauhqui, the Aztec moon goddess, dismembered by her brother, the war god Huitzilopochtli. The discovery led archaeologists to excavate the Templo Mayor, the great twin temple at the heart of Tenochtitlan, which had been buried under colonial buildings for more than 450 years. The site and its museum now reveal thousands of Aztec offerings and treasures."],
g:[["Tenochtitlan","The capital city of the Aztec Empire"],["Chinampa","An artificial island used for farming"],["New Spain","The Spanish colony that included Mexico and governed the Philippines"],["Mural","A large painting on a wall"],["Subsidence","The gradual sinking of land"]],
f:["Mexico City was built on the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan.","It lies about 2,240 meters above sea level.","Parts of the city sink many centimeters each year."],
fun:"Workers digging for an electric cable discovered the Aztecs’ great temple in 1978."});

