/* data/modern5.js */
// The Modern World, part 4: work, money, digital life and changing societies — the cost of living, start-ups, automation, online learning, cyberbullying, the digital divide, smart homes, housing, loneliness and urban farming.
ADDART({id:"costliving",t:"The Cost of Living",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧾",w:"Cost of living",r:["inflation","money","labor","foodsecurity","housing"],
p:[["Everyday prices","The cost of living is how much money people need for food, housing, transport and other basics."],["When prices jump","After 2021, prices rose quickly in many countries, causing a cost-of-living crisis."],["Making ends meet","Families, businesses and governments respond in different ways."]],
l:"The cost of living is the amount of money needed to pay for basic needs such as food, housing, transport, electricity, water, health care and education in a particular place and time. When prices rise faster than incomes, people can afford less, and families struggle to make ends meet. From 2021 to 2023, many countries suffered a cost-of-living crisis, as prices for food, fuel and housing surged after the COVID-19 pandemic and Russia’s invasion of Ukraine. Governments and central banks responded with interest rate rises, subsidies, wage increases and price controls, which remain subjects of debate.",
s:[["Measuring prices","Governments track the cost of living using a consumer price index, which measures the prices of a basket of common goods and services over time. The rate at which these prices rise is called inflation. In the Philippines, the Philippine Statistics Authority publishes inflation figures every month."],["The crisis of the early 2020s","As economies reopened after pandemic lockdowns, demand recovered while supply chains were disrupted. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 pushed up prices of oil, gas, wheat and fertilizer. Global inflation reached its highest levels in decades, and many households saw their real incomes fall."],["Who is hit hardest","Rising prices hurt poorer households most, because they spend a larger share of their income on food, fuel and rent. People on fixed incomes, such as pensioners, also suffer when prices rise faster than their payments."],["Wages and prices","When prices rise, workers often ask for higher wages. In the Philippines, regional wage boards set minimum wages. Economists debate how to raise incomes without pushing prices even higher."]],
k:[["Measure of prices","Consumer price index"],["Rise in prices","Inflation"],["Recent crisis","2021–2023"],["Philippine inflation data","Philippine Statistics Authority"],["Main tool of central banks","Interest rates"]],
d:[["1970s","Oil price shocks cause high inflation worldwide"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic disrupts supply chains"],["2022","Russia’s invasion of Ukraine pushes up energy and food prices"],["2022","Onion prices soar in the Philippines"],["2023","Philippine inflation peaks at its highest level in over a decade"],["2023","The Philippines sets a temporary ceiling on rice prices"]],
s2:[["The onion crisis","In late 2022 and early 2023, onion prices in the Philippines soared because of poor harvests, limited imports and hoarding, at one point costing more per kilo than meat. Some restaurants removed onions from their menus, and the episode became a symbol of rising food prices. Authorities investigated price manipulation and allowed more imports."],["Rice prices","Rice is the staple food of most Filipinos, so its price strongly affects household budgets. When rice prices rose in 2023, the government briefly set price ceilings, and in 2024 it cut import tariffs on rice to lower prices. Such measures help consumers but can affect farmers’ incomes, a long-running debate."],["Central banks","To slow inflation, central banks, such as the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, raise interest rates, making borrowing more expensive and cooling spending. Higher rates can bring inflation down but may also slow economic growth and raise loan costs."],["Shrinkflation","When costs rise, some companies keep prices the same but put less product in each package, a practice nicknamed shrinkflation. Shoppers may notice smaller chocolate bars or snack packs, and some governments require clearer labeling."],["Coping with high prices","Families cope by budgeting, buying in bulk, comparing prices, cooking at home, growing vegetables and cutting unnecessary spending. Community programs, cash transfers and food assistance help the most vulnerable."],["Debates","Economists and politicians disagree about how much governments should intervene with subsidies, price controls or tax cuts, which can provide quick relief but may be costly or distort markets, versus letting central banks and markets adjust. Views often depend on how people weigh short-term relief against long-term effects."]],
sp:["The basket of goods","To measure inflation, statisticians imagine a basket filled with the things a typical household buys: rice, bread, fish, meat, vegetables, cooking oil, rent, electricity, transport fares, school supplies, haircuts and more. Every month, they check the prices of these items in markets and stores across the country. If the basket costs more than it did a year earlier, prices have risen. The contents are updated over time; mobile phone plans and internet subscriptions were added as they became part of everyday life, while items such as typewriters were removed."],
g:[["Cost of living","The money needed to pay for basic needs"],["Inflation","A general rise in prices over time"],["Consumer price index","A measure of the prices of a basket of goods and services"],["Shrinkflation","Reducing product size while keeping the price"]],
f:["Prices surged worldwide from 2021 to 2023.","Inflation is measured with a consumer price index.","Onions briefly cost more than meat in the Philippines."],
fun:"In early 2023, onions in the Philippines briefly cost more per kilo than meat."});

ADDART({id:"startups",t:"Start-ups and Entrepreneurs",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🚀",w:"Startup company",r:["money","stockm","internet","gigeconomy","digitalpay"],
p:[["New businesses","A start-up is a young company trying to grow quickly with a new idea."],["Taking risks","Entrepreneurs start businesses, taking risks in hope of success."],["Billion-dollar start-ups","Start-ups valued at a billion dollars are called unicorns."]],
l:"A start-up is a newly founded company that aims to grow rapidly by offering a new product, service or way of doing business, often using technology. Start-ups are founded by entrepreneurs, people willing to take risks to turn ideas into businesses. Famous companies such as Apple, Google, Amazon, Alibaba and Grab began as start-ups, some in garages or small apartments. Many start-ups are funded by investors called venture capitalists, and those valued at over a billion dollars are nicknamed unicorns. Most start-ups fail, but successful ones can create jobs, new industries and great wealth.",
s:[["What makes a start-up","Unlike a typical small business, such as a neighborhood store, a start-up aims to scale quickly, reaching many customers, often across countries. Start-ups usually begin with a small team, an idea and little money, and they experiment rapidly to find what customers want."],["Funding","Founders often start with their own savings, money from family and friends or small grants. Angel investors and venture capital firms then invest in exchange for a share of ownership, hoping a few big successes will make up for many failures. Some start-ups eventually sell shares on the stock market."],["Garage legends","Many famous companies began humbly. Hewlett-Packard started in a garage in California in 1939, Apple in a garage in 1976 and Amazon in a garage in 1994. Silicon Valley in California became the world’s best-known start-up hub."],["Unicorns","In 2013 the investor Aileen Lee coined the term unicorn for privately held start-ups valued at over a billion US dollars, because such companies were then rare. Today there are hundreds of unicorns around the world."]],
k:[["Meaning","A young company aiming to grow quickly"],["Founders","Entrepreneurs"],["Main investors","Venture capitalists"],["Unicorn","A start-up valued at over $1 billion"],["Philippine law","Innovative Startup Act (2019)"]],
d:[["1939","Hewlett-Packard is founded in a garage"],["1976","Apple is founded"],["1999","Alibaba is founded in China"],["2012","Grab is founded"],["2013","The term unicorn is coined for billion-dollar start-ups"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Innovative Startup Act"]],
s2:[["Start-ups in Southeast Asia","Southeast Asia has produced major tech companies, such as Grab, which began in Malaysia and is now based in Singapore, Gojek and Tokopedia in Indonesia and Sea Group, owner of Shopee. They grew by solving local problems, such as hailing safe rides, delivering food and making payments for people without bank cards."],["Start-ups in the Philippines","The Philippine start-up scene has grown, especially in digital payments, online lending, logistics and education. The company behind GCash became the country’s first unicorn in 2021. The Innovative Startup Act of 2019 provides support such as grants, training and easier business registration."],["Africa and Latin America","Start-ups in Africa, such as the payments companies Flutterwave and M-Pesa’s successors, and in Latin America, such as MercadoLibre and Nubank, have grown rapidly by serving large populations with mobile technology."],["Failure","Most start-ups fail, often because there is not enough demand for their product, they run out of money or face strong competition. Many entrepreneurs see failure as a learning experience, and some successful founders failed several times before succeeding."],["Social enterprises","Some entrepreneurs start businesses mainly to solve social or environmental problems, such as providing clean water, affordable lighting or jobs for marginalized people, while still earning money. These are called social enterprises."],["Criticism","Critics say start-up culture can glorify overwork, encourage risky growth at any cost and concentrate wealth among a few founders and investors. Some high-profile start-ups collapsed after misleading investors. Supporters point to the innovations, jobs and services that start-ups create."]],
sp:["The garage in Palo Alto","In 1939 two young Stanford University graduates, Bill Hewlett and David Packard, set up a business in a small rented garage behind a house at 367 Addison Avenue in Palo Alto, California, with about 538 dollars. Their first major product was an audio oscillator, an electronic instrument for testing sound equipment; one of their first customers was Walt Disney Studios, which used it for the film Fantasia. Hewlett-Packard grew into a giant electronics company, and the garage is now a historic landmark known as the birthplace of Silicon Valley."],
g:[["Start-up","A young company aiming for rapid growth"],["Entrepreneur","A person who starts a business, taking risks"],["Venture capital","Money invested in young companies in exchange for ownership"],["Unicorn","A start-up valued at over a billion dollars"]],
f:["Many famous companies began as start-ups in garages.","A unicorn is a start-up worth over $1 billion.","The Philippines passed an Innovative Startup Act in 2019."],
fun:"One of Hewlett-Packard’s first customers was Walt Disney Studios."});

ADDART({id:"automation",t:"Automation and the Future of Work",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🤖",w:"Technological unemployment",r:["robot","ai","labor","gigeconomy","education"],
p:[["Machines at work","Automation means machines and software doing tasks once done by people."],["Jobs lost and created","Technology removes some jobs but creates new ones."],["Learning for the future","New skills help workers adapt as work changes."]],
l:"Automation is the use of machines, robots, computers and artificial intelligence to perform tasks that were once done by people. Since the Industrial Revolution, automation has transformed farming, factories and offices, eliminating some jobs while creating new ones and raising productivity. Today, advances in robotics and AI are spreading automation to new kinds of work, from driving and customer service to writing and analysis. People debate how many jobs may be affected, who will benefit and how societies should help workers adapt, through education, training and social protections.",
s:[["A long history","Machines have replaced human work for centuries, from water mills and steam engines to tractors, assembly lines and computers. In the early 1800s, English textile workers called Luddites smashed machines they feared would destroy their livelihoods. Over time, new industries created new jobs, though the transition was painful for many."],["Robots in factories","The first industrial robot, Unimate, began working on a General Motors assembly line in the United States in 1961, handling hot metal parts. Today millions of industrial robots weld, paint, assemble and pack goods. South Korea, Singapore, China, Germany and Japan use the most robots per worker."],["AI and office work","Artificial intelligence can now handle tasks such as answering customer questions, translating, summarizing documents, writing code and analyzing data. This means automation increasingly affects office and professional jobs, not just manual ones."],["Jobs that change","Most jobs are not entirely replaced; instead, some tasks are automated while others remain, changing what workers do. Tasks needing creativity, complex judgment, empathy, care and physical dexterity in unpredictable settings are harder to automate."]],
k:[["First industrial robot","Unimate (1961)"],["Early protesters","The Luddites (1811–1816)"],["Most robots per worker","South Korea"],["Jobs exposed to AI","Many, according to IMF estimates"],["Key response","Education and retraining"]],
d:[["1811","Luddites begin smashing textile machines in England"],["1913","Henry Ford introduces the moving assembly line"],["1961","The first industrial robot starts work"],["1997","A computer defeats the world chess champion"],["2013","A widely cited study estimates many jobs could be automated"],["2022","AI chatbots bring automation to writing and office tasks"]],
s2:[["How many jobs?","Estimates vary widely. A famous 2013 Oxford study estimated that about 47 percent of US jobs were at high risk of automation, while later studies gave lower figures. In 2024 the International Monetary Fund estimated that around 40 percent of jobs worldwide are exposed to AI, with some likely to benefit and others at risk. Many economists expect new jobs to emerge, as in past technological changes."],["The Philippines","The Philippines’ large business process outsourcing industry, including call centers and back-office work, employs well over a million people. AI tools that handle customer service and data processing could change many of these jobs, while also creating new roles in managing, training and checking AI systems. Training workers in new skills is a priority."],["New jobs","Technology has created jobs that did not exist a generation ago, such as app developers, data scientists, social media managers, drone pilots, cybersecurity experts and electric vehicle technicians. Care work, such as nursing and elderly care, is also growing."],["Responses","Ideas for helping workers include lifelong learning and retraining, stronger social safety nets, shorter working weeks and, more controversially, a universal basic income, regular payments to all citizens regardless of work. Countries are experimenting with different approaches."],["Who benefits?","Automation can raise productivity and lower prices, but its benefits may go mainly to company owners and highly skilled workers unless policies spread them more widely. How to share the gains fairly is a central question."],["Humans and machines together","Many experts emphasize working alongside machines, using technology to take over dangerous, dull or repetitive tasks so that people can focus on creative, social and complex work."]],
sp:["The Luddites","Between 1811 and 1816, skilled textile workers in the north of England, facing falling wages and unemployment, broke into workshops at night and smashed new mechanized looms and knitting frames. They claimed to follow a mysterious leader called General Ned Ludd, probably a legendary figure. The government sent thousands of soldiers to stop them and made machine-breaking punishable by death. Today the word Luddite is used for anyone who opposes new technology, but historians point out that the original Luddites were not against machines as such; they were protesting how machines were used to cut wages and worsen their working conditions."],
g:[["Automation","Using machines to do work once done by people"],["Industrial robot","A programmable machine used in manufacturing"],["Retraining","Learning new skills for a different job"],["Universal basic income","Regular payments to all citizens regardless of work"]],
f:["The first industrial robot began work in 1961.","The Luddites protested against machines in 1811–1816.","South Korea uses the most robots per worker."],
fun:"The original Luddites claimed to follow a leader called General Ned Ludd, who probably never existed."});

ADDART({id:"onlinelearning",t:"Online Learning",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎓",w:"Online learning",r:["education","internet","digitaldivide","covid19","remotework"],
p:[["Learning online","Online learning uses the internet to teach and learn."],["Classes for everyone","Free online courses let anyone study subjects from top universities."],["A pandemic test","During COVID-19, schools around the world moved to distance learning."]],
l:"Online learning, also called e-learning or distance learning when done remotely, is education delivered through the internet using videos, live classes, interactive exercises and digital materials. It ranges from online courses at universities and free lessons on websites to language-learning apps and video tutorials. Online learning makes education more flexible and accessible, but it depends on devices, internet access and self-discipline. During the COVID-19 pandemic, about 1.6 billion students were affected by school closures, and many schools, including in the Philippines, turned to online and modular distance learning, revealing both its promise and its limits.",
s:[["Learning at a distance","Distance learning is older than the internet. In the 1840s, Isaac Pitman taught shorthand by post. Radio and television later carried lessons, and the United Kingdom’s Open University, founded in 1969, taught students at home through broadcasts and printed materials."],["The internet era","From the 1990s, universities began offering online courses. In 2008 Khan Academy began posting free video lessons, and in 2012, called the year of the MOOC, massive open online courses from platforms such as Coursera and edX let millions study university subjects for free."],["Kinds of online learning","Some online classes are live, with teachers and students meeting by video. Others are self-paced, with recorded lessons and exercises. Blended learning combines online and face-to-face teaching. Apps teach languages, mathematics and coding through short, game-like lessons."],["Benefits","Online learning lets people study anytime and anywhere, at their own pace, often for free or at low cost. It can reach learners in remote areas, working adults and people with disabilities, and gives access to courses not available locally."]],
k:[["Also called","E-learning, distance learning"],["Open University founded","1969"],["Year of the MOOC","2012"],["Students affected by pandemic closures","About 1.6 billion"],["Philippine pandemic approach","Modular distance learning"]],
d:[["1840","Isaac Pitman teaches shorthand by post"],["1969","The Open University is founded in the United Kingdom"],["2008","Khan Academy begins posting free lessons"],["2012","Massive open online courses become popular"],["2020","School closures affect about 1.6 billion students worldwide"],["2022","Philippine schools return to full in-person classes"]],
s2:[["The Philippines during the pandemic","When schools closed in 2020, the Department of Education adopted distance learning. Most students used printed self-learning modules, while others had online classes or lessons on television and radio. Parents and older siblings often helped as home teachers. Schools returned to full in-person classes in November 2022."],["Challenges","Online learning requires a device, a reliable internet connection and a quiet place to study, which many students lack. It can be harder to stay motivated without classmates and teachers nearby, and young children especially need in-person interaction. Studies found significant learning losses during long school closures worldwide."],["Teachers online","Teachers had to learn new skills quickly during the pandemic, creating digital lessons, using video platforms and finding ways to keep students engaged. Many continue to use online tools in their classrooms."],["Learning anytime","Millions of people learn new skills online through video platforms, podcasts and courses, from cooking and music to programming and languages. Many Filipino workers take online courses to improve their job prospects."],["AI and learning","Artificial intelligence tools can now explain concepts, give feedback and act as tutors. Supporters see them as a way to give every student personal help; critics worry about cheating, errors and students relying on AI instead of thinking for themselves. Schools are developing rules for their use."],["The future","Most experts expect a mix of in-person and online learning. The pandemic showed that schools are important not just for lessons but for friendship, health, safety and support, while online tools can extend learning beyond the classroom."]],
sp:["Khan Academy began with a cousin","In 2004, Salman Khan, a financial analyst in the United States, began tutoring his young cousin Nadia in mathematics over the phone and internet. Soon other relatives wanted help too, so he started recording short video lessons and posting them on YouTube, where anyone could watch. His cousins said they preferred him on video, because they could pause and replay him without feeling embarrassed. The videos became so popular that in 2008 he founded Khan Academy, a nonprofit offering free lessons. Today it has lessons in dozens of languages used by millions of students around the world."],
g:[["Online learning","Education delivered through the internet"],["MOOC","Massive open online course"],["Blended learning","A mix of online and in-person teaching"],["Module","A self-learning booklet or unit of study"]],
f:["The Open University was founded in 1969.","2012 was called the year of the MOOC.","About 1.6 billion students were affected by pandemic school closures."],
fun:"Khan Academy began when Salman Khan tutored his cousin online."});

ADDART({id:"cyberbullying",t:"Cyberbullying",c:"Technology",i:"🛑",w:"Cyberbullying",r:["socialmedia","mentalyouth","dataprivacy","cybersecurity","misinformation"],
p:[["Bullying online","Cyberbullying is using phones, games or the internet to hurt, threaten or embarrass someone."],["It can be stopped","Blocking, reporting and telling a trusted adult help stop it."],["Be an upstander","Friends and bystanders can make a big difference."]],
l:"Cyberbullying is bullying that takes place through digital devices, such as phones, computers and game consoles, on social media, messaging apps, online games and other platforms. It includes sending hurtful messages, spreading rumors, sharing embarrassing photos or videos, excluding people from groups, impersonating them or threatening them. Because online content can spread quickly, reach many people and be hard to remove, cyberbullying can deeply hurt those targeted. Many countries, including the Philippines, have laws and school policies against it, and experts encourage young people to block, report and tell a trusted adult.",
s:[["Forms of cyberbullying","Cyberbullying can include name-calling and insults, spreading lies, posting embarrassing images, creating fake accounts to mock someone, deliberately leaving someone out of group chats, threatening messages and sharing private information or images without permission."],["How it differs","Unlike face-to-face bullying, cyberbullying can happen at any time of day, follow people home and reach large audiences. Bullies may hide behind anonymous accounts, and hurtful posts can be copied and shared widely."],["Effects","People who are cyberbullied may feel sad, anxious, embarrassed, angry or alone, and may avoid school or lose interest in activities. Cyberbullying can affect sleep, grades and mental health. Support from family, friends and schools helps people recover."],["How common is it","Surveys around the world suggest that a significant share of young people have experienced cyberbullying. A 2019 UNICEF poll of young people in 30 countries found that about one in three said they had been bullied online."]],
k:[["Where it happens","Social media, messaging apps, games"],["UNICEF 2019 poll","About 1 in 3 young people bullied online"],["Philippine law","Anti-Bullying Act of 2013"],["First steps","Don’t reply, save evidence, block, report, tell an adult"],["Stop Cyberbullying Day","A Friday in June"]],
d:[["1990s","Online chat rooms bring new forms of harassment"],["2000s","Social media and mobile phones spread cyberbullying"],["2013","The Philippines passes the Anti-Bullying Act"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Safe Spaces Act, covering online harassment"],["2019","A UNICEF poll highlights online bullying of young people"],["2020s","Platforms add more tools to block and report bullying"]],
s2:[["What to do if you are cyberbullied","Experts advise: do not reply or retaliate; take screenshots to save evidence; block the person; report the content to the platform; and tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, teacher or school counselor. If there are threats of violence, the police can help."],["Laws in the Philippines","The Anti-Bullying Act of 2013 requires schools to adopt policies against bullying, including cyberbullying. The Safe Spaces Act of 2019 covers gender-based harassment online, and other laws address threats, identity theft and sharing intimate images without consent."],["Upstanders","Bystanders who see cyberbullying can help by not sharing or liking hurtful content, supporting the person targeted with a kind message, reporting the content and telling an adult. Being an upstander rather than a bystander can stop bullying from spreading."],["Think before you post","Before posting or sharing, it helps to ask: Is it true? Is it kind? Would I say it face to face? Would I want it said about me? Online posts can last a long time and be seen by many people."],["Platforms and schools","Social media companies provide tools to block, mute, restrict and report users and to filter offensive comments. Schools teach digital citizenship, helping students understand respectful behavior online and how to get help."],["Kindness online","Campaigns encourage people to use the internet to spread kindness, such as posting encouraging messages, standing up for others and calling out hate speech. A positive online community makes the internet safer for everyone."]],
sp:["Pink shirts against bullying","In 2007, in Nova Scotia, Canada, a ninth-grade boy was bullied for wearing a pink shirt to school. Two older students, David Shepherd and Travis Price, decided to take a stand. They bought dozens of pink shirts and handed them out, encouraging classmates to wear them. The next day, hundreds of students came to school dressed in pink, and the bullies backed down. Their simple act grew into Pink Shirt Day, an anti-bullying day now marked in many countries, showing how bystanders acting together can stop bullying."],
g:[["Cyberbullying","Bullying through digital devices and the internet"],["Bystander","A person who sees something happen"],["Upstander","A person who acts to help when they see wrongdoing"],["Digital citizenship","Responsible and respectful behavior online"]],
f:["About one in three young people in a UNICEF poll said they were bullied online.","The Philippines passed the Anti-Bullying Act in 2013.","Save evidence, block, report and tell a trusted adult."],
fun:"Pink Shirt Day began when two students handed out pink shirts to stand up to bullies."});

ADDART({id:"digitaldivide",t:"The Digital Divide",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Digital divide",r:["internet","inequality","onlinelearning","smartphone","sat"],
p:[["Connected and unconnected","The digital divide is the gap between people who have internet access and those who do not."],["Billions still offline","More than 2 billion people have never used the internet or rarely do."],["Closing the gap","Cheaper devices, better networks and digital skills help."]],
l:"The digital divide is the gap between people who have access to computers, smartphones and the internet, and the skills to use them, and those who do not. Although most of the world’s people are now online, more than 2 billion remain offline, most of them in low-income countries, rural areas and among women, older people and the poor. Lack of access can limit education, jobs, health care, banking and participation in society. Governments, companies and communities are working to close the gap through better networks, affordable devices and data, public Wi-Fi and digital skills training.",
s:[["Access","The first part of the divide is access: whether there are mobile networks, fiber cables or satellites to connect people, and whether they have devices. Remote islands, mountains and rural areas are often the hardest and most expensive to connect."],["Affordability","Even where networks exist, many people cannot afford smartphones, computers or data plans. The United Nations has set a goal that basic internet should cost no more than about 2 percent of average monthly income."],["Skills and use","Having access is not enough; people also need digital skills to use the internet safely and productively, to find reliable information, protect their privacy and avoid scams. Older people and those with less education often have fewer digital skills."],["Gender and other gaps","In many low-income countries, women are less likely than men to own a mobile phone or use the internet. People with disabilities may face barriers if websites and apps are not designed to be accessible."]],
k:[["People offline","More than 2 billion"],["Most affected","Low-income countries, rural areas, women, older people"],["UN affordability target","Basic internet under about 2% of monthly income"],["Philippine free Wi-Fi law","2017"],["Key solutions","Networks, affordable devices, digital skills"]],
d:[["1990s","The term digital divide comes into use"],["2005","The One Laptop per Child project is launched"],["2016","The UN declares internet access important for human rights"],["2017","The Philippines passes a law for free Wi-Fi in public places"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic highlights the digital divide"],["2020s","Satellite internet reaches more remote areas"]],
s2:[["The Philippines","Internet use in the Philippines has grown rapidly, and Filipinos are among the world’s heaviest social media users. But many remote barangays, islands and mountain communities have weak or no signal, and data costs are a burden for poor families. The Free Internet Access in Public Places Act of 2017 aims to provide free Wi-Fi in public places such as schools, plazas and hospitals, and new cell towers and satellite services are expanding coverage."],["Learning during the pandemic","When schools closed during the COVID-19 pandemic, students without devices or internet fell behind. In the Philippines, many families shared one phone among several children, and students climbed hills or trees to find a signal, which made the digital divide visible to everyone."],["Mobile phones first","In much of Africa and Asia, many people first connected to the internet through mobile phones rather than computers. Mobile money, such as M-Pesa in Kenya, showed how phones can bring services to people without bank branches or computers."],["Satellites and new networks","Satellite internet services can reach remote islands and villages where cables are too expensive. Other approaches include community networks built by local residents and undersea cables linking islands and countries."],["Libraries and community centers","Public libraries, schools and community centers provide computers, internet access and training for people who lack them at home. In the Philippines, local governments and nonprofit groups run digital literacy programs."],["Why it matters","As more services move online, from government forms and job applications to banking, health care and learning, people without access risk being left further behind. Closing the digital divide is part of the Sustainable Development Goals."]],
sp:["Climbing for a signal","During the COVID-19 pandemic, stories and photos spread of students in the Philippines and other countries climbing hills, trees and rooftops, or walking long distances to roadsides, to find a mobile signal strong enough to submit assignments or join online classes. In some communities, residents built small bamboo huts on hilltops as study spots with better reception. These images showed the determination of students, but also how unequal access to the internet could decide who was able to keep learning."],
g:[["Digital divide","The gap between those with and without digital access"],["Connectivity","The ability to connect to the internet"],["Digital literacy","The skills to use digital technology well"],["Accessibility","Design that allows people with disabilities to use something"]],
f:["More than 2 billion people are offline.","The Philippines passed a free public Wi-Fi law in 2017.","Many people first go online through mobile phones."],
fun:"During the pandemic, some students built bamboo huts on hilltops to get a better phone signal."});

ADDART({id:"smarthome",t:"Smart Homes and the Internet of Things",c:"Technology",i:"🏠",w:"Internet of things",r:["internet","ai","dataprivacy","cybersecurity","elec"],
p:[["Connected things","The Internet of Things connects everyday objects to the internet."],["Smart homes","Lights, speakers, cameras and appliances can be controlled by phone or voice."],["Convenience and risks","Smart devices are convenient but raise privacy and security concerns."]],
l:"The Internet of Things, or IoT, is the growing network of everyday objects, such as lights, thermostats, door locks, cameras, appliances, watches, cars, factory machines and farm sensors, that are connected to the internet and can collect data, communicate and be controlled remotely. A smart home uses such devices so that people can control lighting, appliances, security and entertainment with a phone app or voice commands. IoT devices can save energy, improve safety and help older people and people with disabilities live independently, but they also raise concerns about privacy, hacking and electronic waste.",
s:[["Origins","In 1982 students at Carnegie Mellon University connected a soda machine to their network so they could check whether drinks were cold before walking to it. In 1999 the British technologist Kevin Ashton coined the phrase Internet of Things. Cheap sensors, wireless networks and smartphones later made connected devices common."],["Smart home devices","Common smart home devices include voice-controlled speakers, smart lights and plugs, security cameras and video doorbells, smart locks, thermostats and air conditioners, robot vacuum cleaners and smart televisions. Many can be programmed to follow schedules or respond to sensors."],["Voice assistants","Smart speakers with voice assistants, such as Amazon’s Alexa, launched in 2014, and Google Assistant, let people play music, set timers, check the weather and control devices by speaking. They use artificial intelligence to understand speech."],["Beyond the home","The IoT is used in cities to manage traffic lights and streetlights, in farms to monitor soil moisture and livestock, in hospitals to track equipment and patients, in factories to predict machine failures and in disaster monitoring, such as flood and weather sensors."]],
k:[["Term coined","1999, by Kevin Ashton"],["Early connected device","A soda machine at Carnegie Mellon (1982)"],["Smart speaker with Alexa launched","2014"],["Common standard","Matter (2022)"],["Main concerns","Privacy, security, e-waste"]],
d:[["1982","Students connect a soda machine to a network"],["1991","A coffee pot webcam goes online at Cambridge"],["1999","The term Internet of Things is coined"],["2014","Amazon launches a smart speaker with Alexa"],["2016","A huge cyberattack uses hacked IoT devices"],["2022","The Matter standard aims to make smart devices work together"]],
s2:[["Energy saving","Smart thermostats, air conditioners, lights and plugs can turn devices off when they are not needed and schedule them for cheaper times. Smart electricity meters help households and utilities track power use."],["Helping people live independently","Smart devices can help older people and people with disabilities by allowing voice control of lights and doors, sending reminders to take medicine and alerting family members or caregivers if someone falls."],["Security risks","Many IoT devices have weak security, such as default passwords that are never changed. In 2016 hackers used a program called Mirai to take over hundreds of thousands of poorly secured cameras and routers, using them to launch a huge attack that disrupted major websites. Experts advise changing default passwords and updating devices."],["Privacy","Smart speakers, cameras and other devices collect data about people’s homes and habits. Users should check privacy settings, know when devices are listening or recording and be careful about cameras in private spaces."],["Smart devices in the Philippines","Smart home devices, such as Wi-Fi plugs, cameras and smart TVs, are increasingly common in Filipino homes, along with community uses such as CCTV networks in barangays and sensors that warn of rising river levels during typhoons."],["Electronic waste","As smart devices multiply, they add to electronic waste, especially when they stop receiving software updates or their companies shut down services. Repairable designs and longer support periods can help."]],
sp:["The coffee pot that made history","In 1991, researchers at the University of Cambridge in England grew tired of walking to the coffee pot in the Trojan Room only to find it empty. They pointed a camera at the pot and wrote software so they could see a small picture of it on their computer screens. In 1993 the image was put on the World Wide Web, making it one of the first webcams. People around the world logged on just to watch a coffee pot. When it was finally switched off in 2001, the pot was sold at auction for thousands of pounds."],
g:[["Internet of Things","Everyday objects connected to the internet"],["Smart home","A home with devices controlled remotely or automatically"],["Sensor","A device that detects changes such as heat, light or motion"],["Voice assistant","Software that responds to spoken commands"]],
f:["The term Internet of Things was coined in 1999.","A Cambridge coffee pot was one of the first webcams.","Weak passwords make smart devices vulnerable to hackers."],
fun:"One of the first webcams showed a university coffee pot."});

ADDART({id:"housing",t:"Housing and Homes",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏘️",w:"Affordable housing",r:["urban","megacities","inequality","costliving","architecture"],
p:[["A place to live","Everyone needs a safe, decent and affordable home."],["A global challenge","Billions of people live in inadequate or unaffordable housing."],["Different solutions","Countries try public housing, new building, subsidies and slum upgrading."]],
l:"Housing is a basic human need and is recognized as a human right in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Yet around the world, billions of people live in housing that is crowded, unsafe, insecure or too expensive, and around a billion live in slums or informal settlements. In many cities, house prices and rents have risen much faster than incomes, making homes unaffordable for young people and low-income families. Governments, builders, communities and charities try different solutions, from public housing and subsidies to slum upgrading and community-built homes, and the best approach is widely debated.",
s:[["What makes housing adequate","According to the United Nations, adequate housing means more than four walls and a roof: it includes security from eviction, access to water, sanitation and electricity, affordability, safety, enough space, access to jobs and schools and respect for culture."],["Why housing is expensive","House prices and rents rise when demand grows faster than supply, as when cities attract many workers. Land costs, building rules, slow approvals, interest rates, speculation and investors buying homes can all push prices up. Economists debate which factors matter most in each place."],["Informal settlements","In many fast-growing cities, people who cannot afford formal housing build homes on unused land, along rivers, railways or hillsides, often without secure ownership or services. These communities are vulnerable to floods, fires and evictions, but they also show great resourcefulness."],["Homelessness","Homelessness affects people in rich and poor countries alike. Causes include poverty, high rents, unemployment, family breakdown, disasters and mental illness. Approaches such as Housing First, which gives homeless people a stable home before addressing other problems, have shown success."]],
k:[["Human right","Recognized in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights"],["People in slums","Around 1 billion"],["Singapore public housing","About 80% of residents"],["Philippine housing program","Pambansang Pabahay para sa Pilipino (2023)"],["World Habitat Day","First Monday of October"]],
d:[["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights recognizes the right to housing"],["1960","Singapore creates its Housing and Development Board"],["1976","Habitat for Humanity is founded"],["2003","Gawad Kalinga begins building communities in the Philippines"],["2016","The UN adopts the New Urban Agenda"],["2023","The Philippines launches a national housing program"]],
s2:[["Housing in the Philippines","The Philippines has a large housing backlog, estimated in the millions of units, and many families live in informal settlements, especially in Metro Manila. Government agencies, the Pag-IBIG Fund and private developers build housing, and the Pambansang Pabahay para sa Pilipino program, launched in 2023, aims to build large numbers of affordable homes. Critics have raised concerns about affordability and location."],["Singapore’s public housing","Since 1960, Singapore’s Housing and Development Board has built high-rise public housing estates where about 80 percent of residents live, most of them owning their flats on long leases. The system is admired for providing affordable, well-planned homes, though it relies on unique conditions such as state ownership of most land."],["Vienna’s social housing","In Vienna, Austria, a large share of residents live in social or city-owned housing, built since the 1920s, with good design and affordable rents, mixing people of different incomes. Many cities study Vienna’s model."],["Community-built homes","Organizations such as Habitat for Humanity, founded in 1976, and Gawad Kalinga in the Philippines, founded in 2003, help families build homes with volunteers, often requiring families to contribute their own labor, known as sweat equity, reflecting the Filipino tradition of bayanihan."],["Debates","Some argue that the main solution is to build many more homes by loosening building rules. Others emphasize rent controls, public and social housing, taxes on vacant homes and limits on speculation. Many experts suggest combining several approaches suited to local conditions."],["Building better","Homes in disaster-prone places, like the Philippines, need to withstand typhoons, floods and earthquakes. Architects are developing affordable, climate-resilient and energy-efficient designs, including homes built with bamboo and other local materials."]],
sp:["Bayanihan: moving a house together","In the traditional Filipino custom of bayanihan, when a family in a village needed to move, neighbors would gather to lift their entire nipa hut on bamboo poles and carry it on their shoulders to its new location, sometimes dozens of men walking together. In return, the family would prepare a meal for everyone. The image of villagers carrying a house became a symbol of community spirit in the Philippines, celebrated in art and the famous painting by Carlos Botong Francisco. Today the word bayanihan is used for any act of communities working together, including building homes for those in need."],
g:[["Adequate housing","Safe, secure, affordable housing with basic services"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing without secure ownership"],["Public housing","Homes built or owned by the government"],["Bayanihan","The Filipino spirit of community cooperation"]],
f:["Housing is recognized as a human right.","About 80 percent of Singapore residents live in public housing.","Bayanihan once meant carrying a neighbor’s house together."],
fun:"In traditional bayanihan, neighbors carried a whole nipa hut on their shoulders."});

ADDART({id:"loneliness",t:"Loneliness and Social Connection",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤝",w:"Loneliness",r:["mental","family","agingworld","mentalyouth","socialmedia"],
p:[["Feeling alone","Loneliness is the painful feeling of having fewer close connections than you want."],["A public health issue","Health organizations now treat loneliness as a serious health concern."],["Connection helps","Friendships, family and community protect health and happiness."]],
l:"Loneliness is the distressing feeling that one’s social relationships are fewer or less close than one would like. It is different from being alone; people can feel lonely in a crowd or content in solitude. Research shows that long-lasting loneliness and social isolation are linked to higher risks of heart disease, stroke, depression, dementia and early death. In 2025 a World Health Organization commission estimated that about one in six people worldwide is affected by loneliness. Governments, health organizations and communities increasingly treat social connection as a public health priority.",
s:[["Loneliness and isolation","Social isolation means having few social contacts, while loneliness is the feeling of lacking connection. Both can harm health. Older people living alone, young people, people with disabilities, migrants and those who are poor or face discrimination may be especially at risk."],["Effects on health","Studies link chronic loneliness to higher blood pressure, weaker immunity, poorer sleep and greater risk of heart disease, depression and anxiety. Some researchers have compared the health risks of severe social disconnection with those of smoking."],["Causes","Loneliness can be caused by moving away, losing a loved one, illness, unemployment, long working hours, living alone, bullying or difficulties making friends. Changes in society, such as smaller families, urban living and less participation in community groups, may also contribute."],["Young people","Surprisingly, surveys in many countries find that young adults report loneliness as often as or more often than older people. The roles of social media and screens are debated: they can help people stay connected but may also replace face-to-face time."]],
k:[["People affected by loneliness","About 1 in 6 worldwide (WHO, 2025)"],["UK minister for loneliness","Appointed 2018"],["Japan minister for loneliness","Appointed 2021"],["WHO commission on social connection","Launched 2023"],["Best remedy","Meaningful relationships and community"]],
d:[["1938","A long-running Harvard study of adult life begins"],["2018","The United Kingdom appoints a minister for loneliness"],["2021","Japan appoints a minister for loneliness"],["2023","The WHO launches a commission on social connection"],["2023","US health officials call loneliness an epidemic"],["2025","A WHO report estimates loneliness affects one in six people"]],
s2:[["What helps","Ways to build connection include reaching out to friends and family regularly, joining clubs, sports teams, religious groups or volunteer activities, talking to neighbors, sharing meals and helping others. Even small interactions, such as greeting a shop owner, can lift mood."],["Communities in the Philippines","Filipino culture values close family ties, barkada friendships, fiestas and bayanihan, which can protect against loneliness. But many Filipinos experience long separations from loved ones, such as families of overseas workers. Video calls, community groups and shared celebrations help families stay close across distances."],["The Harvard study","Since 1938, a Harvard University study has followed hundreds of men, and later their families, throughout their lives. One of its main findings is that close, warm relationships are a strong predictor of long, healthy and happy lives, more than wealth or fame."],["Governments act","In 2018 the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness, and Japan did the same in 2021. Some doctors now practice social prescribing, referring lonely patients to community activities such as gardening clubs, art classes or walking groups."],["Older people","Older people may become isolated after retirement, the death of a partner or loss of mobility. Programs that bring generations together, such as students visiting elderly neighbors or shared community centers, benefit both young and old."],["Being alone is not always lonely","Time alone can be restful and creative, and some people enjoy solitude. Loneliness becomes a problem when people feel unwanted, misunderstood or disconnected for long periods. Anyone who feels persistently lonely or low can talk to someone they trust or seek help from a health professional."]],
sp:["Chatty benches","In 2019, police in the town of Burnham-on-Sea in England put up signs on park benches reading: Happy to chat bench. Sit here if you don’t mind someone stopping to say hello. The idea was simple: anyone who sat on the bench was signaling that they would welcome a conversation with a stranger. Chat benches spread to many towns in the United Kingdom and other countries, from Australia to Canada, helping lonely people, especially older people, find someone to talk to. Some cafés followed with chatter tables where customers could join others for conversation."],
g:[["Loneliness","The painful feeling of lacking close connections"],["Social isolation","Having few social contacts"],["Social prescribing","Doctors referring patients to community activities"],["Barkada","A Filipino word for a close group of friends"]],
f:["About one in six people worldwide is affected by loneliness.","The UK appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018.","Close relationships predict long, healthy lives."],
fun:"Some towns have chat benches where sitting down means you are happy to talk."});

ADDART({id:"urbanfarm",t:"Urban Farming",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥬",w:"Urban agriculture",r:["agri","foodsecurity","urban","megacities","recycling"],
p:[["Farms in the city","Urban farming means growing food in and around cities."],["Rooftops and walls","Food grows on rooftops, balconies, vacant lots and even indoors."],["Fresh and local","City farms provide fresh food, green space and community."]],
l:"Urban farming, or urban agriculture, is the growing of food, and sometimes raising of animals, in and around cities, on rooftops, balconies, backyards, vacant lots, schoolyards, walls and even indoors. It ranges from family vegetable gardens and community gardens to high-tech vertical farms. Urban farming can provide fresh, nutritious food, income, green spaces, cooler neighborhoods and stronger communities, and it can make cities more resilient to food price shocks and disasters. In the Philippines, urban gardening grew rapidly during the COVID-19 pandemic. Challenges include limited space, soil contamination and costs.",
s:[["An old idea","People have always grown food near their homes. The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan fed its people with chinampas, floating gardens. During the World Wars, millions of people in Britain, the United States and elsewhere planted victory gardens in backyards and parks to grow food."],["Kinds of urban farms","Urban farms include home gardens, community gardens shared by neighbors, school gardens, rooftop farms, container gardens on balconies and indoor farms. Some raise chickens, fish, bees or mushrooms."],["Growing without soil","Hydroponics grows plants in nutrient-rich water instead of soil, and aquaponics combines fish tanks with plant beds, with fish waste fertilizing the plants. Vertical farms grow crops in stacked layers indoors under LED lights, using little water and space."],["Benefits","Urban farms provide fresh vegetables close to where people live, reduce transport, reuse food waste as compost, cool the air, absorb rainwater and create green spaces where neighbors meet. Gardening is also good exercise and can reduce stress."]],
k:[["Also called","Urban agriculture"],["Where food grows","Rooftops, balconies, vacant lots, indoors"],["Soil-free methods","Hydroponics, aquaponics"],["Historic example","Victory gardens in the World Wars"],["Philippine program","Plant, Plant, Plant (2020)"]],
d:[["1300s","The Aztecs farm chinampas in Tenochtitlan"],["1940s","Victory gardens spread during World War II"],["1990s","Havana turns to urban organic farms during an economic crisis"],["2010","Quezon City launches an urban farming program"],["2019","Singapore sets a goal to grow more of its own food"],["2020","The Philippines promotes home gardening during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["Havana’s organopónicos","When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, Cuba lost much of its imported food, fuel and fertilizer, causing severe shortages. In response, people in Havana turned vacant lots and rooftops into organic gardens called organopónicos, which produced large amounts of vegetables for the city. Havana became a famous example of urban farming."],["Urban gardening in the Philippines","During the COVID-19 pandemic, many Filipinos started growing vegetables at home in pots, recycled containers and small plots. The Department of Agriculture’s Plant, Plant, Plant program distributed seeds and seedlings, and local governments, such as Quezon City with its Joy of Urban Farming program, supported community gardens. Popular crops include pechay, kangkong, tomatoes, eggplant and okra."],["Singapore","Singapore, a small island city-state that imports most of its food, set a goal in 2019 to produce much more of its own food, encouraging rooftop farms, vertical farms and fish farms. Progress proved harder than expected, and the targets were later revised."],["Challenges","City soil may be contaminated by lead or other pollutants, so gardeners often test soil or use raised beds and containers. Space, water, sunlight, time and the cost of high-tech systems can also be challenges. Urban farms can supplement but not replace rural farming."],["Schools and gardens","School gardens teach children where food comes from, how plants grow and how to eat healthily. In the Philippines, the Gulayan sa Paaralan program encourages schools to grow vegetables, which can be used in school feeding programs."],["Start your own","Anyone can start small: herbs such as basil or tanglad in a pot, kangkong in a bucket of water, or vegetables in recycled plastic bottles. Compost from kitchen scraps can enrich the soil, turning waste into food."]],
sp:["Kangkong in a bottle","One of the easiest vegetables to grow in a small space is kangkong, water spinach, a favorite in Filipino dishes such as adobong kangkong and sinigang. Gardeners can take kangkong stems bought at the market, remove the leaves for cooking and place the stems in a container of water or moist soil near a sunny window. Within days, roots appear and new leaves sprout, ready to harvest within weeks. Many Filipino families grow kangkong in cut plastic bottles hung on walls or fences, turning trash into a small garden."],
g:[["Urban agriculture","Growing food in and around cities"],["Hydroponics","Growing plants in water with nutrients instead of soil"],["Vertical farm","An indoor farm with crops grown in stacked layers"],["Compost","Decayed organic matter used to enrich soil"]],
f:["Victory gardens fed people during the World Wars.","Hydroponics grows plants without soil.","Many Filipinos started home gardens during the pandemic."],
fun:"Kangkong can regrow from market stems placed in a bottle of water."});

;
/* data/lang2.js */
// Languages, part 2: Japanese, Korean, Hindi, French, Swahili and Malay–Indonesian.
ADDART({id:"japanese",t:"Japanese",c:"Languages",i:"🗾",w:"Japanese language",r:["japancountry","murasaki","haikuart","anime","mandarin"],
p:[["Three scripts","Japanese is written with three scripts used together: hiragana, katakana and kanji."],["Polite speech","Japanese has special forms for showing respect."],["Words for the world","Words such as tsunami, karaoke and emoji come from Japanese."]],
l:"Japanese is the language of Japan, spoken by about 125 million people, almost all in Japan, with communities abroad in places such as Brazil, the United States, Peru and the Philippines. It is unusual for using three scripts together: kanji, characters borrowed from Chinese that represent meanings, and two syllabic scripts, hiragana and katakana, that represent sounds. Japanese has a rich system of polite and honorific language and has given the world words such as tsunami, karaoke, sushi, anime and emoji. Its literature includes The Tale of Genji, often called the world’s first novel, and haiku poetry.",
s:[["Where it comes from","Japanese belongs to the small Japonic family, which also includes the Ryukyuan languages of Okinawa. Its origins are debated. Over centuries it borrowed many words from Chinese, much as English borrowed from Latin and French."],["Kanji","Kanji are characters adopted from Chinese writing, beginning around the 400s CE. Each represents a meaning and can have more than one pronunciation. Japanese students learn about 2,000 commonly used kanji by the end of high school."],["Hiragana and katakana","Hiragana and katakana each have about 46 basic characters, each standing for a syllable, such as ka, ki or ku. Hiragana is used for grammar endings and native words, while katakana is used mainly for foreign words, names and emphasis. Japanese can also be written in the Latin alphabet, called romaji."],["Grammar","Japanese usually places the verb at the end of a sentence, as in I sushi eat. It uses small words called particles to show the role of each word, has no plural endings for most nouns and often leaves out the subject when it is clear from context."]],
k:[["Speakers","About 125 million"],["Scripts","Kanji, hiragana, katakana"],["Family","Japonic"],["Oldest surviving book","Kojiki (712)"],["Words in English","Tsunami, karaoke, emoji, sushi"]],
d:[["400s","Chinese characters reach Japan"],["712","The Kojiki, Japan’s oldest surviving book, is completed"],["c. 900","Hiragana and katakana develop"],["c. 1010","Murasaki Shikibu writes The Tale of Genji"],["1543","Portuguese traders arrive, bringing new words"],["1946","Japan simplifies kanji and publishes an official list"]],
s2:[["Politeness","Japanese has several levels of polite speech, called keigo, used depending on who is speaking to whom. People use humble forms for themselves and respectful forms for others, such as customers, teachers and elders. Even simple words, such as eat or go, change in polite speech."],["Borrowed words","Japanese has borrowed many words from other languages. Pan, meaning bread, came from Portuguese in the 1500s. Today many words come from English, adapted to Japanese sounds, such as konpyūtā for computer and terebi for television."],["Japanese words in English","English has borrowed Japanese words such as tsunami, karaoke, sushi, origami, anime, manga, futon, tycoon and emoji. Emoji comes from e, meaning picture, and moji, meaning character, and is not related to the English word emotion."],["Japanese in the Philippines","Before World War II, a large Japanese community lived in Davao, working on abacá plantations. Many Filipinos study Japanese today for work, study and travel, and Japanese is one of the foreign languages offered in some Philippine high schools. Anime and manga have also inspired many Filipino learners."],["Writing vertically","Japanese can be written horizontally, left to right, or vertically, from top to bottom and right to left. Newspapers, novels and manga often use vertical writing, while websites and science books usually use horizontal writing."],["Learning Japanese","Japanese pronunciation is fairly simple, with few vowel sounds, and is relatively easy for Filipino speakers. The writing system, especially kanji, takes longer to learn. Many learners take the Japanese-Language Proficiency Test, offered around the world."]],
sp:["Emoji, a Japanese word","In 1999, Shigetaka Kurita, working for the Japanese mobile company NTT Docomo, designed a set of 176 tiny pictures, each just 12 by 12 pixels, so that phone users could express emotions and ideas quickly in short messages: hearts, faces, weather symbols and more. They were called emoji, from the Japanese words for picture and character. After emoji were added to international computer standards and smartphones in the 2010s, they spread worldwide. Kurita’s original set is now in the collection of the Museum of Modern Art in New York."],
g:[["Kanji","Chinese characters used in Japanese"],["Hiragana","A Japanese script for native words and grammar"],["Katakana","A Japanese script mainly for foreign words"],["Keigo","Polite and honorific Japanese"]],
f:["Japanese uses three scripts together.","The Kojiki of 712 is Japan’s oldest surviving book.","The word emoji comes from Japanese."],
fun:"The first emoji were just 12 by 12 pixels."});

ADDART({id:"korean",t:"Korean",c:"Languages",i:"📝",w:"Korean language",r:["sejong","southkoreacountry","kpop","kdrama","alphabet"],
p:[["A scientific alphabet","Korean is written in Hangul, an alphabet invented in the 1400s."],["Two Koreas","Korean is spoken in South Korea, North Korea and by Koreans worldwide."],["Popular to learn","K-pop and K-dramas have made Korean popular with learners around the world."]],
l:"Korean is the language of South Korea and North Korea, spoken by about 80 million people, including Korean communities in China, the United States, Japan and elsewhere. It is written in Hangul, an alphabet created in 1443 under King Sejong the Great and admired by linguists for its logical design, in which letter shapes reflect how sounds are made. Korean has a complex system of speech levels for showing respect. The global popularity of K-pop and Korean dramas has made it one of the fastest-growing languages for learners, including in the Philippines.",
s:[["Where it comes from","Korean is usually considered a language isolate or part of the small Koreanic family, with no proven relatives, though some scholars have suggested links to other Asian languages. Over many centuries, it borrowed a large number of words from Chinese."],["Before Hangul","For centuries, Koreans wrote using Chinese characters, called hanja, which were difficult to learn and did not fit the Korean language well. Only the educated elite could read and write."],["The creation of Hangul","In 1443 King Sejong and his scholars created a new alphabet, announced in 1446 in a document called Hunminjeongeum, the correct sounds for the instruction of the people. Sejong wanted ordinary people to be able to read and write. Some scholars opposed it, and it took centuries to replace Chinese characters fully."],["How Hangul works","Hangul has 24 basic letters, 14 consonants and 10 vowels. Letters are grouped into square syllable blocks, so a word looks somewhat like Chinese characters but is made of alphabet letters. The shapes of consonants imitate the position of the mouth and tongue when making the sound."]],
k:[["Speakers","About 80 million"],["Alphabet","Hangul (created 1443)"],["Creator","King Sejong the Great"],["Basic letters","24"],["Hangul Day","9 October (South Korea)"]],
d:[["1443","King Sejong creates Hangul"],["1446","Hangul is officially announced"],["1504","A king bans the use of Hangul"],["1894","Hangul becomes official in government documents"],["1945","Korea is divided into North and South"],["2017","Korean is added to foreign language classes in Philippine public schools"]],
s2:[["Speech levels","Korean has several speech levels that change verb endings depending on the relationship between speakers, such as age and social position. Speaking to elders, teachers or strangers uses polite forms, while close friends may use casual speech. Learning when to use each level is an important part of Korean culture."],["North and South","After Korea was divided in 1945, the Korean spoken in North and South Korea gradually developed differences in vocabulary, spelling and pronunciation. South Korean Korean has borrowed many English words, while North Korea has tried to replace foreign words with native Korean ones."],["Hangul Day","South Korea celebrates Hangul Day on 9 October as a national holiday, and North Korea marks its own Korean Alphabet Day in January. Hangul is a source of great national pride."],["Korean in the Philippines","Interest in Korean has grown rapidly in the Philippines, thanks to K-pop, K-dramas and work and study opportunities. In 2017 Korean became one of the foreign languages offered in Philippine public high schools, and many Filipinos learn it online and in language centers."],["Korean words","Words now known around the world include kimchi, hallyu, taekwondo, chaebol and oppa, a word used by younger women for an older brother or male friend, popularized by K-pop and dramas."],["Easy to read","Because Hangul is so systematic, many learners can learn to read it in a few hours or days, though understanding the language takes much longer. King Sejong is said to have written that a wise person can learn it in a morning."]],
sp:["A wise person can learn it in a morning","When King Sejong announced his new alphabet in 1446, an accompanying text praised its simplicity, saying that a wise person could learn it in a morning, and even a less clever person within ten days. Sejong designed it so that farmers, women and servants, who had no time to master thousands of Chinese characters, could read and write and make their voices heard. Today South Korea has one of the highest literacy rates in the world, and UNESCO named its literacy prize after King Sejong."],
g:[["Hangul","The Korean alphabet"],["Hanja","Chinese characters used in Korean"],["Syllable block","A group of Hangul letters forming one syllable"],["Speech level","A form of speech showing respect or familiarity"]],
f:["Hangul was created in 1443 under King Sejong.","Hangul has 24 basic letters.","Hangul Day is 9 October in South Korea."],
fun:"Hangul’s creators said a wise person could learn it in a morning."});

ADDART({id:"hindi",t:"Hindi",c:"Languages",i:"🕉️",w:"Hindi",r:["indiacountry","bollywood","indianphil","hindu","alphabet"],
p:[["One of the biggest languages","Hindi is one of the most spoken languages in the world."],["Devanagari script","It is written in Devanagari, with a line along the top of the letters."],["Words we share","English words such as shampoo and pajamas come from Hindi."]],
l:"Hindi is an Indo-Aryan language spoken mainly in northern and central India, where it is one of the two official languages of the national government, alongside English. With hundreds of millions of native speakers and many more who speak it as a second language, Hindi is among the most spoken languages in the world. It is written in the Devanagari script and is closely related to Urdu; together they are sometimes called Hindustani. Hindi is the language of most Bollywood films and songs, and it has given English words such as shampoo, jungle, bungalow and pajamas.",
s:[["Where it comes from","Hindi descends from Sanskrit, the ancient classical language of India, through medieval languages called Prakrits and Apabhramsha. It belongs to the Indo-European family, making it a distant relative of English, Persian, Spanish and Russian."],["Devanagari","Hindi is written left to right in Devanagari, an abugida in which each consonant carries a built-in vowel that can be changed with marks. Most letters hang from a horizontal line called the shirorekha. Devanagari is also used for Sanskrit, Marathi and Nepali."],["Hindi and Urdu","Everyday spoken Hindi and Urdu are very similar and largely understandable to each other. They differ in script, Urdu using a Persian-Arabic script, and in formal vocabulary, with Hindi drawing on Sanskrit and Urdu on Persian and Arabic. Urdu is the national language of Pakistan."],["Official language","India’s constitution, adopted in 1950, made Hindi in Devanagari the official language of the central government, with English also used. India recognizes 22 scheduled languages, and many Indians speak their regional language, Hindi and English."]],
k:[["Speakers","Hundreds of millions (one of the most spoken languages)"],["Script","Devanagari"],["Family","Indo-Aryan (Indo-European)"],["Close relative","Urdu"],["Hindi Day","14 September"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Early forms of Hindi emerge"],["1500s","Poets such as Kabir and Tulsidas write in early Hindi"],["1949","India’s Constituent Assembly adopts Hindi as an official language"],["1950","India’s constitution takes effect"],["1975","The first World Hindi Conference is held"],["2006","World Hindi Day is first celebrated on 10 January"]],
s2:[["Hindi in film and music","Most Bollywood films are in Hindi, often mixed with Urdu and English, and their songs are loved across South Asia, the Middle East, Africa and beyond. Films have helped spread Hindi to people whose first language is different."],["Words from Hindi in English","English borrowed many words from Hindi and related languages during British rule in India, including shampoo, from champo, to press or massage, jungle, bungalow, pajamas, loot, thug, cheetah, bandana and guru."],["Sanskrit words in Filipino","Long before the Spanish arrived, Sanskrit words reached the Philippines through trade with Malay and Indianized kingdoms of Southeast Asia. Filipino words such as guro, teacher, from guru, diwa, spirit, mukha, face, and budhi, conscience, share roots with Sanskrit words that are also found in Hindi."],["Languages of India","India has hundreds of languages from several families, including Indo-Aryan languages such as Hindi, Bengali and Marathi, and Dravidian languages such as Tamil, Telugu and Kannada. Debates over whether Hindi should be promoted as a national language have sometimes caused tension in non-Hindi-speaking states, especially in the south."],["Hindi around the world","Hindi and related languages are spoken by communities of Indian origin in Fiji, Mauritius, Suriname, Trinidad and Tobago, the Gulf countries, the United Kingdom and the United States, as well as by Indian communities in the Philippines."],["Greetings","Namaste, said with palms pressed together and a slight bow, is a common Hindi greeting meaning I bow to you. It has become widely known around the world, especially through yoga."]],
sp:["Shampoo, a gift from India","The word shampoo comes from the Hindi word champo, meaning to press or massage, related to a word for kneading the body. In the 1700s, British travelers in India encountered head massages with oils and herbs. In 1814 Sake Dean Mahomed, a businessman from India, opened a bathhouse in Brighton, England, offering shampooing, a therapeutic massage with steam and oils. It became fashionable, and he was even appointed shampooing surgeon to King George IV. Only later did the word shampoo come to mean washing hair with soap."],
g:[["Devanagari","The script used to write Hindi"],["Abugida","A writing system where consonants carry built-in vowels"],["Sanskrit","The ancient classical language of India"],["Hindustani","The shared everyday language of Hindi and Urdu"]],
f:["Hindi is written in Devanagari.","Hindi and Urdu are closely related.","The word shampoo comes from Hindi."],
fun:"An Indian businessman was once appointed shampooing surgeon to a British king."});

ADDART({id:"french",t:"French",c:"Languages",i:"🥐",w:"French language",r:["francecountry","latin","english","spanish","africacolon"],
p:[["A world language","French is spoken on five continents."],["From Latin","French is a Romance language that grew from Latin."],["Africa’s French","Most French speakers today live in Africa."]],
l:"French is a Romance language that developed from the Latin spoken in Roman Gaul, now France. Today more than 300 million people speak it, and it is an official language in more than two dozen countries in Europe, Africa, the Americas and the Pacific, including France, Belgium, Switzerland, Canada, Haiti and many African countries. Most French speakers now live in Africa. French was long the language of diplomacy and remains an official language of the United Nations, the Olympic Games and many international organizations. It has strongly influenced English, which borrowed thousands of French words.",
s:[["From Latin to French","When the Roman Empire ruled Gaul, people adopted Latin. After Rome fell, the Latin spoken there changed over centuries, influenced by the Germanic Franks, who gave France its name. The Strasbourg Oaths of 842 are one of the earliest texts in an early form of French."],["Standard French","In 1539 King Francis I ordered that official documents be written in French instead of Latin. In 1635 Cardinal Richelieu founded the Académie française to protect and standardize the language, a role it still plays, issuing a dictionary and guidance."],["French around the world","Through colonization, French spread to North America, the Caribbean, Africa, the Middle East, Southeast Asia and the Pacific. After independence, many countries kept French as an official language, alongside local languages."],["Sounds and spelling","French is known for nasal vowels, a throaty r and silent letters at the ends of words. Its spelling is complex, with many letters not pronounced. It has accents, such as é and è, and nouns are either masculine or feminine."]],
k:[["Speakers","More than 300 million"],["Family","Romance (from Latin)"],["Most speakers live in","Africa"],["Académie française founded","1635"],["International Francophonie Day","20 March"]],
d:[["842","The Strasbourg Oaths are written in early French"],["1066","The Norman Conquest brings French to England"],["1539","French replaces Latin in official documents in France"],["1635","The Académie française is founded"],["1970","The organization of French-speaking countries is founded"],["2020s","Africa is home to most of the world’s French speakers"]],
s2:[["French in English","After the Norman Conquest of England in 1066, French became the language of the English court and law for centuries. English absorbed thousands of French words, such as government, justice, beef, pork, restaurant, fashion and ballet. By some estimates, a large share of English vocabulary comes from French or Latin through French."],["Africa","Countries such as the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Côte d’Ivoire, Cameroon, Senegal, Madagascar and Mali use French in government and schools, and Kinshasa is often described as the largest French-speaking city in the world. African French includes many local words and expressions."],["Canada","In Canada, French and English are both official languages. The province of Quebec, where most people speak French, has laws to protect the language, and Canadian French has its own accent and vocabulary."],["La Francophonie","The International Organisation of La Francophonie, founded in 1970, brings together countries and governments that share the French language, promoting culture, education and cooperation. International Francophonie Day is celebrated on 20 March."],["Language of diplomacy","From the 1600s to the early 1900s, French was the main language of European diplomacy and of educated elites. It remains an official language of the United Nations, the European Union, the African Union and the Olympic Games, where announcements are made in French first."],["French and the Philippines","French is one of the foreign languages taught in some Philippine schools and universities, and Filipino workers in Canada, Africa and Europe often learn it. A few Filipino words come from French through Spanish, and French cuisine has influenced Filipino baking."]],
sp:["Beef from the French, cows from the English","After the Normans conquered England in 1066, French-speaking nobles ruled over English-speaking peasants. This left a curious trace in English. The farmers who raised the animals used old English words: cow, pig, sheep and deer. But the nobles who ate the meat used French words: boeuf became beef, porc became pork, mouton became mutton and venaison became venison. Today English speakers still use English words for the animals in the field and French-derived words for the meat on the plate."],
g:[["Romance language","A language that developed from Latin"],["Francophone","French-speaking"],["Nasal vowel","A vowel pronounced with air passing through the nose"],["Académie française","The institution that guards the French language"]],
f:["Most French speakers live in Africa.","The Académie française was founded in 1635.","English borrowed thousands of French words."],
fun:"English uses English words for farm animals but French words for their meat."});

ADDART({id:"swahili",t:"Swahili",c:"Languages",i:"🌍",w:"Swahili language",r:["tanzaniacountry","kenyacountry","ugandacountry","africacolon","arabic"],
p:[["East Africa’s language","Swahili is spoken by tens of millions of people across East and Central Africa."],["A language of trade","It grew on the coast through trade between Africans, Arabs and others."],["Hakuna matata","The phrase hakuna matata, meaning no worries, is Swahili."]],
l:"Swahili, known to its speakers as Kiswahili, is a Bantu language that is the most widely spoken African language, used by an estimated 200 million people as a first or second language across East and Central Africa, including Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, Rwanda, Burundi, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Mozambique. It developed over many centuries on the East African coast, where Bantu-speaking communities traded with Arab, Persian and Indian merchants, and it contains many Arabic loanwords. Swahili is an official language in several countries and a working language of the African Union, and words such as safari and hakuna matata are known worldwide.",
s:[["Origins","Swahili grew among the people of the East African coast, from Somalia to Mozambique, in trading towns such as Kilwa, Mombasa, Lamu and Zanzibar. Its grammar is Bantu, but it borrowed many words from Arabic, as well as from Persian, Portuguese, Hindi and English. The name comes from the Arabic word sawahil, meaning coasts."],["Spreading inland","Traders, caravans and later colonial governments and missionaries spread Swahili inland. After independence, Tanzania’s first president, Julius Nyerere, made Swahili the national language to unite more than a hundred ethnic groups, and he translated Shakespeare’s plays into Swahili."],["Writing","Swahili was first written in Arabic script; the oldest known manuscripts date to the 1700s. Since the colonial period, it has been written in the Latin alphabet. Swahili spelling is very regular, so words are pronounced as they are written."],["Grammar","Swahili nouns are grouped into many noun classes, marked by prefixes, which affect other words in the sentence. For example, mtoto means child and watoto means children. Verbs are built by adding prefixes for subject, tense and object to a root."]],
k:[["Speakers","About 200 million (first and second language)"],["Family","Bantu"],["Name means","Coasts (from Arabic)"],["Official in","Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, Rwanda and others"],["World Kiswahili Language Day","7 July"]],
d:[["c. 800","Swahili-speaking trading towns grow on the East African coast"],["1728","The oldest known Swahili manuscript poem is written"],["1961","Tanganyika becomes independent and promotes Swahili"],["2010","Kenya’s new constitution makes Swahili an official language"],["2022","The African Union adopts Swahili as a working language"],["2022","The first World Kiswahili Language Day is celebrated"]],
s2:[["Swahili words","Common Swahili words include jambo, hello; asante, thank you; rafiki, friend; simba, lion; safari, journey; and hakuna matata, there are no worries. Safari comes from the Arabic word for travel and entered English through East Africa."],["A language of unity","In Tanzania, Swahili is used in government, primary schools, newspapers and parliament, helping create a strong national identity across many ethnic groups. Kenya adopted Swahili as an official language alongside English in its 2010 constitution."],["Swahili and the world","UNESCO declared 7 July World Kiswahili Language Day, first celebrated in 2022, the first African language to be honored with such a UN day. Swahili is taught in universities around the world and broadcast by international radio stations."],["Literature","Swahili has a rich tradition of poetry, including long epic poems called utendi, and modern novels, plays and songs. Shaaban Robert, a Tanzanian writer of the 20th century, is celebrated as a great Swahili poet and author."],["A distant cousin of Filipino?","Swahili and Filipino are not related, but both have absorbed words from trade languages, including Arabic. Interestingly, Swahili has borrowed words from many languages of the Indian Ocean world, reflecting centuries of seafaring between Africa, Arabia, India and Southeast Asia."],["Swahili in pop culture","The 1994 Disney film The Lion King made Swahili words famous, including Simba, lion, Rafiki, friend, and hakuna matata. Swahili songs by East African musicians, such as bongo flava from Tanzania, have large audiences."]],
sp:["Nyerere’s Shakespeare","Julius Nyerere, the first president of Tanzania, was a teacher before he was a politician; Tanzanians still call him Mwalimu, meaning teacher. He believed Swahili could express any idea as well as English and that it should unite his young nation. To prove it, he translated two of William Shakespeare’s plays, Julius Caesar and The Merchant of Venice, into Swahili in the 1960s. His translations showed that Swahili could carry great world literature and inspired pride in an African language as a language of learning and government."],
g:[["Kiswahili","The Swahili name for the Swahili language"],["Bantu","A large family of African languages"],["Noun class","A grammatical group of nouns marked by prefixes"],["Lingua franca","A common language used between people with different first languages"]],
f:["Swahili is the most widely spoken African language.","Its name comes from an Arabic word for coasts.","World Kiswahili Language Day is 7 July."],
fun:"Tanzania’s first president translated Shakespeare into Swahili."});

ADDART({id:"malay",t:"Malay and Indonesian",c:"Languages",i:"🌺",w:"Malay language",r:["malaysiacountry","indonesiacountry","phlang","seaempires","baybayin"],
p:[["A language of the islands","Malay is spoken across Malaysia, Indonesia, Brunei and Singapore."],["A national language","Indonesian is a standard form of Malay used by over 270 million people."],["A relative of Filipino","Malay and Filipino are related Austronesian languages with many shared words."]],
l:"Malay is an Austronesian language spoken across maritime Southeast Asia. Its standard forms include Malaysian, the national language of Malaysia, and Indonesian, the national language of Indonesia, as well as the Malay of Brunei and Singapore. Counting Indonesian, it is used by around 300 million people, making it one of the most widely spoken languages in the world, though most Indonesians speak it as a second language alongside their regional languages. For centuries Malay was the lingua franca of trade in the region, and it is closely related to Filipino, sharing many words such as mata, eye, anak, child, and langit, sky.",
s:[["Austronesian roots","Malay belongs to the Austronesian family, which spread from Taiwan through the Philippines, Indonesia and the Pacific to Madagascar. Its closest relatives are other languages of Indonesia and Malaysia, but it also shares many words with Philippine languages."],["Old Malay","The oldest Malay inscriptions date from the 600s CE in Sumatra, during the Srivijaya Empire. Malay became the language of trade in the ports of the region, used by merchants from India, China, Arabia and Europe. Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition recorded Malay words in the 1500s, and a Malay-speaking slave named Enrique served as an interpreter."],["Scripts","Malay was written in Indic scripts, then from the 1300s in Jawi, an Arabic-based script, and later in the Latin alphabet, called Rumi in Malaysia. Jawi is still used in some religious and cultural contexts in Malaysia and Brunei."],["Indonesian","At the Youth Pledge of 1928, Indonesian nationalists declared bahasa Indonesia, based on Malay, as the language of unity for a future nation of many peoples. It became the national language at independence in 1945 and is now used by nearly all Indonesians."]],
k:[["Speakers","Around 300 million (including Indonesian)"],["Family","Austronesian"],["Official in","Malaysia, Indonesia (as Indonesian), Brunei, Singapore"],["Scripts","Latin (Rumi), Jawi"],["Shared words with Filipino","Mata, anak, langit, lima"]],
d:[["683","One of the oldest Old Malay inscriptions is carved in Sumatra"],["c. 900","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is written in a Malay-influenced language"],["1300s","Malay begins to be written in Jawi script"],["1521","Magellan’s expedition records Malay words"],["1928","The Youth Pledge declares Indonesian the language of unity"],["1945","Indonesian becomes the national language of independent Indonesia"]],
s2:[["Words shared with Filipino","Because Malay and Philippine languages are related, many basic words are similar or identical: mata, eye; anak, child; langit, sky; bulan, moon; lima, five; and ikan, fish, compared with Filipino isda. Other shared words came through trade, such as Filipino words borrowed from Malay, including some from Sanskrit and Arabic."],["The Laguna Copperplate Inscription","The oldest known written document from the Philippines, the Laguna Copperplate Inscription, dated to 900 CE, is written in a form of Old Malay mixed with Old Tagalog, Old Javanese and Sanskrit, using the Kawi script. It records the cancellation of a debt and shows the Philippines’ links with the Malay world long before the Spanish arrived."],["Malay in the Philippines","Malay was a trading language in the southern Philippines before Spanish rule, and Sulu and Maguindanao rulers corresponded in Malay written in Jawi. Today Malaysian and Indonesian are close neighbors of the Philippines, and their languages are taught in some Philippine universities."],["Malaysian and Indonesian","Malaysian and Indonesian are largely mutually understandable but differ in some vocabulary, spelling and pronunciation, partly due to British influence in Malaysia and Dutch influence in Indonesia. In 1972 the two countries agreed on a common spelling system."],["Malay words in English","English has borrowed Malay words such as amok, from amuk, gong, rattan, bamboo, orangutan, from orang hutan, meaning person of the forest, ketchup, possibly from a Chinese-Malay word, and compound."],["A simple grammar","Malay grammar is considered relatively easy to learn, with no verb conjugations for tense, no grammatical gender and plurals often formed by repeating the word, such as anak-anak for children, similar to Filipino doubling."]],
sp:["Person of the forest","The word orangutan comes from the Malay words orang, meaning person, and hutan, meaning forest: person of the forest. The great apes, found only in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra, earned their name from people who saw their humanlike faces, intelligence and gentle behavior. Interestingly, in Malay and Indonesian today, people usually call them orang utan, and the word orang alone means person, much as tao does in Filipino. Many other animal and plant names in English, such as cockatoo and durian, also come from Malay."],
g:[["Austronesian","A language family spread from Taiwan across the islands of Asia and the Pacific"],["Lingua franca","A shared language used for trade between peoples"],["Jawi","An Arabic-based script used to write Malay"],["Bahasa","The Malay word for language"]],
f:["Malay and Filipino are related Austronesian languages.","Indonesian is a standard form of Malay.","The word orangutan means person of the forest."],
fun:"The Philippines’ oldest known document, from 900 CE, is written partly in Old Malay."});

;
/* data/science7.js */
// Science articles, part 7: great discoveries (X-rays, the double helix, insulin), the mind (dreams, habits, optical illusions) and how life works (symbiosis, metamorphosis).
ADDART({id:"xrays",t:"The Discovery of X-rays",c:"History of Science",i:"🦴",w:"X-ray",r:["medimaging","light","curie","radioact","scimeth"],
p:[["Seeing inside","X-rays let doctors see bones and other structures inside the body."],["A lucky discovery","Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays by accident in 1895."],["Many uses","X-rays are used in medicine, airports, science and astronomy."]],
l:"X-rays are a form of invisible, high-energy light that can pass through soft materials such as skin and muscle but are blocked by denser materials such as bone and metal. They were discovered by accident in November 1895 by the German physicist Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen, who noticed a glowing screen near a covered tube. Within weeks, X-ray pictures of bones amazed the world, and doctors began using them to find fractures and swallowed objects. Röntgen received the first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901. X-rays transformed medicine and science, though their dangers were learned at great cost.",
s:[["The discovery","On 8 November 1895, in his laboratory in Würzburg, Röntgen was experimenting with a cathode-ray tube covered in black cardboard. He noticed that a screen coated with a fluorescent chemical, a meter or so away, began to glow. Some unknown rays were passing through the cardboard. He called them X-rays, X standing for unknown."],["The first X-ray picture","Röntgen worked in secret for weeks. On 22 December 1895, he made an image of his wife Anna Bertha’s hand, showing her bones and wedding ring. When she saw it, she is said to have exclaimed that she had seen her own death. He published his findings at the end of December."],["A worldwide sensation","News spread rapidly in early 1896. Within months, doctors around the world were using X-rays to locate bullets, find broken bones and diagnose disease. Röntgen refused to patent his discovery, wanting it to benefit everyone."],["What X-rays are","X-rays are electromagnetic waves, like visible light and radio waves, but with much shorter wavelengths and higher energy. In 1912 scientists showed that X-rays are diffracted by crystals, proving they are waves and opening a new way to study the structure of matter."]],
k:[["Discovered by","Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen"],["Date","8 November 1895"],["First X-ray image of a hand","22 December 1895"],["Nobel Prize","First in Physics, 1901"],["What they are","High-energy electromagnetic waves"]],
d:[["1895","Röntgen discovers X-rays"],["1896","Henri Becquerel, inspired by X-rays, discovers radioactivity"],["1901","Röntgen receives the first Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1912","X-rays are shown to be diffracted by crystals"],["1971","The first CT scan of a patient is made"],["1999","The Chandra X-ray Observatory is launched"]],
s2:[["Dangers discovered","Early X-ray workers did not know the rays were harmful. Many suffered burns, hair loss and cancer. Clarence Dally, an assistant of the inventor Thomas Edison, died in 1904 after years of X-ray experiments. Scientists learned to use lead shielding and limit exposure, and modern X-rays use very low doses."],["Shoe shop X-rays","From the 1920s to the 1950s, many shoe shops had X-ray machines called fluoroscopes, so customers could see their foot bones inside new shoes. Children loved them, but the machines exposed people to unnecessary radiation and were banned once the dangers were understood."],["CT scans","In the early 1970s, the British engineer Godfrey Hounsfield built the first computed tomography, or CT, scanner, which takes many X-ray images from different angles and uses a computer to create detailed cross-sections of the body. He shared the 1979 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Allan Cormack."],["Revealing molecules","X-ray crystallography, which shines X-rays through crystals to work out the arrangement of atoms, revealed the structures of salt, DNA, proteins and many medicines. Rosalind Franklin’s X-ray photographs were key to discovering the double helix of DNA."],["X-rays in daily life","Besides hospitals and dental clinics, X-rays are used in airport security to check bags, in factories to find hidden cracks in metal and by museums to look beneath the surfaces of paintings, revealing earlier sketches."],["X-ray astronomy","Hot objects in space, such as black holes and exploding stars, give off X-rays. Because Earth’s atmosphere blocks X-rays, telescopes such as NASA’s Chandra X-ray Observatory, launched in 1999, study them from orbit."]],
sp:["The hand with the ring","Just before Christmas in 1895, Wilhelm Röntgen asked his wife, Anna Bertha, to place her hand on a photographic plate in front of his mysterious tube. After about 15 minutes, the developed plate showed the ghostly bones of her fingers and the dark band of her wedding ring. It was the first X-ray photograph of a human body part. Röntgen sent copies to fellow scientists on 1 January 1896, and newspapers around the world printed it. The image made the invisible visible and became one of the most famous pictures in the history of science."],
g:[["X-ray","High-energy invisible light that can pass through soft tissue"],["Fluorescent","Glowing when struck by radiation"],["CT scan","A detailed X-ray image made with a computer"],["Crystallography","Studying the arrangement of atoms using X-rays"]],
f:["Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895.","He received the first Nobel Prize in Physics.","The X in X-ray means unknown."],
fun:"The X in X-ray stands for unknown, because Röntgen did not know what the rays were."});

ADDART({id:"doublehelix",t:"The Discovery of the Double Helix",c:"History of Science",i:"🧬",w:"Molecular structure of Nucleic Acids",r:["dna","rfranklin","genome","xrays","mendel"],
p:[["The shape of life","In 1953 scientists discovered that DNA is shaped like a twisted ladder."],["A team effort","Watson, Crick, Franklin and Wilkins all contributed to the discovery."],["A key to biology","The double helix explained how genetic information is copied."]],
l:"The double helix is the shape of DNA, the molecule that carries genetic instructions in living things. In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick, at the University of Cambridge, proposed that DNA consists of two strands twisted around each other like a spiral staircase, held together by pairs of chemical bases. Their model relied heavily on X-ray photographs and data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins at King’s College London. The discovery explained how DNA stores information and copies itself, launching modern molecular biology. Watson, Crick and Wilkins received the Nobel Prize in 1962; Franklin had died in 1958.",
s:[["Before 1953","In 1869 the Swiss chemist Friedrich Miescher discovered DNA in cell nuclei. In 1944 Oswald Avery and colleagues showed that DNA carries genetic information. Erwin Chargaff found that DNA always contains equal amounts of the bases adenine and thymine, and of guanine and cytosine. But no one knew DNA’s shape."],["Photo 51","At King’s College London, Rosalind Franklin, an expert in X-ray crystallography, and her student Raymond Gosling produced sharp X-ray images of DNA in 1952. One, called Photo 51, showed an X-shaped pattern revealing a helix. Maurice Wilkins showed it to Watson without Franklin’s knowledge."],["The model","Using Franklin’s images and data, Chargaff’s rules and cardboard and metal models, Watson and Crick built a model of DNA as a double helix, with sugar and phosphate backbones on the outside and pairs of bases, A with T and G with C, in the middle. They published it in the journal Nature on 25 April 1953, alongside papers by Wilkins’s and Franklin’s teams."],["How DNA copies itself","Watson and Crick noted that the base pairing immediately suggested how DNA could copy itself: the two strands separate, and each serves as a template for a new partner strand. This explained how genetic information passes from cell to cell and parent to child."]],
k:[["Shape","A double helix, like a twisted ladder"],["Published","25 April 1953, in Nature"],["Key scientists","Watson, Crick, Franklin, Wilkins"],["Base pairs","A with T, G with C"],["Nobel Prize","1962 (Watson, Crick, Wilkins)"]],
d:[["1869","Friedrich Miescher discovers DNA"],["1944","DNA is shown to carry genetic information"],["1952","Rosalind Franklin’s team takes Photo 51"],["1953","The double helix model is published"],["1962","Watson, Crick and Wilkins receive the Nobel Prize"],["2003","The Human Genome Project is completed"]],
s2:[["Rosalind Franklin","Rosalind Franklin’s careful X-ray work was essential to the discovery, but for many years she received little credit. She died of cancer in 1958, aged 37, and Nobel Prizes are not awarded after death. Today she is widely recognized as one of the key figures behind the double helix."],["Controversy","Watson’s 1968 memoir, The Double Helix, described the race to the discovery but portrayed Franklin unfairly, sparking criticism. Historians have since emphasized how Watson and Crick used her data without her knowledge and how she was close to solving the structure herself."],["What came next","The double helix led to discovering the genetic code, how cells make proteins and how to read and edit DNA. The Human Genome Project, completed in 2003, read the full human genetic sequence, and today DNA is used in medicine, forensics, agriculture and tracing ancestry."],["A famous announcement","According to Watson’s account, on 28 February 1953, Crick walked into the Eagle pub in Cambridge and announced that they had found the secret of life. A plaque on the pub now marks the event."],["The model’s beauty","The double helix is often called one of the most beautiful structures in science, and its spiral shape has become a symbol of biology and genetics, appearing in art, architecture and logos around the world."],["DNA in daily life","Understanding DNA has helped solve crimes, identify victims of disasters, diagnose inherited diseases, develop medicines and breed better crops. It also raises questions about privacy and the ethics of genetic testing and editing."]],
sp:["The secret of life at the Eagle pub","The Eagle, a pub near the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge, was where scientists often ate lunch. According to James Watson’s memoir, on 28 February 1953, after he and Francis Crick realized how the bases of DNA fit together, Crick strode in and told everyone within hearing that they had found the secret of life. Whether he said exactly those words is debated, but the story became legend. In 2003, on the 50th anniversary of the discovery, a blue plaque was unveiled at the pub, and visitors still come to see where the announcement was said to have been made."],
g:[["DNA","The molecule that carries genetic instructions"],["Double helix","Two strands twisted around each other"],["Base pair","Two chemical bases joined across the DNA ladder"],["X-ray crystallography","Using X-rays to find the arrangement of atoms"]],
f:["The double helix was published in 1953.","Rosalind Franklin took Photo 51.","A pairs with T and G with C."],
fun:"Crick is said to have announced the discovery of the secret of life in a pub."});

ADDART({id:"insulin",t:"The Discovery of Insulin",c:"History of Science",i:"💉",w:"History of insulin",r:["diabetes","hormone","penicil","vacc","antibiotics"],
p:[["A life-saving hormone","Insulin is a hormone that controls the amount of sugar in the blood."],["A medical miracle","Its discovery in 1921 turned a deadly disease into a manageable one."],["Sold for a dollar","Its discoverers sold the patent for just one dollar each."]],
l:"Insulin is a hormone made by the pancreas that allows the body’s cells to take in sugar from the blood for energy. Before 1922, people with type 1 diabetes, whose bodies cannot make insulin, usually died within months or a few years of diagnosis. In 1921, at the University of Toronto in Canada, Frederick Banting and Charles Best, working in the laboratory of John Macleod and later joined by the chemist James Collip, succeeded in extracting insulin. In January 1922 a 14-year-old boy, Leonard Thompson, became the first person treated with it, and his life was saved. The discovery won the Nobel Prize in 1923 and has since saved millions of lives.",
s:[["Diabetes before insulin","Diabetes had been known since ancient times; the Greek name means to pass through, referring to heavy urination, and doctors noticed the urine tasted sweet. Before insulin, the only treatment for type 1 diabetes was a near-starvation diet, which could extend life only briefly."],["The pancreas","In 1889 scientists found that removing a dog’s pancreas caused diabetes. Researchers suspected that small clusters of cells in the pancreas, the islets of Langerhans, produced a substance that controlled blood sugar, but extracting it proved very difficult."],["Toronto, 1921","Frederick Banting, a young surgeon, had an idea for isolating the substance. In the summer of 1921, with the student Charles Best, he experimented on dogs in John Macleod’s laboratory and succeeded in lowering blood sugar with extracts. James Collip then purified the extract so it could be used safely in people."],["The first patient","On 11 January 1922, Leonard Thompson, a 14-year-old boy dying of diabetes in Toronto General Hospital, received the first injection. It caused a reaction because it was impure, but a second injection of Collip’s purified insulin on 23 January dramatically lowered his blood sugar. He lived for 13 more years."]],
k:[["Made by","The pancreas"],["Discovered","1921, University of Toronto"],["Discoverers","Banting, Best, Macleod, Collip"],["First patient","Leonard Thompson (January 1922)"],["Nobel Prize","1923 (Banting and Macleod)"]],
d:[["1889","Removing the pancreas is shown to cause diabetes"],["1921","Banting and Best extract insulin in Toronto"],["1922","Leonard Thompson becomes the first patient treated with insulin"],["1923","Banting and Macleod receive the Nobel Prize"],["1955","Frederick Sanger works out the structure of insulin"],["1982","Human insulin made by genetically engineered bacteria is approved"]],
s2:[["Sharing the credit","The 1923 Nobel Prize went to Banting and Macleod. Banting was angry that Best was left out and shared his prize money with him, and Macleod shared his with Collip. The Romanian scientist Nicolae Paulescu had also extracted a pancreatic substance in 1921, and some argue his work deserved more recognition."],["A one-dollar patent","Banting, Best and Collip sold their patent rights for insulin to the University of Toronto for one dollar each, wanting it to be available to everyone. Banting reportedly said that insulin belonged to the world. Drug companies then worked with the university to mass-produce it."],["Engineered insulin","At first, insulin came from the pancreases of cows and pigs. In 1955 Frederick Sanger worked out the sequence of insulin, the first protein ever sequenced, winning a Nobel Prize. In 1978 scientists produced human insulin using genetically engineered bacteria, approved for use in 1982, one of the first products of biotechnology."],["Diabetes today","Hundreds of millions of people live with diabetes, most with type 2, which is linked to lifestyle and genes and may be treated with diet, exercise, tablets or insulin. People with type 1 diabetes need insulin every day, delivered by injections or pumps."],["Access and cost","More than a century after its discovery, insulin is still too expensive or hard to obtain for many people in low- and middle-income countries and even in some rich ones. The World Health Organization lists it as an essential medicine and campaigns for affordable access."],["World Diabetes Day","World Diabetes Day is celebrated on 14 November, the birthday of Frederick Banting, to raise awareness of diabetes and its prevention and care."]],
sp:["The ward of sleeping children","Before insulin, children with type 1 diabetes were often kept on starvation diets in hospital wards, where many fell into comas. According to a story often told about the early days of insulin, doctors went from bed to bed injecting the sleeping children with the new extract. Before they had reached the last bed, the first children were waking up from their comas, while families wept with joy. Whether every detail is exact or not, the story captures how insulin seemed like a miracle, turning a death sentence into a manageable condition."],
g:[["Insulin","A hormone that controls blood sugar"],["Pancreas","An organ that makes insulin and digestive juices"],["Diabetes","A disease in which blood sugar is too high"],["Biotechnology","Using living things, such as bacteria, to make products"]],
f:["Insulin was discovered in Toronto in 1921.","The first patient was 14-year-old Leonard Thompson.","World Diabetes Day is Banting’s birthday, 14 November."],
fun:"The discoverers of insulin sold their patent for one dollar each."});

ADDART({id:"dreams",t:"Dreams",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💭",w:"Dream",r:["sleep","brain","memory","emotions","psychology"],
p:[["Stories in sleep","Dreams are images, feelings and stories the mind experiences during sleep."],["Everyone dreams","Most people dream several times a night but forget most dreams."],["Why we dream","Scientists have several theories, but dreams are still a mystery."]],
l:"Dreams are the images, sounds, feelings and stories that people experience during sleep. Most vivid dreams happen during rapid eye movement, or REM, sleep, discovered in 1953, when the brain is very active and the eyes move quickly under closed lids. People typically have several periods of REM sleep a night and may dream for a total of about two hours, but they forget most dreams soon after waking. Scientists have proposed that dreams help process memories and emotions, rehearse responses to threats or simply result from brain activity during sleep, but why we dream is still not fully understood.",
s:[["REM sleep","In 1953 the American scientists Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman noticed that sleepers’ eyes darted around under their eyelids during certain periods, and that people woken then usually reported vivid dreams. They called this rapid eye movement sleep. During REM sleep, most muscles are temporarily paralyzed, which stops people acting out their dreams."],["What dreams are like","Dreams can be ordinary or strange, happy or frightening. They often mix recent events, worries and memories in odd ways, and the dreamer usually accepts strange events as normal. People dream in color, and many dream with all their senses."],["Why we forget","Most dreams are forgotten within minutes of waking, possibly because the brain chemistry during sleep does not favor storing new memories. Keeping a notebook by the bed and writing dreams down immediately can help people remember them."],["Nightmares","Nightmares are frightening dreams that often wake the dreamer. They are common in children and can be triggered by stress, fear, illness or frightening experiences. Talking about them, a calm bedtime routine and, when they are frequent, help from a health professional can make them less frequent."]],
k:[["Most vivid dreams","During REM sleep"],["REM sleep discovered","1953"],["Dream time per night","About two hours in total"],["Dreams remembered","Only a small fraction"],["Dreams while aware","Lucid dreams"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","Ancient Mesopotamians record dreams and their meanings"],["1899","Sigmund Freud publishes The Interpretation of Dreams"],["1953","REM sleep is discovered"],["1977","A theory links dreams to random brain activity during sleep"],["2001","Studies suggest rats replay their day during sleep"],["2010s","Brain scans help scientists study dreaming"]],
s2:[["Theories of dreaming","Sigmund Freud thought dreams expressed hidden wishes. Later scientists suggested that dreams are the brain’s attempt to make sense of random signals during sleep. Others propose that dreams help sort memories, process emotions or rehearse responses to threats. Many researchers think dreaming may serve several purposes."],["Dreams and memory","Studies show that sleep helps people remember what they have learned. In experiments, rats that learned mazes showed similar patterns of brain activity during sleep, as if replaying their route. Some people dream about tasks they practiced, and their performance improves after sleep."],["Lucid dreams","In a lucid dream, the dreamer realizes they are dreaming and may even control what happens. Lucid dreaming has been confirmed in sleep laboratories, where dreamers signaled with eye movements while still asleep."],["Inspired by dreams","Some discoveries and creations have been linked to dreams. The chemist August Kekulé said the ring shape of the benzene molecule came to him in a dream of a snake biting its tail, and Paul McCartney said the melody of the song Yesterday came to him in a dream. Such stories are hard to verify but show how dreams can spark ideas."],["Dreams across cultures","Many cultures see dreams as messages, omens or visits from ancestors. In the Philippines, people traditionally interpret certain dreams as signs, and the word bangungot refers both to nightmares and to sudden unexplained death during sleep, which doctors now link to certain heart conditions."],["Animals dream too","Many mammals and birds have REM sleep and probably dream. Dogs and cats may twitch, move their paws or make sounds while asleep, as if chasing something in a dream."]],
sp:["The snake that bit its tail","In the 1860s, the German chemist August Kekulé was trying to work out the structure of benzene, a puzzling molecule with six carbon atoms. Years later, in 1890, he told a famous story: dozing by the fire, he saw atoms dancing before his eyes, forming chains that twisted like snakes. Then one snake seized its own tail, whirling in a ring. He woke with the idea that benzene’s atoms form a ring, which turned out to be correct. Some historians doubt the story, but it remains one of the most famous examples of a dream inspiring science."],
g:[["Dream","Images and stories experienced during sleep"],["REM sleep","A stage of sleep with rapid eye movements and vivid dreams"],["Lucid dream","A dream in which you know you are dreaming"],["Nightmare","A frightening dream"]],
f:["Most vivid dreams happen during REM sleep.","REM sleep was discovered in 1953.","Most dreams are forgotten within minutes."],
fun:"A chemist said the shape of a molecule came to him in a dream of a snake biting its tail."});

ADDART({id:"habits",t:"How Habits Work",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🔁",w:"Habit",r:["brain","memory","psychology","sleep","exercise"],
p:[["Automatic actions","Habits are actions we do automatically, often without thinking."],["Cue and reward","Habits follow a pattern of cue, routine and reward."],["Changing habits","Good habits can be built and bad ones changed with practice."]],
l:"A habit is a behavior that has been repeated so often that it happens almost automatically in response to a familiar situation, such as brushing teeth before bed or reaching for a phone on waking. Habits save the brain effort, freeing attention for other things, and a large part of daily behavior is habitual. Scientists describe habits as a loop of cue, routine and reward, involving brain regions such as the basal ganglia. Understanding how habits form helps people build healthy habits, such as exercise and study routines, and change unhelpful ones.",
s:[["Why we have habits","The brain looks for ways to save energy. When an action is repeated in the same situation, the brain links the situation to the action, so it can be done with little conscious thought. This lets people perform complex tasks, like riding a bicycle or typing, automatically."],["The habit loop","Many habits follow a pattern: a cue, such as a time, place, feeling or other people, triggers a routine, the behavior itself, which leads to a reward, such as pleasure or relief. Over time, the brain begins to crave the reward as soon as it notices the cue."],["In the brain","Habits rely on the basal ganglia, a group of structures deep in the brain involved in movement and learning. As a behavior becomes a habit, activity shifts from the parts of the brain involved in decision-making to these deeper regions."],["How long it takes","A popular myth says it takes 21 days to form a habit. A 2009 study at University College London found that it took participants an average of 66 days for a new behavior to become automatic, with a range of 18 to 254 days, depending on the person and the habit."]],
k:[["Habit loop","Cue, routine, reward"],["Brain region","Basal ganglia"],["Average time to form a habit (2009 study)","About 66 days"],["Myth","Habits take 21 days"],["Early writer on habit","William James (1890)"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle writes that we become virtuous by practicing virtue"],["1890","William James writes a famous chapter on habit"],["1890s","Ivan Pavlov studies conditioned reflexes in dogs"],["1938","B. F. Skinner describes learning through rewards"],["2009","A study finds habits take about 66 days on average to form"],["2010s","Books on habits become worldwide bestsellers"]],
s2:[["Building good habits","Tips for building habits include starting very small, such as one minute of exercise; linking the new habit to an existing one, such as reading after dinner; making it easy and visible; repeating it at the same time and place; and rewarding yourself. Missing a day occasionally does not ruin progress."],["Breaking bad habits","It is often easier to replace a habit than to simply stop it. Identifying the cue and reward helps: someone who snacks when bored might take a short walk instead. Changing the environment, such as keeping a phone out of the bedroom, removes cues."],["Habits and character","Long ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle wrote that we become just by doing just acts and brave by doing brave acts, suggesting that character is built through habits. Many religious and cultural traditions emphasize daily practices."],["Habits in children","Routines, such as regular bedtimes, washing hands, brushing teeth and reading together, help children develop healthy habits that can last a lifetime. Children learn many habits by watching adults."],["Technology and habits","Apps and games are often designed to form habits, using notifications, streaks and rewards to keep people coming back. Being aware of these designs helps people decide how they want to use technology."],["Pavlov’s dogs","In the 1890s, the Russian scientist Ivan Pavlov noticed that dogs began to drool when they heard the footsteps of the assistant who fed them. He showed that animals can learn to link a signal with a reward, a process called conditioning, which helps explain how habits and associations form."]],
sp:["The myth of 21 days","Where does the popular idea that it takes 21 days to form a habit come from? It is often traced to a 1960 book, Psycho-Cybernetics, by the American plastic surgeon Maxwell Maltz, who noticed that his patients took about 21 days to get used to their new appearance. He wrote that it takes a minimum of about 21 days for old mental images to dissolve. Over time, the minimum was forgotten and 21 days became a rule. Research has since shown that habits often take much longer to form and that the time varies greatly from person to person."],
g:[["Habit","A behavior repeated until it becomes automatic"],["Cue","A trigger that starts a habit"],["Basal ganglia","Brain structures involved in movement and habits"],["Conditioning","Learning to link a signal with a response"]],
f:["Habits follow a cue, routine and reward loop.","A 2009 study found habits take about 66 days on average.","The 21-day rule is a myth."],
fun:"The idea that habits take 21 days to form came from a plastic surgeon’s observations."});

ADDART({id:"illusions",t:"Optical Illusions",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👁️",w:"Optical illusion",r:["eye","brain","light","modernart","psychology"],
p:[["Tricking the eye","Optical illusions are images that make us see something different from reality."],["The brain fills in","Illusions show that the brain interprets what the eyes see."],["Art and science","Artists and scientists use illusions to explore how vision works."]],
l:"An optical illusion is an image or scene that makes us perceive something different from what is actually there, such as lines that look different lengths but are equal, colors that look different but are the same or still pictures that seem to move. Illusions happen because seeing is not just recording light: the brain constantly interprets signals from the eyes, using shortcuts and expectations to make sense of the world quickly. Usually these shortcuts help us, but illusions reveal how they work. Scientists study illusions to understand perception, and artists use them to create surprising works.",
s:[["How seeing works","Light enters the eyes and forms images on the retina, which sends signals to the brain. The brain must work out sizes, distances, colors and movement from this information, using context, past experience and assumptions, such as that light usually comes from above."],["Length and size illusions","In the Müller-Lyer illusion, created in 1889, two lines of equal length look different because of arrow-like fins at their ends. In the Ponzo illusion, two equal lines look different sizes when placed between converging lines, like railway tracks, because the brain interprets them as being at different distances."],["Color and brightness illusions","In the checker shadow illusion, created by Edward Adelson in 1995, two squares that are exactly the same shade of gray look very different because one appears to be in shadow. The brain adjusts for the shadow, as it does in real life."],["Ambiguous images","Some images can be seen in two ways. The duck-rabbit drawing, published in 1892, can look like a duck or a rabbit, and the Necker cube, described in 1832, seems to flip between two orientations. The brain switches between interpretations but cannot see both at once."]],
k:[["Cause","The brain’s interpretation of visual signals"],["Müller-Lyer illusion","1889"],["Duck-rabbit drawing","1892"],["Checker shadow illusion","1995"],["Famous viral illusion","The dress (2015)"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle notes that visual effects can deceive the senses"],["1832","The Necker cube is described"],["1889","The Müller-Lyer illusion is created"],["1892","The duck-rabbit drawing is published"],["1995","The checker shadow illusion is created"],["2015","A photo of a dress divides viewers over its colors"]],
s2:[["The dress","In February 2015, a photo of a striped dress went viral as people argued whether it was blue and black or white and gold. The dress was actually blue and black. Scientists explained that viewers’ brains made different assumptions about the lighting, some assuming bluish shade and others warm light, leading them to see different colors."],["Motion illusions","Some still images seem to move. The rotating snakes illusion, created by the Japanese psychologist Akiyoshi Kitaoka in 2003, shows circles that appear to turn as the eyes move across them. Films and animation themselves rely on an illusion: a series of still pictures shown quickly looks like smooth motion."],["The moon illusion","The Moon often looks much larger near the horizon than high in the sky, though its size in the sky is almost the same. The cause is still debated, but most explanations involve how the brain judges distance and size using the landscape."],["Impossible objects","Some drawings show objects that could not exist, such as the Penrose triangle, a triangle that seems to twist impossibly. The Dutch artist M. C. Escher used such ideas in famous prints of endless staircases and waterfalls that flow uphill."],["Illusions in art and design","Artists have used illusions for centuries, from painted ceilings that seem to open to the sky to op art, which uses patterns to create movement and vibration. Designers use illusions to make rooms look larger or roads safer, such as painted lines that make drivers slow down."],["Not just the eyes","Illusions also affect other senses. In the McGurk effect, what we see a person’s lips doing changes the sound we think we hear. Such illusions show that the brain combines information from different senses."]],
sp:["The dress that divided the internet","On 26 February 2015, a Scottish woman posted a photograph of a dress that her daughter’s future mother-in-law planned to wear to a wedding. Friends could not agree on its colors. Within hours, millions of people worldwide were arguing: blue and black, or white and gold? Celebrities joined in, and news programs covered the debate. The dress was in fact blue and black. Scientists studied why people saw it so differently, and their research showed how much the brain’s guesses about lighting shape the colors we see."],
g:[["Optical illusion","An image that tricks visual perception"],["Perception","The brain’s interpretation of signals from the senses"],["Retina","The light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye"],["Ambiguous image","A picture that can be seen in two ways"]],
f:["Illusions show that the brain interprets what we see.","The duck-rabbit drawing was published in 1892.","The viral dress of 2015 was blue and black."],
fun:"Films work because of an illusion: still pictures shown quickly look like motion."});

ADDART({id:"symbiosis",t:"Symbiosis",c:"Science",i:"🐠",w:"Symbiosis",r:["eco","microbiome","coral","fungi","evo","clownfish"],
p:[["Living together","Symbiosis is a close, long-term relationship between different species."],["Helping each other","In mutualism, both partners benefit."],["Inside our cells","Even parts of our cells began as partnerships with bacteria."]],
l:"Symbiosis is a close and long-lasting relationship between two different species. The word comes from Greek, meaning living together. In mutualism, both partners benefit, as when bees pollinate flowers while collecting nectar, or when tiny algae live inside corals and share food. In commensalism, one partner benefits and the other is unaffected, and in parasitism, one benefits at the other’s expense. Symbiosis is everywhere in nature, from lichens and the fungi on plant roots to the trillions of microbes in the human gut, and it has shaped the evolution of life, including the origins of complex cells.",
s:[["Mutualism","In mutualism, both species gain. Clownfish live among the stinging tentacles of sea anemones, protected from predators, while cleaning the anemone and scaring off fish that eat it. Flowering plants provide nectar to bees, birds and bats, which carry pollen from flower to flower."],["Commensalism","In commensalism, one species benefits while the other is neither helped nor harmed. Remora fish attach to sharks and whales, getting a free ride and scraps of food. Some birds nest in trees without affecting them."],["Parasitism","Parasites, such as tapeworms, ticks, lice and the organisms that cause malaria, live on or in a host and take nutrients, often harming it. Parasites are extremely common, and many have complex life cycles involving several hosts."],["Lichens","A lichen is a partnership between a fungus and algae or bacteria that can photosynthesize. The fungus provides shelter and absorbs water and minerals, while its partner makes food from sunlight. Together they can live on bare rocks, tree bark and in harsh places such as deserts and the Arctic."]],
k:[["Word means","Living together (Greek)"],["Types","Mutualism, commensalism, parasitism"],["Coined in biology","1870s"],["Famous mutualists","Clownfish and sea anemones"],["Endosymbiotic theory","Lynn Margulis (1967)"]],
d:[["1877","Albert Frank uses the word symbiosis for lichens"],["1879","Anton de Bary defines symbiosis broadly"],["1885","Fungi that partner with plant roots are named mycorrhizae"],["1967","Lynn Margulis proposes that mitochondria came from bacteria"],["2007","Studies begin mapping the human microbiome in detail"],["2016","A study confirms birds and humans cooperate to find honey"]],
s2:[["Coral and algae","Reef-building corals contain tiny algae called zooxanthellae in their tissues. The algae photosynthesize and give the coral most of its food, while the coral provides shelter and nutrients. When water gets too hot, corals expel the algae and turn white, called coral bleaching, which can kill them."],["Fungi and roots","Most land plants form partnerships with fungi on their roots, called mycorrhizae. The fungi’s thread-like networks absorb water and minerals from the soil and pass them to the plant, receiving sugars in return. These networks link plants underground."],["Cells that were once bacteria","In 1967 the American biologist Lynn Margulis proposed that mitochondria, the energy factories of our cells, and chloroplasts in plants were once free-living bacteria that came to live inside other cells. Her theory, at first rejected, is now widely accepted, showing that symbiosis helped create complex life."],["Our microbes","The human body hosts trillions of microbes, especially in the gut, where bacteria help digest food, make vitamins and train the immune system. In return, they get a warm home and a supply of food."],["Ants and fungi","Leafcutter ants in the Americas cut leaves not to eat them but to feed a fungus that they farm in underground gardens. The ants eat the fungus and protect it from pests, a farming partnership that has lasted millions of years."],["Symbiosis in the Philippines","Philippine coral reefs, such as Tubbataha, depend on coral and algae partnerships, and clownfish and anemones are favorite sights for divers. In Philippine forests, figs and the tiny wasps that pollinate them depend entirely on each other."]],
sp:["The bird that leads people to honey","In parts of Africa, a small bird called the greater honeyguide has a remarkable partnership with people. The bird guides honey hunters to wild bees’ nests hidden in trees, flying ahead and calling. The hunters open the nest and take the honey, and the bird feeds on the leftover wax and larvae. In 2016 scientists working with Yao honey hunters in Mozambique showed that the birds respond to a special call, a trill followed by a grunt, used by the hunters to recruit them. It is one of the few known cases of wild animals and humans cooperating as partners."],
g:[["Symbiosis","A close, long-term relationship between species"],["Mutualism","A relationship in which both partners benefit"],["Parasite","An organism that lives on or in a host and harms it"],["Lichen","A partnership between a fungus and algae or bacteria"]],
f:["Symbiosis means living together.","Mitochondria were once free-living bacteria.","Most land plants partner with fungi on their roots."],
fun:"In Africa, a wild bird leads people to honey and shares the reward."});

ADDART({id:"metamorph",t:"Metamorphosis",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦋",w:"Metamorphosis",r:["butterfly","frog","insect","beetle","dragonfly"],
p:[["Changing shape","Metamorphosis is a dramatic change in body form as an animal grows."],["Caterpillar to butterfly","Butterflies transform completely inside a chrysalis."],["Tadpole to frog","Frogs change from swimming tadpoles into air-breathing adults."]],
l:"Metamorphosis is a dramatic change in body shape and structure that some animals undergo as they develop from young to adult. Butterflies, moths, beetles, flies and bees go through complete metamorphosis, with four stages: egg, larva, pupa and adult. Grasshoppers, dragonflies and cicadas go through incomplete metamorphosis, in which young nymphs gradually come to resemble adults. Frogs and other amphibians also undergo metamorphosis, changing from water-dwelling tadpoles into adults. Metamorphosis allows young and adults to live in different ways and eat different foods, reducing competition between them.",
s:[["Complete metamorphosis","In complete metamorphosis, an egg hatches into a larva, such as a caterpillar, grub or maggot, which eats and grows. The larva then becomes a pupa, often inside a cocoon or chrysalis, where its body is rebuilt. Finally, an adult emerges, usually with wings. Most insect species develop this way."],["Inside the chrysalis","Inside a chrysalis, much of the caterpillar’s body breaks down into a kind of cellular soup. Groups of cells called imaginal discs, present since the caterpillar hatched, then grow into the wings, legs, eyes and other adult parts. Remarkably, some studies suggest that moths can remember things learned as caterpillars."],["Incomplete metamorphosis","In incomplete metamorphosis, young insects called nymphs look like small, wingless versions of adults. They grow by molting, shedding their outer skin several times, and develop wings at the final molt. Dragonfly nymphs live underwater for months or years before climbing out to become flying adults."],["Frogs and toads","A frog’s egg hatches into a tadpole, which lives in water, breathes with gills and has a tail. Over weeks, it grows legs, its tail shrinks, its gills are replaced by lungs and its mouth and gut change for eating insects. This transformation is controlled by thyroid hormone."]],
k:[["Complete stages","Egg, larva, pupa, adult"],["Incomplete stages","Egg, nymph, adult"],["Examples of complete","Butterflies, beetles, flies, bees"],["Examples of incomplete","Grasshoppers, dragonflies, cicadas"],["Controls frog metamorphosis","Thyroid hormone"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle describes insect development"],["1669","Jan Swammerdam shows that the adult butterfly forms inside the pupa"],["1705","Maria Sibylla Merian publishes her paintings of insect metamorphosis"],["1912","Thyroid hormone is shown to trigger frog metamorphosis"],["2008","A study suggests moths remember what they learned as caterpillars"],["2020s","Scientists map the genes that control metamorphosis"]],
s2:[["Why metamorphosis?","Having different young and adult forms lets each stage specialize: larvae are eating machines that grow, while adults focus on reproduction and spreading to new places. Because caterpillars eat leaves and butterflies drink nectar, they do not compete for food."],["Maria Sibylla Merian","In the 1600s, many Europeans believed insects arose spontaneously from mud or rotting matter. The German-born artist and naturalist Maria Sibylla Merian raised caterpillars and carefully painted every stage of their lives. In 1699 she traveled to Suriname in South America, and her 1705 book of insect metamorphosis there became a scientific and artistic masterpiece."],["Other animals","Many marine animals, such as sea stars, crabs and corals, have tiny larvae that drift in the ocean before transforming into adults. Flatfish, such as flounders, start life swimming upright, and then one eye moves across the head so they can lie flat on the seabed."],["The axolotl","Some salamanders, such as the axolotl of Mexico, can stay in their larval form throughout life, keeping their gills and living in water while becoming able to reproduce. This is called neoteny."],["Butterflies of the Philippines","The Philippines is home to hundreds of butterfly species, many found nowhere else, and butterfly gardens let visitors watch caterpillars, chrysalises and adults. Children often learn about metamorphosis by raising caterpillars at school."],["A symbol of change","Because of its dramatic transformation, the butterfly has become a symbol of change, growth and rebirth in many cultures, appearing in art, poetry and stories around the world."]],
sp:["Remembering life as a caterpillar","Can a butterfly or moth remember its life as a caterpillar, even though its body and brain are reorganized inside the pupa? In a 2008 experiment, scientists at Georgetown University in the United States trained tobacco hornworm caterpillars to avoid a particular smell by pairing it with a mild shock. After the caterpillars turned into adult moths, many of them still avoided the smell. The results suggested that some memories can survive the dramatic transformation of metamorphosis, a surprising discovery about how nervous systems change."],
g:[["Metamorphosis","A dramatic change in form during development"],["Larva","The young, wingless feeding stage of an insect"],["Pupa","The resting stage in which an insect transforms"],["Nymph","A young insect that resembles the adult"]],
f:["Complete metamorphosis has four stages.","Tadpoles change into frogs.","Some moths may remember their caterpillar days."],
fun:"Some moths seem to remember things they learned as caterpillars."});

