ADDART({id:"uaecountry",t:"United Arab Emirates",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏙️",w:"United Arab Emirates",r:["saudicountry","middleeast","islam","desert","ofw","skyscrapers"],
p:[["Seven emirates","The United Arab Emirates is a federation of seven emirates on the Arabian Gulf."],["From pearls to oil","Once a land of pearl divers and traders, it grew rich from oil."],["A global hub","Dubai and Abu Dhabi are centers of trade, travel and business."]],
l:"The United Arab Emirates, or UAE, is a country on the eastern Arabian Peninsula, a federation of seven emirates: Abu Dhabi, the capital, Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, Umm al-Quwain, Ras al-Khaimah and Fujairah. About 11 million people live there, the great majority of them foreign residents from South Asia, the Philippines and elsewhere. Founded in 1971, the UAE transformed within decades from a land of desert towns, pearl divers and traders into a wealthy hub of trade, aviation, finance and tourism, built on oil and gas. It is home to the world’s tallest building, the Burj Khalifa. Its rapid development is admired, while its treatment of migrant workers and limits on political freedoms draw criticism.",
s:[["Land","Most of the UAE is desert, with sand dunes, salt flats and oases such as Al Ain and Liwa. The Hajar Mountains rise in the east, near the border with Oman. The long coast on the Arabian Gulf has islands, mangroves and shallow seas, and Fujairah faces the Gulf of Oman."],["Before oil","For centuries, people on this coast lived by fishing, herding, date farming, trading and diving for pearls. From 1820 the coastal sheikhdoms signed treaties with Britain and became known as the Trucial States. The pearl industry collapsed in the 1930s when cultured pearls from Japan appeared."],["Oil and independence","Oil was discovered off Abu Dhabi in 1958, and exports began in the 1960s. When Britain withdrew from the Gulf, six emirates formed the United Arab Emirates on 2 December 1971, and Ras al-Khaimah joined in 1972. Sheikh Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan of Abu Dhabi became its first president."],["Government","The UAE is a federation of monarchies. Each emirate is ruled by its own hereditary ruler, and the seven rulers form the Federal Supreme Council, which chooses the president and vice president. There are no political parties, and an advisory Federal National Council is partly elected."]],
k:[["Capital","Abu Dhabi"],["Population","About 11 million (2024)"],["Area","About 83,600 km²"],["Highest point","Jebel Jais, about 1,900 m"],["Currency","UAE dirham"]],
d:[["1820","Coastal sheikhdoms sign a treaty with Britain"],["1958","Oil is discovered off Abu Dhabi"],["1971","The United Arab Emirates is founded"],["1972","Ras al-Khaimah joins the federation"],["2010","The Burj Khalifa opens in Dubai"],["2021","The Hope probe reaches orbit around Mars"],["2023","Dubai hosts the COP28 climate conference"]],
s2:[["Dubai","Dubai grew from a small trading port into a global city of skyscrapers, artificial islands, shopping malls and one of the world’s busiest international airports. The Burj Khalifa, opened in 2010, is about 828 meters tall, the tallest building in the world. Dubai built its economy on trade, tourism, real estate and finance rather than oil alone."],["Filipinos in the UAE","Hundreds of thousands of Filipinos live and work in the UAE, one of the largest Filipino communities abroad, as nurses, engineers, hotel and retail workers, domestic workers and professionals. Filipino churches, shops and restaurants are part of daily life in Dubai and Abu Dhabi, and remittances from the UAE support many families in the Philippines."],["Migrant workers","Foreign workers make up most of the UAE’s population and workforce. Many have found good opportunities, but human rights groups have criticized long working hours, low wages, debts owed to recruiters and sponsorship rules that tie workers to employers, especially for construction and domestic workers. The government has introduced labor reforms in recent years."],["Space and science","The UAE has invested in space and science. In 2021 its Hope probe became the first Arab mission to reach Mars, studying the planet’s atmosphere. It has also sent astronauts to the International Space Station and built the Arab world’s first nuclear power plant, Barakah."],["Culture","Emirati culture is rooted in Islam, Bedouin traditions, falconry, camel racing, poetry and hospitality, such as serving Arabic coffee and dates to guests. Abu Dhabi’s Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque and the Louvre Abu Dhabi museum attract visitors from around the world."],["Debates","Supporters praise the UAE’s stability, safety, religious tolerance and economic success. Critics point to restrictions on free speech and political activity, the treatment of migrant workers and its role in regional conflicts. Views differ, and the government emphasizes its development and diplomacy."]],
sp:["The world’s tallest building","When construction of the Burj Khalifa began in Dubai in 2004, it was designed to rise higher than any building before it. Its Y-shaped tower, inspired partly by a desert flower, steps back as it rises to reduce wind forces. Thousands of workers, many from South Asia and the Philippines, built it over six years. When it opened on 4 January 2010, it stood about 828 meters tall, with more than 160 floors, and its spire can be seen from almost 100 kilometers away. From its observation decks, visitors watch the sun set twice: once at the base and again higher up, as the sun dips below the horizon later at greater heights."],
g:[["Emirate","A territory ruled by an emir or sheikh"],["Federation","A union of states under a central government"],["Trucial States","The name for the coastal sheikhdoms under British treaties"],["Expatriate","A person living outside their home country"],["Falconry","Hunting with trained falcons"]],
f:["The UAE has seven emirates.","It was founded on 2 December 1971.","The Burj Khalifa is the world’s tallest building."],
fun:"From the Burj Khalifa, people can watch the sun set twice on the same evening."});

ADDART({id:"iraqcountry",t:"Iraq",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏺",w:"Iraq",r:["mesop","persia","caliphate","middleeast","terrorism","irancountry"],
p:[["Cradle of civilization","Iraq lies in Mesopotamia, where the world’s first cities and writing appeared."],["Two great rivers","The Tigris and Euphrates rivers flow through Iraq."],["A troubled modern history","Iraq has suffered wars, dictatorship and conflict but is rebuilding."]],
l:"Iraq is a country in western Asia, with about 46 million people. Its capital is Baghdad. Iraq lies in Mesopotamia, the land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, where some of the world’s first cities, writing systems and laws arose in ancient Sumer, Akkad, Babylon and Assyria. Baghdad later became the glittering capital of the Abbasid Caliphate and a world center of learning. In modern times, Iraq has one of the world’s largest oil reserves but has suffered dictatorship, wars, sanctions, foreign invasion and the rise and defeat of the Islamic State group. Its people include Arabs, Kurds, Turkmen, Assyrians, Yazidis and others.",
s:[["Land","Most of Iraq is flat, with fertile river plains in the center and south, deserts in the west and mountains in the Kurdish north. The Tigris and Euphrates join in the south to form the Shatt al-Arab, flowing to the Persian Gulf through the famous marshes."],["Ancient Mesopotamia","Around 3500 to 3000 BCE, the Sumerians built cities such as Uruk and Ur and invented cuneiform writing. Later, Akkad, Babylon and Assyria built empires. King Hammurabi of Babylon issued one of the earliest written law codes, around 1754 BCE."],["Baghdad and the golden age","Founded in 762 CE by the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur, Baghdad became one of the largest and richest cities in the world. Its House of Wisdom gathered scholars who translated and advanced knowledge in mathematics, astronomy, medicine and philosophy. In 1258 the Mongols sacked Baghdad, ending its golden age."],["The modern state","After centuries of Ottoman rule, Britain took control after World War I and created the Kingdom of Iraq, which became independent in 1932. A revolution in 1958 ended the monarchy. The Ba’ath Party took power in 1968, and Saddam Hussein ruled as president from 1979 to 2003."]],
k:[["Capital","Baghdad"],["Population","About 46 million (2024)"],["Area","About 438,000 km²"],["Highest point","Cheekha Dar, 3,611 m"],["Currency","Iraqi dinar"]],
d:[["c. 1754 BCE","Hammurabi’s law code is issued in Babylon"],["762","Baghdad is founded"],["1258","The Mongols sack Baghdad"],["1932","Iraq becomes independent"],["1979","Saddam Hussein becomes president"],["2003","A US-led invasion topples Saddam Hussein"],["2017","Iraqi and allied forces defeat the Islamic State group’s territorial control"]],
s2:[["Wars and dictatorship","Under Saddam Hussein, Iraq fought a long war with Iran from 1980 to 1988 that killed hundreds of thousands. In 1988 his forces attacked the Kurdish town of Halabja with chemical weapons. In 1990 Iraq invaded Kuwait and was driven out by a US-led coalition in 1991, followed by years of sanctions."],["The 2003 invasion","In 2003 a US-led coalition invaded Iraq, citing claims of weapons of mass destruction, which were never found, and toppled Saddam Hussein, who was later tried and executed. The war and the sectarian violence and insurgency that followed killed hundreds of thousands of Iraqis. Debates continue about the war’s justification and consequences."],["The Islamic State","In 2014 the Islamic State group seized large parts of northern and western Iraq, including Mosul, and committed atrocities, including genocide against the Yazidi people. Iraqi forces, Kurdish Peshmerga, militias and an international coalition drove it from its territory by 2017, though the group still carries out attacks."],["The Kurdistan Region","Kurds in northern Iraq have their own autonomous regional government, with its capital at Erbil, its own parliament and security forces. It has been more stable than much of Iraq, though relations with Baghdad over oil and territory remain tense."],["Heritage","Iraq’s ancient sites, such as Babylon, Ur, Hatra and Samarra, are among the most important in the world, though many suffered looting and destruction during wars. The Mesopotamian Marshes in the south, home to the Marsh Arabs and a unique way of life, were drained under Saddam Hussein and partly restored after 2003."],["Rebuilding","Iraq holds regular elections under its 2005 constitution and has used its oil wealth to rebuild, but it faces challenges such as corruption, unemployment, water shortages from droughts and upstream dams, and tensions between communities. Young Iraqis have led protests calling for better services and jobs."]],
sp:["The House of Wisdom","In the 800s, the Abbasid caliphs of Baghdad supported a great center of learning known as the House of Wisdom. Scholars of many faiths and backgrounds, Muslims, Christians, Jews and others, gathered there to translate works from Greek, Persian, Sanskrit and Syriac into Arabic and to make new discoveries. The mathematician al-Khwarizmi worked in Baghdad, writing books that gave us the words algebra and algorithm. Paper, newly introduced from China, made books cheaper. When the Mongols sacked Baghdad in 1258, legend says the Tigris ran black with the ink of books thrown into the river."],
g:[["Mesopotamia","The land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers"],["Cuneiform","The wedge-shaped writing of ancient Mesopotamia"],["Caliphate","An Islamic state led by a caliph"],["Sectarian","Related to conflict between religious groups"],["Autonomous region","An area with its own government within a country"]],
f:["Writing was invented in ancient Mesopotamia.","Baghdad was founded in 762.","The Kurdistan Region has its own government."],
fun:"The words algebra and algorithm come from a scholar who worked in Baghdad."});

ADDART({id:"jordancountry",t:"Jordan",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏜️",w:"Jordan",r:["middleeast","israelcountry","saudicountry","iraqcountry","islam","rome"],
p:[["The rose-red city","Jordan is home to Petra, an ancient city carved into rock."],["The lowest place on land","The shores of the Dead Sea are the lowest land on Earth."],["A welcoming country","Jordan has taken in millions of refugees from neighboring countries."]],
l:"Jordan, officially the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan, is a country in the Middle East, with about 11.5 million people. Its capital is Amman. Jordan is famous for Petra, the rock-cut city of the ancient Nabataeans, the desert landscapes of Wadi Rum and the Dead Sea, whose shores are the lowest land on Earth. Independent since 1946, Jordan is a constitutional monarchy ruled by the Hashemite dynasty. Though it has few natural resources and very little water, it has remained relatively stable in a turbulent region and has hosted millions of refugees from Palestine, Iraq and Syria.",
s:[["Land","Jordan has the Jordan Valley and the Dead Sea in the west, highlands with most of the cities, and deserts covering most of the east. Its only seaport is Aqaba, on the Red Sea. Jordan is one of the most water-scarce countries in the world."],["Ancient peoples","The region was home to ancient kingdoms such as Ammon, Moab and Edom. The Nabataeans, Arab traders, built Petra as their capital, growing rich from caravan routes. The Romans took control in 106 CE, leaving cities such as Jerash, one of the best-preserved Roman cities outside Italy."],["Islamic and Ottoman rule","Arab Muslim armies arrived in the 600s, and the region was later ruled by various dynasties and, from the 1500s, by the Ottoman Empire. During World War I, the Arab Revolt led by Sharif Hussein of Mecca, with British support, fought the Ottomans."],["The kingdom","After the war, Britain created the Emirate of Transjordan in 1921 under Abdullah, son of Sharif Hussein. It became independent on 25 May 1946 as the Hashemite Kingdom. King Hussein ruled from 1952 to 1999, and his son, King Abdullah II, succeeded him."]],
k:[["Capital","Amman"],["Population","About 11.5 million (2024)"],["Area","About 89,300 km²"],["Highest point","Jabal Umm al-Dami, 1,854 m"],["Currency","Jordanian dinar"]],
d:[["c. 300 BCE","The Nabataeans build Petra"],["106 CE","The Romans annex the Nabataean kingdom"],["1921","The Emirate of Transjordan is created"],["1946","Jordan becomes independent"],["1994","Jordan signs a peace treaty with Israel"],["1999","King Abdullah II succeeds King Hussein"],["2007","Petra is voted one of the New Seven Wonders of the World"]],
s2:[["Petra","Petra, carved into rose-red sandstone cliffs, was the Nabataean capital from around the 300s BCE. Visitors walk through a narrow gorge called the Siq to reach the Treasury, a temple-like facade about 40 meters tall. Petra has tombs, theaters and water channels. Forgotten by the outside world for centuries, it was revealed to Europeans in 1812 and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["The Dead Sea","The Dead Sea, shared with Israel and the West Bank, is so salty that people float easily on its surface, and almost nothing can live in it. Its shore lies more than 400 meters below sea level. The sea is shrinking as water from the Jordan River is diverted, and sinkholes are appearing along its shores."],["Refugees","Jordan has taken in waves of refugees: Palestinians after the wars of 1948 and 1967, many of whom became citizens, Iraqis after 2003 and Syrians after 2011, with hundreds of thousands living in Jordan, some in camps such as Zaatari. Hosting refugees has strained water, schools and jobs, and international aid helps."],["Politics","Jordan is a constitutional monarchy with an elected parliament, but the king holds broad powers. It has made peace with Israel, signing a treaty in 1994, while supporting Palestinian rights, and its kings act as custodians of Muslim and Christian holy sites in Jerusalem. Supporters praise its stability; critics call for more political freedom."],["Wadi Rum","Wadi Rum is a desert valley of red sand, towering sandstone mountains and ancient rock carvings, where Bedouin communities live. Its Mars-like landscape has been used to film movies set on other planets, and visitors camp there under the stars."],["Food and hospitality","Jordanian hospitality is famous, with guests welcomed with Arabic coffee. The national dish, mansaf, is lamb cooked in a sauce of fermented dried yogurt, served over rice and flatbread and traditionally eaten from a shared platter."]],
sp:["The rediscovery of Petra","For centuries, Petra was known only to the local Bedouin. In 1812 a young Swiss explorer, Johann Ludwig Burckhardt, traveling disguised as an Arab scholar named Sheikh Ibrahim, heard rumors of a lost city in the mountains. Pretending he wanted to sacrifice a goat at the nearby tomb of the prophet Aaron, he persuaded a guide to lead him through the narrow Siq. He glimpsed the Treasury and other monuments but dared not stay long or take notes openly, fearing suspicion. His account introduced Petra to the Western world."],
g:[["Nabataeans","Ancient Arab traders who built Petra"],["Hashemite","The royal family of Jordan, descended from Sharif Hussein"],["Siq","The narrow gorge leading into Petra"],["Constitutional monarchy","A monarchy limited by a constitution"],["Mansaf","Jordan’s national dish of lamb, yogurt sauce and rice"]],
f:["Petra was carved into sandstone cliffs.","The Dead Sea shore is the lowest land on Earth.","Jordan became independent in 1946."],
fun:"People float easily in the Dead Sea because it is so salty."});

ADDART({id:"rwandacountry",t:"Rwanda",c:"Countries and Places",i:"⛰️",w:"Rwanda",r:["ugandacountry","drccountry","tanzaniacountry","gorilla","hrights","africacolon"],
p:[["Land of a thousand hills","Rwanda is a small, green country of hills and mountains in central Africa."],["Genocide and recovery","In 1994 about 800,000 people were killed in a genocide; Rwanda has since rebuilt."],["Women leaders","Rwanda has the highest share of women in parliament in the world."]],
l:"Rwanda is a small, landlocked country in east-central Africa, with about 14 million people, making it one of the most densely populated countries on the continent. Its capital is Kigali. Known as the land of a thousand hills, Rwanda is home to mountain gorillas, volcanoes and lakes. In 1994 Rwanda suffered one of the worst atrocities of the 20th century, the genocide against the Tutsi, in which about 800,000 people, mostly Tutsi along with moderate Hutu, were killed in about 100 days. Since then the country has rebuilt, achieving strong economic growth and health improvements, while its government faces criticism over political freedoms.",
s:[["Land","Rwanda lies on high plateaus and hills, with volcanoes of the Virunga range in the northwest, Lake Kivu on the western border and the forests of Nyungwe in the southwest. Its mild climate and fertile soils support farming, especially of bananas, beans, coffee and tea."],["The kingdom","For centuries, Rwanda was a kingdom ruled by a king called the mwami. Its people, Hutu, Tutsi and Twa, shared the Kinyarwanda language and culture. Social differences existed but were fluid before colonial rule."],["Colonial rule","Germany claimed Rwanda in the 1890s, and Belgium took over after World War I. The Belgians favored the Tutsi minority and introduced identity cards stating each person’s ethnic group, hardening divisions. Before and after independence on 1 July 1962, violence against Tutsi drove many into exile."],["The genocide","On 6 April 1994, the plane carrying President Juvénal Habyarimana was shot down. Extremists then launched a planned campaign of mass killing. Over about 100 days, militias, soldiers and ordinary people, urged on by radio propaganda, killed some 800,000 people. The world largely failed to intervene. The Rwandan Patriotic Front, led by Paul Kagame, ended the genocide in July 1994."]],
k:[["Capital","Kigali"],["Population","About 14 million (2024)"],["Area","About 26,300 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Karisimbi, 4,507 m"],["Currency","Rwandan franc"]],
d:[["1890s","Germany claims Rwanda"],["1916","Belgium takes control"],["1962","Rwanda becomes independent"],["1994","The genocide against the Tutsi"],["2002","Community gacaca courts begin hearing genocide cases"],["2008","Rwanda becomes the first country with a majority of women in parliament"],["2008","Rwanda bans plastic bags"]],
s2:[["Justice and reconciliation","After the genocide, Rwanda faced the task of bringing justice for hundreds of thousands of suspects. An international tribunal tried leaders, while from 2002 to 2012 community courts called gacaca heard nearly two million cases, combining justice with confession and reconciliation. Memorials, such as the Kigali Genocide Memorial, preserve the memory of the victims."],["Recovery","Since 1994, Rwanda has achieved rapid economic growth, reduced poverty and child deaths, expanded health insurance to most citizens and become known for cleanliness and order. Kigali is one of Africa’s cleanest cities, and on the last Saturday of each month citizens join umuganda, community work such as cleaning and building."],["Women in power","Rwanda’s 2003 constitution reserves at least 30 percent of decision-making positions for women, and in 2008 it became the first country where women held a majority of seats in parliament. Today women hold more than three-fifths of the seats in its lower house."],["Politics","Paul Kagame has led Rwanda since 2000, after serving as vice president. Supporters credit his government with stability, reconciliation, development and fighting corruption. Critics, including human rights groups, point to restrictions on opposition parties and the press, the treatment of critics and accusations by UN experts of support for rebel groups in the neighboring Democratic Republic of the Congo, which Rwanda denies."],["Gorillas","Volcanoes National Park in the Virunga Mountains is home to mountain gorillas. Each year, Rwanda holds Kwita Izina, a ceremony to name baby gorillas, based on a traditional naming ritual for children. Gorilla tourism provides income for conservation and local communities."],["A ban on plastic bags","In 2008 Rwanda became one of the first countries to ban plastic bags, with checks at borders and airports. The ban helped keep the country clean and inspired other African countries to follow."]],
sp:["Umuganda","On the last Saturday morning of every month, life in Rwanda pauses for umuganda, a word that means coming together in common purpose. Businesses close, traffic stops and citizens aged 18 to 65, including the president, join their neighbors in community work: sweeping streets, planting trees, building homes for poor families, repairing roads or clearing drains. Afterwards, communities hold meetings to discuss local issues. The practice has roots in Rwandan tradition and was revived after the genocide as a way of rebuilding the country and bringing people together."],
g:[["Genocide","The deliberate killing of a people because of their identity"],["Gacaca","Community courts that tried genocide cases in Rwanda"],["Umuganda","Monthly community work in Rwanda"],["Mwami","The traditional king of Rwanda"],["Reconciliation","Restoring friendly relations after conflict"]],
f:["Rwanda is called the land of a thousand hills.","About 800,000 people were killed in the 1994 genocide.","Women hold a majority of seats in Rwanda’s parliament."],
fun:"Rwanda names its baby gorillas in a yearly national ceremony."});

ADDART({id:"mozambiquecountry",t:"Mozambique",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏝️",w:"Mozambique",r:["africacolon","portugalcountry","southafricacountry","zimbabwecountry","tanzaniacountry","angolacountry"],
p:[["A long coastline","Mozambique has one of the longest coastlines in Africa, on the Indian Ocean."],["Portuguese heritage","It was a Portuguese colony for centuries, and Portuguese is its official language."],["Wildlife returns","Gorongosa National Park has been restored after a long civil war."]],
l:"Mozambique is a country in southeastern Africa, with a long coastline on the Indian Ocean and about 34 million people. Its capital is Maputo. Long a center of Indian Ocean trade between Africa, Arabia and India, it was colonized by Portugal and became independent in 1975 after a war of liberation. A devastating civil war followed until 1992. Since then Mozambique has rebuilt, with beaches, islands, coral reefs and national parks drawing visitors, and large gas reserves offering hope for growth, but it remains one of the world’s poorest countries and has faced cyclones and an insurgency in the north.",
s:[["Land","Mozambique stretches about 2,500 kilometers along the coast, with sandy beaches, mangroves, coral reefs and islands such as the Bazaruto Archipelago. The Zambezi River crosses the country, and highlands rise in the west and north."],["Trade and Portuguese rule","Swahili-speaking traders and Arab merchants traded gold, ivory and other goods along the coast for centuries. Vasco da Gama reached the region in 1498, and the Portuguese took control of coastal towns, including the Island of Mozambique, which became their capital. Later Portugal used forced labor and exported enslaved people."],["Independence","In 1962 the Mozambique Liberation Front, FRELIMO, was founded and began an armed struggle in 1964. After Portugal’s 1974 Carnation Revolution, Mozambique became independent on 25 June 1975, with Samora Machel as president."],["Civil war","A civil war between the FRELIMO government and the rebel movement RENAMO, backed by white-minority governments in Rhodesia and South Africa, lasted from 1977 to 1992 and killed about a million people, through fighting, famine and disease. A peace agreement was signed in Rome in 1992."]],
k:[["Capital","Maputo"],["Population","About 34 million (2024)"],["Area","About 802,000 km²"],["Highest point","Monte Binga, 2,436 m"],["Currency","Mozambican metical"]],
d:[["1498","Vasco da Gama reaches the coast"],["1964","The war for independence begins"],["1975","Mozambique becomes independent"],["1977","Civil war begins"],["1992","A peace agreement ends the civil war"],["2017","An insurgency begins in Cabo Delgado province"],["2019","Cyclone Idai devastates central Mozambique"]],
s2:[["Gorongosa","Gorongosa National Park lost most of its large animals during the civil war. Since the 2000s, a restoration project has brought wildlife back, reintroducing species such as wild dogs and leopards, while supporting schools, health care and farming for surrounding communities. It has become a celebrated example of conservation and recovery."],["Cyclones","Mozambique’s low-lying coast is exposed to tropical cyclones from the Indian Ocean. In March 2019 Cyclone Idai struck the port city of Beira and flooded large areas, killing hundreds and affecting millions in Mozambique, Malawi and Zimbabwe. Cyclone Kenneth hit the north weeks later."],["The north","Since 2017 an insurgency by armed groups linked to the Islamic State has affected Cabo Delgado province in the north, near large natural gas fields, killing thousands and displacing hundreds of thousands. Mozambican forces, with Rwandan and southern African troops, have fought to regain control."],["Island of Mozambique","The Island of Mozambique, once the Portuguese colonial capital, has old forts, churches, mosques and houses built of coral stone, reflecting Swahili, Arab, Indian and Portuguese cultures. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Culture","Mozambican music includes marrabenta, a dance music from Maputo, and the timbila xylophone orchestras of the Chopi people, recognized by UNESCO. Portuguese is the official language, but most people also speak Bantu languages such as Makhuwa, Changana and Sena. Spicy piri-piri chicken is linked to Mozambican and Portuguese cooking."],["A rifle on the flag","Mozambique’s flag includes an AK-47 rifle with a bayonet, crossed with a hoe, over a book, symbolizing defense, agriculture and education. It is the only national flag to show a modern rifle, and there have been debates about changing it."]],
sp:["The return of Gorongosa","By the end of Mozambique’s civil war in 1992, Gorongosa National Park, once famous for its huge herds, had lost more than 90 percent of its large animals to war and hunting. In the 2000s, the Mozambican government and the American philanthropist Greg Carr formed a partnership to restore it. Rangers, many recruited from nearby villages, protected the animals, and species were brought back from other parks. Elephant, buffalo and antelope numbers grew, and lions recovered. The park also built schools and clinics. Gorongosa showed that even a devastated ecosystem can recover with people’s help."],
g:[["FRELIMO","The movement that led Mozambique to independence"],["RENAMO","The rebel movement in Mozambique’s civil war"],["Insurgency","An armed rebellion against a government"],["Marrabenta","A popular dance music from Maputo"],["Cyclone","A powerful tropical storm, like a typhoon"]],
f:["Mozambique became independent in 1975.","Its civil war ended in 1992.","Its flag shows an AK-47 rifle."],
fun:"Mozambique is the only country with a modern rifle on its flag."});

ADDART({id:"camerooncountry",t:"Cameroon",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌋",w:"Cameroon",r:["nigeriacountry","africacolon","french","soccer","volc","drccountry"],
p:[["Africa in miniature","Cameroon has deserts, savannas, rainforests, mountains and beaches."],["Two official languages","Both French and English are official languages."],["The Indomitable Lions","Cameroon’s football team was the first African side to reach a World Cup quarter-final."]],
l:"Cameroon is a country in Central Africa, with about 29 million people. Its capital is Yaoundé, and its largest city and main port is Douala. Often called Africa in miniature because of its great variety of landscapes, peoples and cultures, it has rainforests, savannas, mountains, volcanoes, lakes and a coastline on the Gulf of Guinea. Colonized by Germany and then divided between France and Britain, Cameroon became independent in 1960 and united in 1961, leaving it with both French and English as official languages. It is famous for its football, music and more than 250 languages, while facing conflicts in its English-speaking regions and the far north.",
s:[["Land","Cameroon’s south is covered by dense rainforest, the center by plateaus and the north by savanna reaching Lake Chad. Mount Cameroon, an active volcano near the coast, is the highest peak in West and Central Africa. The southwest coast near it is one of the rainiest places on Earth."],["Name and early history","Portuguese sailors arrived in 1472 and named the Wouri River Rio dos Camarões, river of shrimps, after the many shrimps they saw, which gave the country its name. Kingdoms such as Bamum, whose king Njoya created a writing system, flourished inland."],["Colonial era","Germany made Kamerun a colony in 1884. After World War I, it was divided between France and Britain. French Cameroon became independent on 1 January 1960, and in 1961 the southern part of British Cameroons voted to join it, while the northern part joined Nigeria."],["Since independence","Ahmadou Ahidjo was the first president. Cameroon became a unitary state in 1972, celebrated on 20 May as national day. Paul Biya became president in 1982 and is one of the world’s longest-serving leaders; supporters credit him with stability, while critics point to limited political freedoms and disputed elections."]],
k:[["Capital","Yaoundé"],["Population","About 29 million (2024)"],["Area","About 475,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Cameroon, about 4,040 m"],["Currency","Central African CFA franc"]],
d:[["1472","Portuguese sailors name the Rio dos Camarões"],["1884","Germany makes Kamerun a colony"],["1960","French Cameroon becomes independent"],["1961","Southern British Cameroons joins Cameroon"],["1972","Cameroon becomes a unitary state"],["1986","A gas eruption from Lake Nyos kills more than 1,700 people"],["1990","Cameroon reaches the World Cup quarter-finals"]],
s2:[["The Anglophone crisis","Since 2016, protests by lawyers and teachers in the English-speaking Northwest and Southwest regions over the dominance of French in courts and schools grew into an armed conflict between separatists and government forces. Thousands have been killed and hundreds of thousands displaced, and efforts at dialogue continue."],["Lake Nyos","On 21 August 1986, Lake Nyos, a crater lake in northwestern Cameroon, suddenly released a huge cloud of carbon dioxide gas from its depths. The heavy gas flowed into nearby valleys, suffocating more than 1,700 people and thousands of cattle. Scientists now pump gas out of the lake through pipes to prevent another disaster."],["Football","Cameroon’s national team, the Indomitable Lions, shocked the world at the 1990 World Cup by beating defending champions Argentina and reaching the quarter-finals, the first African team to do so. The 38-year-old Roger Milla celebrated his goals with a famous dance by the corner flag. Samuel Eto’o later became one of Africa’s greatest players."],["Music","Cameroon is famous for makossa, a dance music from Douala, made world-famous by Manu Dibango’s 1972 hit Soul Makossa, and for bikutsi, a fast rhythm from the central region. Cameroonian musicians remain popular across Africa."],["Many peoples","Cameroon has more than 250 ethnic groups and languages, including the Baka people of the rainforest, known for their forest knowledge and polyphonic singing. French and English are official, and Cameroonian Pidgin English is widely spoken."],["Wildlife","Cameroon’s forests shelter gorillas, chimpanzees, forest elephants and many birds and frogs, including the goliath frog, the largest frog in the world. The Dja Faunal Reserve is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
sp:["King Njoya’s alphabet","Around 1896, Ibrahim Njoya, king of the Bamum people in western Cameroon, created a writing system for his language after a dream, it is said, and refined it over the following years with his court. Starting as picture-like symbols, it gradually became a syllabic script. Njoya founded schools, wrote a history of his people and a book of laws, designed a calendar and even a map of his kingdom. Under colonial rule, his schools were closed, but the Bamum script survived and is now included in international computer standards, so it can be used on phones and computers."],
g:[["Anglophone","English-speaking"],["Francophone","French-speaking"],["Unitary state","A country governed as a single unit"],["Makossa","A popular dance music from Douala"],["Crater lake","A lake formed in a volcanic crater"]],
f:["Cameroon has both French and English as official languages.","Its name comes from the Portuguese word for shrimps.","The goliath frog lives in Cameroon."],
fun:"Cameroon is named after shrimps seen by Portuguese sailors in 1472."});

ADDART({id:"cotedivoirecountry",t:"Côte d’Ivoire",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🍫",w:"Ivory Coast",r:["ghanacountry","choco","africacolon","french","soccer","senegalcountry"],
p:[["Cocoa giant","Côte d’Ivoire grows more cocoa than any other country."],["Two capitals","Yamoussoukro is the official capital, while Abidjan is the largest city."],["Football and peace","Its football stars once helped call for an end to civil war."]],
l:"Côte d’Ivoire, also known as Ivory Coast, is a country in West Africa on the Gulf of Guinea, with about 32 million people. Its official capital is Yamoussoukro, but Abidjan is the largest city and economic center. The country is the world’s largest producer of cocoa, the main ingredient of chocolate. A French colony until 1960, it was long one of West Africa’s most prosperous and stable countries under its first president, Félix Houphouët-Boigny, before suffering civil conflict in the 2000s. Since 2011 its economy has grown rapidly. It is known for its music, cuisine and football.",
s:[["Land","The south has lagoons, beaches and tropical rainforest, while the north is covered by savanna. Mount Nimba, on the border with Guinea and Liberia, is the highest point. Taï National Park protects one of the last large areas of West African rainforest, home to chimpanzees and pygmy hippopotamuses."],["Colonial history","European traders named the coast after the ivory trade. France made it a colony in 1893, with capitals at Grand-Bassam and later Bingerville and Abidjan. French colonists developed plantations of coffee and cocoa, often using forced labor."],["Independence","Côte d’Ivoire became independent on 7 August 1960, led by Félix Houphouët-Boigny, a cocoa planter and politician who ruled until his death in 1993. Coffee and cocoa exports brought rapid growth, called the Ivorian miracle, and Abidjan became a modern city with skyscrapers."],["Conflict","After Houphouët-Boigny’s death, disputes over identity, citizenship and power led to a coup in 1999, a civil war that began in 2002 and divided the country between north and south, and a violent crisis after the disputed 2010 election in which about 3,000 people died. Alassane Ouattara became president in 2011."]],
k:[["Official capital","Yamoussoukro"],["Largest city","Abidjan"],["Population","About 32 million (2024)"],["Highest point","Mount Nimba, 1,752 m"],["Currency","West African CFA franc"]],
d:[["1893","France makes the territory a colony"],["1960","Côte d’Ivoire becomes independent"],["1983","Yamoussoukro becomes the official capital"],["1990","The Basilica of Our Lady of Peace is consecrated"],["2002","Civil war begins"],["2011","A post-election crisis ends"],["2024","Côte d’Ivoire wins the Africa Cup of Nations as host"]],
s2:[["Cocoa","Côte d’Ivoire produces around two-fifths of the world’s cocoa, grown mostly by hundreds of thousands of small farmers. The cocoa industry supports millions of people but has faced problems such as low prices for farmers, deforestation and child labor. Governments and companies have launched programs to address them."],["The basilica","In Yamoussoukro, Houphouët-Boigny’s hometown, stands the Basilica of Our Lady of Peace, modeled on St Peter’s Basilica in Rome and consecrated by Pope John Paul II in 1990. It is one of the largest churches in the world, and its cost in a poor country caused controversy."],["Drogba’s appeal","In October 2005, after Côte d’Ivoire qualified for its first World Cup during the civil war, the team captain, Didier Drogba, knelt with his teammates in the dressing room and, on live television, begged both sides to put down their weapons. His plea is often credited with helping encourage peace talks."],["Football","The national team, the Elephants, has won the Africa Cup of Nations three times, most recently in 2024, when, as host, it came back from near elimination to win the title. Players such as Drogba and Yaya Touré became global stars."],["Music and food","Côte d’Ivoire is famous for coupé-décalé, an energetic dance music born among Ivorians in Paris, and for zouglou. Popular foods include attiéké, fermented cassava couscous, often served with grilled fish, and alloco, fried plantains."],["Abidjan","Abidjan, built around the Ébrié Lagoon, is one of the largest French-speaking cities in the world and a hub for business, banking and culture in West Africa. It hosts the headquarters of the African Development Bank."]],
sp:["The captain who asked for peace","On 8 October 2005, Côte d’Ivoire beat Sudan and qualified for the World Cup for the first time, while the country was split by civil war. In the dressing room, as television cameras filmed the celebrations, the captain, Didier Drogba, asked for a microphone. Surrounded by his teammates, he knelt and said that the people of Côte d’Ivoire had proved they could live together and play together, and he begged the warring sides to lay down their weapons and hold elections. The appeal was broadcast across the country. A ceasefire followed in 2007, and many Ivorians credit the team with helping bring the country together."],
g:[["Cocoa","Seeds of the cacao tree, used to make chocolate"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea"],["Attiéké","Fermented cassava couscous"],["Coup","The sudden seizure of power, often by the military"],["Basilica","A large, important church"]],
f:["Côte d’Ivoire is the world’s largest cocoa producer.","Yamoussoukro is the official capital.","Its football team won the 2024 Africa Cup of Nations."],
fun:"A football captain’s televised plea helped encourage peace in Côte d’Ivoire."});

ADDART({id:"tunisiacountry",t:"Tunisia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏛️",w:"Tunisia",r:["phoenicians","rome","algeriacountry","middleeast","islam","sahara"],
p:[["Ancient Carthage","Tunisia was home to Carthage, the great rival of ancient Rome."],["Where the Arab Spring began","Protests in Tunisia in 2010–2011 sparked uprisings across the Arab world."],["A Nobel for dialogue","Tunisian civil society groups won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2015."]],
l:"Tunisia is the northernmost country in Africa, on the Mediterranean Sea, with about 12 million people. Its capital is Tunis. Home of ancient Carthage, founded by Phoenicians and the great rival of Rome, Tunisia was later a Roman province, an early center of Islamic civilization and part of the Ottoman Empire before becoming a French protectorate. Independent since 1956, it became known for women’s rights and education. In 2010 and 2011 its revolution sparked the Arab Spring, and it built a democracy that won a Nobel Peace Prize for its dialogue, though its politics have since grown more contested.",
s:[["Land","Northern Tunisia has green hills, forests and fertile plains growing wheat, olives and citrus fruits. The center has steppes and salt lakes, and the south is part of the Sahara Desert. The long Mediterranean coast has beaches and islands such as Djerba."],["Carthage","According to tradition, Phoenician settlers from Tyre founded Carthage in 814 BCE. It became a powerful trading empire across the western Mediterranean and fought three Punic Wars with Rome. The general Hannibal famously crossed the Alps with elephants. Rome destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE but later rebuilt it as a Roman city."],["Islamic Tunisia","Arab armies arrived in the 600s, founding Kairouan in 670, whose Great Mosque is one of the oldest in Africa. Dynasties such as the Aghlabids and Hafsids ruled, and Tunis became a center of trade and learning. The Ottomans took control in the 1500s."],["Protectorate and independence","France made Tunisia a protectorate in 1881. After a nationalist movement led by Habib Bourguiba, Tunisia became independent on 20 March 1956. Bourguiba, its first president, promoted education and women’s rights, banning polygamy through the 1956 Code of Personal Status."]],
k:[["Capital","Tunis"],["Population","About 12 million (2024)"],["Area","About 164,000 km²"],["Highest point","Jebel ech Chambi, 1,544 m"],["Currency","Tunisian dinar"]],
d:[["814 BCE","Carthage is founded, according to tradition"],["146 BCE","Rome destroys Carthage"],["670","Kairouan is founded"],["1881","France makes Tunisia a protectorate"],["1956","Tunisia becomes independent"],["2011","President Ben Ali flees after a popular uprising"],["2015","The Tunisian National Dialogue Quartet wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["The revolution","On 17 December 2010, Mohamed Bouazizi, a young street vendor in Sidi Bouzid, set himself on fire after officials confiscated his goods. Protests against unemployment, corruption and repression spread across Tunisia, and on 14 January 2011, President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali, in power since 1987, fled the country. The uprising inspired protests across the Arab world, known as the Arab Spring."],["Building democracy","Tunisia held free elections and adopted a new constitution in 2014. When political tensions threatened to turn violent, four civil society groups, representing trade unions, employers, lawyers and human rights defenders, brought parties together in a national dialogue. This National Dialogue Quartet received the 2015 Nobel Peace Prize."],["Recent politics","In 2021 President Kais Saied suspended parliament, later dissolved it and introduced a new constitution in 2022 that greatly increased presidential powers. Supporters said he was rescuing the country from corruption and political paralysis; critics, including many who supported the 2011 revolution, described it as a reversal of democracy, and some opponents have been jailed."],["Ancient sites","Tunisia has remarkable Roman ruins, including the amphitheater of El Djem, one of the largest in the Roman world, and beautiful mosaics in the Bardo Museum in Tunis. The ruins of Carthage and the medinas of Tunis, Sousse and Kairouan are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."],["Food","Tunisian cuisine includes couscous, recognized by UNESCO as shared heritage of North Africa, brik, a crispy pastry with egg, and harissa, a spicy chili paste also recognized by UNESCO. Tunisia is one of the world’s largest exporters of olive oil."],["Film location","Desert villages and underground homes in southern Tunisia, such as those in Matmata and near Tozeur, were used to film scenes of the planet Tatooine in the Star Wars films, attracting tourists to the sets."]],
sp:["Elephants over the Alps","In 218 BCE, during the Second Punic War between Carthage and Rome, the Carthaginian general Hannibal Barca set out from Spain with tens of thousands of soldiers, horses and around 37 war elephants. Instead of attacking Rome by sea, he marched across the Pyrenees and then over the snowy Alps into Italy, a journey few thought possible. Many soldiers and most elephants died from cold and hardship, but Hannibal won stunning victories, including at Cannae in 216 BCE. Although Carthage eventually lost the war, Hannibal is remembered as one of history’s greatest military leaders."],
g:[["Carthage","An ancient city-state in Tunisia, rival of Rome"],["Punic Wars","Three wars between Carthage and Rome"],["Protectorate","A territory controlled by a foreign power"],["Arab Spring","Uprisings across the Arab world from 2010–2011"],["Medina","The old walled part of a North African city"]],
f:["Carthage was in present-day Tunisia.","Tunisia’s 2011 revolution sparked the Arab Spring.","Tunisian groups won the 2015 Nobel Peace Prize."],
fun:"Star Wars scenes of the desert planet Tatooine were filmed in Tunisia."});

