ADDART({id:"guatemalacountry",t:"Guatemala",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌋",w:"Guatemala",r:["maya","mexicocountry","volc","coffee","hrights","spanph"],
p:[["Heart of the Maya","Guatemala was a center of the ancient Maya civilization."],["Land of volcanoes","Volcanoes tower over its highlands and lakes."],["Living Maya cultures","Millions of Maya people keep their languages and traditions alive."]],
l:"Guatemala is a country in Central America, with about 18 million people, the most populous in the region. Its capital is Guatemala City. Guatemala was a heartland of the ancient Maya civilization, whose great cities, such as Tikal, rise from its rainforests. Today about two-fifths of Guatemalans are Indigenous Maya, speaking more than 20 Mayan languages and famous for their colorful woven textiles. Conquered by Spain in the 1500s, Guatemala became independent in 1821. In the 20th century it suffered a long civil war, from 1960 to 1996, in which some 200,000 people were killed or disappeared, most of them Maya.",
s:[["Land","Guatemala has Pacific lowlands, central highlands crowned by more than 30 volcanoes, and the northern lowland rainforests of Petén. Volcán Tajumulco is the highest peak in Central America. Lake Atitlán, surrounded by volcanoes and Maya villages, is famous for its beauty."],["The Maya","The Maya built cities with pyramids, palaces and ball courts, developed a writing system, a calendar and advanced mathematics, including the concept of zero. Tikal, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, was one of their greatest cities, flourishing between about 200 and 900 CE."],["Spanish conquest","Spanish forces led by Pedro de Alvarado conquered the region from 1524. Antigua Guatemala became the capital of a large colonial region until earthquakes in 1773 led to the founding of Guatemala City. The colonial city of Antigua is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Independence and the 20th century","Guatemala declared independence from Spain on 15 September 1821. In 1954 a coup supported by the United States overthrew the elected president Jacobo Árbenz, whose land reforms had angered a powerful American fruit company. Political instability followed."]],
k:[["Capital","Guatemala City"],["Population","About 18 million (2024)"],["Area","About 108,900 km²"],["Highest point","Volcán Tajumulco, 4,220 m"],["Currency","Quetzal"]],
d:[["c. 200–900","Classic Maya cities such as Tikal flourish"],["1524","Spanish conquest begins"],["1773","Earthquakes destroy Antigua Guatemala"],["1821","Guatemala becomes independent from Spain"],["1954","A US-backed coup overthrows President Árbenz"],["1996","Peace accords end the civil war"],["2018","The Fuego volcano erupts, killing hundreds"]],
s2:[["The civil war","From 1960 to 1996, government forces fought leftist guerrillas. The army and paramilitary groups killed and disappeared tens of thousands of civilians, especially Maya villagers. A UN-backed truth commission found that state forces committed acts of genocide against Maya communities in the early 1980s. Peace accords were signed on 29 December 1996."],["Rigoberta Menchú","Rigoberta Menchú, a K’iche’ Maya woman whose family members were killed during the war, became an internationally known campaigner for Indigenous rights. She was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1992, the 500th anniversary of Columbus’s arrival in the Americas."],["Maya cultures today","Maya communities speak languages such as K’iche’, Q’eqchi’, Kaqchikel and Mam, and many wear traditional clothing such as the huipil, a woven blouse whose patterns show a person’s community. Maya spiritual traditions, markets and festivals remain vibrant, though Indigenous people still face poverty and discrimination."],["The quetzal","The resplendent quetzal, a bird with brilliant green feathers and a long tail, is Guatemala’s national symbol and gives its name to the currency. It was sacred to the ancient Maya and appears on the national flag."],["Volcanoes and disasters","Guatemala’s volcanoes provide fertile soils for coffee but also danger. In June 2018, the Fuego volcano erupted with fast-moving flows of hot gas and ash, killing hundreds of people. Earthquakes, such as the one in 1976, have also caused great destruction."],["Economy and migration","Guatemala exports coffee, bananas, sugar and cardamom, of which it is one of the world’s largest producers. Many Guatemalans have migrated to the United States, and their remittances are important to the economy. In 2023, voters elected Bernardo Arévalo as president on a promise to fight corruption."]],
sp:["Tikal in the rainforest","Deep in the rainforest of northern Guatemala, the temples of Tikal rise above the treetops, some more than 60 meters tall. At its height, around 700 CE, Tikal may have been home to tens of thousands of people, with plazas, palaces, reservoirs and ball courts. After its decline, the jungle swallowed it for centuries. In recent years, scientists using lidar, laser scanning from aircraft, revealed thousands of previously hidden Maya structures, roads and farms beneath the forest around Tikal, showing that the Maya world was far larger and more connected than once thought."],
g:[["Maya","An Indigenous people and ancient civilization of Central America"],["Huipil","A traditional woven Maya blouse"],["Quetzal","A brilliantly colored bird and Guatemala’s currency"],["Peace accords","Agreements that end a war"],["Lidar","Laser scanning used to map land, even under forests"]],
f:["Guatemala was a heartland of the Maya.","Its civil war lasted from 1960 to 1996.","Rigoberta Menchú won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1992."],
fun:"Lasers from aircraft have revealed thousands of hidden Maya structures in Guatemala’s forests."});

ADDART({id:"costaricacountry",t:"Costa Rica",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦥",w:"Costa Rica",r:["conservation","renew","deforestation","coffee","panamacountry","tourism"],
p:[["A country without an army","Costa Rica abolished its army in 1948."],["Rich in nature","It holds a large share of the world’s species in a small area."],["Pura vida","Costa Ricans’ motto, pura vida, means pure life."]],
l:"Costa Rica is a small country in Central America, between the Caribbean Sea and the Pacific Ocean, with about 5 million people. Its capital is San José. Costa Rica is famous for abolishing its army in 1948 and investing instead in education and health, for its long-standing democracy and for its extraordinary biodiversity, protected in national parks covering a large part of its territory. It generates almost all its electricity from renewable sources and has reversed deforestation, becoming a world leader in ecotourism and conservation. Costa Ricans, called Ticos, often greet each other with pura vida, pure life.",
s:[["Land","Costa Rica has beaches on two oceans, volcanic mountains, cloud forests, rainforests and dry forests. Volcanoes such as Arenal and Poás attract visitors, and Cerro Chirripó is the highest peak. The Osa Peninsula and Corcovado National Park are among the most biodiverse places on Earth."],["History","Before the Spanish arrived, the region was home to Indigenous peoples, including those who made mysterious stone spheres in the south. Christopher Columbus reached its Caribbean coast in 1502. Costa Rica became independent from Spain in 1821 and grew rich from coffee in the 1800s."],["Abolishing the army","After a short civil war in 1948, President José Figueres Ferrer abolished the army, symbolically breaking down a wall of the army headquarters with a hammer. The constitution of 1949 banned a permanent army. Money once spent on the military went to schools, health care and social programs."],["Democracy","Costa Rica has one of the oldest and most stable democracies in Latin America, with free elections and peaceful transfers of power. In 1987 President Óscar Arias won the Nobel Peace Prize for his plan to end wars in Central America."]],
k:[["Capital","San José"],["Population","About 5.1 million (2024)"],["Area","About 51,100 km²"],["Highest point","Cerro Chirripó, 3,820 m"],["Currency","Costa Rican colón"]],
d:[["1502","Columbus reaches the Caribbean coast"],["1821","Costa Rica becomes independent from Spain"],["1869","Primary education becomes free and compulsory"],["1948","Costa Rica abolishes its army"],["1987","Óscar Arias wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["1996","A forestry law begins paying landowners to protect forests"],["2021","Costa Rica wins an Earthshot Prize for restoring forests"]],
s2:[["Biodiversity","Though it covers only a tiny fraction of the Earth’s land, Costa Rica is home to about 5 percent of the world’s species, including sloths, toucans, quetzals, jaguars, poison dart frogs, hummingbirds and sea turtles that nest on its beaches. About a quarter of the country is protected in parks and reserves."],["Bringing back the forests","By the 1980s, cattle ranching and logging had cleared much of Costa Rica’s forest. The country then began paying landowners to protect and restore forests, banned clearing of mature forest and promoted ecotourism. Forest cover has grown back to more than half the country, and in 2021 Costa Rica won the Earthshot Prize for this work."],["Clean energy","Costa Rica generates almost all of its electricity from renewable sources, mainly hydropower, along with geothermal, wind and solar power. It has set goals to cut greenhouse gas emissions across its economy."],["Ecotourism","Visitors come to watch wildlife, hike in cloud forests such as Monteverde, surf and see volcanoes. Ecotourism is a major source of income and jobs, encouraging communities to protect nature."],["Long lives","The Nicoya Peninsula is often cited as one of the world’s regions where people live unusually long lives, linked to strong family and community ties, physical activity and traditional diets, though researchers debate the reasons."],["Economy","Costa Rica exports coffee, bananas and pineapples, of which it is the world’s largest exporter, as well as medical devices made by international companies. Challenges include inequality, rising crime linked to drug trafficking and the cost of living."]],
sp:["The hammer that ended an army","On 1 December 1948, after winning a brief civil war, José Figueres Ferrer, head of Costa Rica’s provisional government, stood at the Bellavista military barracks in San José and struck a wall with a sledgehammer, announcing the abolition of the army. He handed the keys of the barracks to the education minister. The building became the National Museum of Costa Rica. Since then, Costa Rica has had no army, relying on its police and international law for security, and 1 December is celebrated as the Day of the Abolition of the Army."],
g:[["Biodiversity","The variety of living things in an area"],["Ecotourism","Travel to natural areas that supports conservation"],["Cloud forest","A mountain forest often covered in mist"],["Ticos","A nickname for Costa Ricans"],["Pura vida","A Costa Rican saying meaning pure life"]],
f:["Costa Rica abolished its army in 1948.","It has about 5 percent of the world’s species.","Almost all its electricity is renewable."],
fun:"Costa Rica’s former army headquarters is now the National Museum."});

ADDART({id:"panamacountry",t:"Panama",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🚢",w:"Panama",r:["canals","colombiacountry","costaricacountry","usacountry","ships","trade"],
p:[["Between two oceans","The Panama Canal links the Atlantic and Pacific oceans."],["Joining the Americas","Panama connects North and South America."],["Crossroads of the world","Ships, goods and people from around the world pass through Panama."]],
l:"Panama is a country on the narrow isthmus that connects Central and South America, with about 4.5 million people. Its capital is Panama City. Panama is best known for the Panama Canal, which cuts across the isthmus to link the Atlantic and Pacific oceans, carrying a significant share of world trade. Once part of Colombia, Panama became independent in 1903 with American support, and the United States built and controlled the canal until handing it to Panama in 1999. Panama is a hub for shipping, banking and air travel, with rainforests, islands and Indigenous peoples such as the Guna.",
s:[["A land bridge","About three million years ago, the Isthmus of Panama rose from the sea, joining North and South America. Animals crossed in both directions, and the land bridge changed ocean currents and climates. Today Panama’s forests are rich in wildlife."],["Spanish era","In 1513 the Spanish explorer Vasco Núñez de Balboa crossed the isthmus and became the first European to see the Pacific Ocean from the Americas. Panama City, founded in 1519, was the first European city on the Pacific coast of the Americas. Gold and silver from Peru were carried across Panama to ships bound for Spain."],["Independence","Panama became independent from Spain in 1821 and joined Colombia. In 1903, when Colombia refused to approve an American canal treaty, Panama declared independence with US support, and the United States gained control of a strip of land, the Canal Zone."],["The modern country","Panama was ruled by military leaders for parts of the 20th century. In December 1989 the United States invaded Panama and removed the military ruler Manuel Noriega. Since then Panama has held democratic elections and grown into one of the fastest-growing economies in Latin America."]],
k:[["Capital","Panama City"],["Population","About 4.5 million (2024)"],["Area","About 75,000 km²"],["Highest point","Volcán Barú, 3,475 m"],["Currency","Balboa and US dollar"]],
d:[["1513","Balboa sees the Pacific Ocean"],["1519","Panama City is founded"],["1821","Panama becomes independent from Spain and joins Colombia"],["1903","Panama separates from Colombia"],["1914","The Panama Canal opens"],["1999","The canal is handed over to Panama"],["2016","The expanded canal opens"]],
s2:[["The Panama Canal","The Panama Canal, about 80 kilometers long, opened on 15 August 1914. Ships are raised and lowered by locks, water-filled chambers, to cross Gatun Lake, an artificial lake about 26 meters above sea level. A trip from New York to San Francisco through the canal saves about 13,000 kilometers compared with sailing around South America."],["Building the canal","France began building a canal in 1881 under Ferdinand de Lesseps, builder of the Suez Canal, but gave up after disease, landslides and financial scandal; more than 20,000 workers died. The United States took over in 1904, controlled yellow fever and malaria by fighting mosquitoes and completed the canal using tens of thousands of workers, many from the Caribbean."],["Returning the canal","The Torrijos–Carter Treaties of 1977 agreed to transfer the canal to Panama. On 31 December 1999, Panama took full control. Under Panamanian management, the canal was expanded with larger locks, opened in 2016, allowing much bigger ships to pass."],["Water and the canal","Each ship passing through the canal uses millions of liters of fresh water from Gatun Lake. In 2023 a severe drought lowered the lake, forcing the canal to limit the number and size of ships, showing how climate affects world trade."],["The Guna","The Guna people live in the autonomous region of Guna Yala, a chain of islands and coast on the Caribbean. They have their own government and are famous for molas, colorful layered textile panels sewn by women. In 1925 the Guna revolted to protect their culture and won autonomy."],["The Darién Gap","The Darién, a roadless region of rainforest and mountains on the border with Colombia, interrupts the Pan-American Highway. In recent years, hundreds of thousands of migrants seeking to reach North America have made dangerous journeys through it."]],
sp:["The ship that opened the canal","On 15 August 1914, the cargo and passenger ship SS Ancon made the first official passage through the Panama Canal, traveling from the Atlantic to the Pacific in about nine hours. The opening received less attention than planned because World War I had broken out in Europe just days earlier. Yet the canal transformed world shipping. Today, roughly 13,000 to 14,000 ships pass through it in a typical year, paying tolls based on their size. The highest tolls have been hundreds of thousands of dollars for large cruise ships, while the lowest ever was 36 cents, paid in 1928 by the adventurer Richard Halliburton, who swam the canal."],
g:[["Isthmus","A narrow strip of land connecting two larger areas"],["Lock","A water-filled chamber that raises or lowers ships"],["Canal Zone","The strip of land around the canal once controlled by the US"],["Mola","A colorful layered textile made by Guna women"],["Autonomy","Self-government"]],
f:["The Panama Canal opened in 1914.","Panama took control of the canal in 1999.","The isthmus joins North and South America."],
fun:"A man paid just 36 cents to swim through the Panama Canal in 1928."});

ADDART({id:"haiticountry",t:"Haiti",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏰",w:"Haiti",r:["toussaint","slavetrade","frrev","cubacountry","quake","francecountry"],
p:[["The first free Black republic","Haiti won independence in 1804 after a revolution by enslaved people."],["A proud culture","Haiti is famous for its art, music, food and Creole language."],["Facing hardship","Haiti has faced disasters, poverty and political crises."]],
l:"Haiti is a country on the western part of the Caribbean island of Hispaniola, which it shares with the Dominican Republic, with about 12 million people. Its capital is Port-au-Prince. Once the French colony of Saint-Domingue, the richest colony in the Caribbean, built on the labor of enslaved Africans, Haiti became in 1804 the first independent nation founded by formerly enslaved people, after the only slave revolution to create a state. Haiti has a rich culture of art, music and the Haitian Creole language, but it has suffered foreign debts and interventions, dictatorship, a devastating earthquake in 2010 and, in recent years, severe insecurity caused by armed gangs.",
s:[["Land","Haiti is mountainous; its name comes from the Taíno word Ayiti, land of high mountains. Much of its forest has been cut down, making it vulnerable to floods and landslides during hurricanes. The island of La Gonâve and the Citadelle in the north are among its landmarks."],["Saint-Domingue","Christopher Columbus reached Hispaniola in 1492, and the Taíno population was devastated. In 1697 France gained the western part of the island. Saint-Domingue produced huge amounts of sugar and coffee, worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans under brutal conditions."],["The Haitian Revolution","In August 1791, enslaved people rose up in the north. Led by Toussaint Louverture and later Jean-Jacques Dessalines and Henri Christophe, they defeated French, Spanish and British forces, including an army sent by Napoleon. On 1 January 1804, Dessalines declared the independence of Haiti."],["A costly freedom","In 1825 France demanded a huge payment from Haiti, as compensation for former slave owners, in exchange for recognizing its independence, backed by warships. Haiti paid this debt and the loans taken to pay it for many decades, draining its economy. The United States occupied Haiti from 1915 to 1934."]],
k:[["Capital","Port-au-Prince"],["Population","About 12 million (2024)"],["Area","About 27,750 km²"],["Highest point","Pic la Selle, 2,680 m"],["Currency","Haitian gourde"]],
d:[["1492","Columbus reaches Hispaniola"],["1697","France gains the western part of Hispaniola"],["1791","The Haitian Revolution begins"],["1804","Haiti declares independence"],["1825","France demands a huge payment for recognizing Haiti"],["1915","The United States begins occupying Haiti"],["2010","A powerful earthquake strikes near Port-au-Prince"]],
s2:[["The 2010 earthquake","On 12 January 2010, a magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck near Port-au-Prince, destroying homes, schools, hospitals and government buildings. Estimates of the dead range from about 100,000 to more than 300,000. The world sent aid, but recovery was slow, and a cholera outbreak introduced by UN peacekeepers later killed thousands."],["Political crises","After the Duvalier family’s dictatorship from 1957 to 1986, Haiti struggled to build stable democracy, with coups, disputed elections and foreign interventions. In July 2021 President Jovenel Moïse was assassinated. Armed gangs have since taken control of much of Port-au-Prince, causing violence, displacement and hunger, and international security missions have been authorized to support Haitian police."],["The Citadelle","After independence, King Henri Christophe built the Citadelle Laferrière, a massive fortress on a mountaintop in the north, to defend against a French return. With the nearby Sans-Souci Palace, it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and the largest fortress in the Americas."],["Language and faith","Haitian Creole, based on French with African and other influences, is spoken by nearly all Haitians, and French is also official. Most Haitians are Christian, and many also practice Vodou, a religion that blends African traditions with Catholicism."],["Art and food","Haiti is famous for its vibrant paintings, metal sculptures and music such as kompa. On Independence Day, 1 January, Haitians eat soup joumou, a pumpkin soup once forbidden to the enslaved, as a symbol of freedom; UNESCO recognized it in 2021."],["Haitians abroad","Large Haitian communities live in the United States, Canada, France, the Dominican Republic and elsewhere. Money sent home by Haitians abroad is a major part of the economy."]],
sp:["Soup joumou, the soup of freedom","In colonial Saint-Domingue, soup made with joumou, a kind of squash or pumpkin, was a delicacy enjoyed by French slave owners, and enslaved people who cooked it were forbidden to eat it. After Haiti won its independence on 1 January 1804, Haitians began eating soup joumou on New Year’s Day to celebrate their freedom. The tradition continues in Haitian homes around the world, with families sharing the soup, rich with beef, vegetables and pasta, every 1 January. In 2021 UNESCO added soup joumou to its list of intangible cultural heritage."],
g:[["Hispaniola","The Caribbean island shared by Haiti and the Dominican Republic"],["Haitian Creole","The language spoken by nearly all Haitians"],["Indemnity","A payment demanded as compensation"],["Vodou","A Haitian religion blending African and Catholic traditions"],["Kompa","A popular Haitian dance music"]],
f:["Haiti became independent in 1804.","It was founded by formerly enslaved people.","Soup joumou is eaten on Independence Day."],
fun:"Haitians eat pumpkin soup every 1 January to celebrate their freedom."});

ADDART({id:"uruguaycountry",t:"Uruguay",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🧉",w:"Uruguay",r:["argentinacountry","brazilcountry","soccer","democ","renew","wind"],
p:[["A small, stable country","Uruguay is known for its democracy, social policies and quality of life."],["World Cup history","Uruguay hosted and won the first football World Cup in 1930."],["Mate culture","Uruguayans drink more mate tea per person than almost anyone."]],
l:"Uruguay is a small country in southeastern South America, between Argentina and Brazil, with about 3.4 million people, nearly half of whom live in the capital, Montevideo. It is known for its strong democracy, low corruption, progressive social laws and high quality of life. Uruguay hosted and won the first football World Cup in 1930 and won again in 1950. It is a land of rolling grasslands and cattle, with more cows than people, and it generates most of its electricity from renewable sources, especially wind. Its people share with Argentina a love of tango, beef and mate tea.",
s:[["Land","Uruguay is mostly low rolling grassland, called pampas, ideal for cattle and sheep, with rivers, lagoons and Atlantic beaches. Its highest hill is only about 514 meters high. The Río de la Plata estuary separates it from Argentina."],["History","The Charrúa and other Indigenous peoples lived in the region. The Portuguese founded Colonia del Sacramento in 1680, and the Spanish founded Montevideo in the 1720s. After struggles between Spain, Portugal, Brazil and Argentina, Uruguay became independent, declaring it in 1825 and gaining recognition in 1828."],["A welfare state","In the early 1900s, President José Batlle y Ordóñez introduced reforms such as free public education, workers’ rights, pensions and the separation of church and state, making Uruguay one of the first welfare states in the Americas."],["Dictatorship and democracy","A military dictatorship ruled from 1973 to 1985, imprisoning and torturing thousands of opponents. Democracy returned in 1985, and Uruguay is now regularly ranked among the most democratic countries in the world."]],
k:[["Capital","Montevideo"],["Population","About 3.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 176,000 km²"],["Highest point","Cerro Catedral, 514 m"],["Currency","Uruguayan peso"]],
d:[["1680","The Portuguese found Colonia del Sacramento"],["1726","Montevideo is founded"],["1825","Uruguay declares independence"],["1930","Uruguay hosts and wins the first World Cup"],["1973","A military dictatorship begins"],["2007","Uruguay begins giving a laptop to every schoolchild"],["2013","Uruguay legalizes the cannabis market and same-sex marriage"]],
s2:[["Football","In 1930 Uruguay hosted and won the first FIFA World Cup, beating Argentina in the final. In 1950 it shocked the world by beating host Brazil in the decisive match at the Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro, a victory called the Maracanazo. For a small country, it has produced many great players."],["Social laws","Uruguay has been a pioneer in social policies, legalizing divorce early in the 1900s and, in 2013, becoming the first country to create a legal, regulated market for cannabis and one of the first in Latin America to legalize same-sex marriage. These laws have supporters and critics."],["A laptop for every child","In 2007 Uruguay began Plan Ceibal, giving a free laptop to every child and teacher in public primary schools, one of the first countries to do so nationally, and connecting schools to the internet."],["Clean energy","Within about a decade, Uruguay transformed its electricity system, building wind farms and solar parks alongside hydropower. Renewable sources now provide most of its electricity, making it a model for clean energy."],["José Mujica","José Mujica, a former guerrilla imprisoned for years under the dictatorship, served as president from 2010 to 2015. He became internationally famous for his simple lifestyle, living on a small farm, driving an old car and giving most of his salary to charity, and was nicknamed the world’s poorest president. He died in 2025."],["Mate and asado","Uruguayans drink mate, a herbal tea sipped through a metal straw from a gourd, carrying thermos flasks of hot water everywhere. Asado, barbecued beef, is a beloved social tradition, and candombe drumming, of African origin, fills the streets during Carnival."]],
sp:["The Maracanazo","On 16 July 1950, almost 200,000 people packed the Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro for the final match of the World Cup between Brazil and Uruguay. Brazil needed only a draw to win, and newspapers had already printed headlines celebrating Brazil as champions. Brazil scored first, but Uruguay equalized, and with 11 minutes left Alcides Ghiggia scored again for Uruguay. The huge crowd fell silent. Uruguay won 2–1. The defeat became known in Brazil as the Maracanazo, and Ghiggia later said that only three people had ever silenced the Maracanã: Frank Sinatra, the Pope and himself."],
g:[["Pampas","Grassy plains of South America"],["Welfare state","A state that provides social services and protections"],["Mate","A herbal tea popular in South America"],["Candombe","An Afro-Uruguayan drumming tradition"],["Estuary","Where a river meets the sea"]],
f:["Uruguay won the first World Cup in 1930.","It has more cattle than people.","Most of its electricity comes from renewable sources."],
fun:"Uruguay silenced nearly 200,000 Brazilian fans by winning the 1950 World Cup decider."});

ADDART({id:"irelandcountry",t:"Ireland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"☘️",w:"Republic of Ireland",r:["ukcountry","celts","christ","eu","poetry","potato"],
p:[["The Emerald Isle","Ireland is famous for its green landscapes, castles and coast."],["Great writers","Ireland has produced many celebrated writers and four Nobel laureates in literature."],["From famine to prosperity","Once devastated by famine, Ireland is now one of Europe’s richest countries."]],
l:"Ireland, officially the Republic of Ireland, occupies most of the island of Ireland in northwestern Europe, with about 5.4 million people; the northeast of the island, Northern Ireland, is part of the United Kingdom. Its capital is Dublin. Known as the Emerald Isle for its green countryside, Ireland has ancient monuments, a Celtic heritage and a rich tradition of literature, music and storytelling. It endured centuries of English and British rule and the Great Famine of the 1840s, which killed about a million people and caused mass emigration. Independent since 1922, Ireland joined the European Union and became one of Europe’s fastest-growing economies.",
s:[["Land","Ireland has a central lowland of farms, lakes and bogs, ringed by coastal hills and mountains. Its mild, rainy climate, shaped by the Atlantic Ocean, keeps the land green. The Cliffs of Moher and the Giant’s Causeway, in Northern Ireland, are famous sights."],["Ancient and Christian Ireland","Newgrange, a huge passage tomb built around 3200 BCE, is older than the pyramids of Giza. Celtic culture spread later, and St Patrick brought Christianity in the 400s. Irish monasteries preserved learning in the early Middle Ages, producing illuminated manuscripts such as the Book of Kells."],["English rule","Vikings founded Dublin in the 800s, and Anglo-Norman knights invaded in 1169, beginning centuries of English involvement. English and Scottish Protestants were settled on Irish land, and Catholics faced harsh laws. In 1801 Ireland was united with Great Britain."],["Independence","After the Easter Rising of 1916 and the War of Independence from 1919 to 1921, the Irish Free State was created in 1922, while Northern Ireland remained in the United Kingdom. A civil war followed over the treaty. Ireland declared itself a republic in 1949."]],
k:[["Capital","Dublin"],["Population","About 5.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 70,300 km²"],["Highest point","Carrauntoohil, 1,038 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["c. 3200 BCE","Newgrange is built"],["841","Vikings found Dublin"],["1845","The Great Famine begins"],["1916","The Easter Rising takes place in Dublin"],["1922","The Irish Free State is created"],["1973","Ireland joins the European Economic Community"],["1998","The Good Friday Agreement is signed"]],
s2:[["The Great Famine","From 1845 to 1852, a disease called potato blight destroyed the potato crop, the main food of millions of poor Irish people. About a million people died of hunger and disease, and more than a million emigrated, many to the United States, Canada, Britain and Australia. Food continued to be exported from Ireland during the famine, which many blame on British policies. Ireland’s population has never returned to its pre-famine level."],["The Irish around the world","Millions of people worldwide have Irish ancestry, especially in the United States, Britain, Australia and Canada. St Patrick’s Day, 17 March, is celebrated around the world with parades and the color green."],["Northern Ireland and peace","From the late 1960s, a conflict known as the Troubles between mainly Catholic nationalists seeking a united Ireland and mainly Protestant unionists wishing to remain in the United Kingdom killed more than 3,500 people. The Good Friday Agreement of 1998 brought a peace process and power-sharing government."],["Literature","Ireland has produced writers such as Jonathan Swift, Oscar Wilde, James Joyce, Samuel Beckett and four Nobel Prize winners in literature: W. B. Yeats, George Bernard Shaw, Beckett and Seamus Heaney. Irish storytelling, poetry and drama are celebrated worldwide."],["Modern Ireland","From the 1990s, Ireland’s economy grew rapidly in a boom called the Celtic Tiger, attracting technology and pharmaceutical companies. It suffered a severe crash in 2008 but recovered. Ireland has also changed socially, becoming in 2015 the first country to approve same-sex marriage by popular vote."],["Language and culture","Irish, or Gaelic, is the first official language and is taught in schools, though English is spoken by most people; Irish-speaking areas are called the Gaeltacht. Traditional music, dancing and sports such as hurling and Gaelic football are beloved. Many Filipino nurses and health workers live and work in Ireland."]],
sp:["The midwinter sunrise at Newgrange","Newgrange, a great mound of stone and earth in the Boyne Valley, was built around 3200 BCE by Stone Age farmers. Above its entrance is a small opening called a roof box. For a few days around the winter solstice, the shortest day of the year, the rising sun shines through the roof box and along a passage about 19 meters long, lighting up the inner chamber for about 17 minutes. The builders aligned it precisely more than 5,000 years ago. Today so many people want to witness the event that places inside are given out by lottery."],
g:[["Celtic","Relating to the Celts, an ancient people of Europe"],["Passage tomb","An ancient tomb with a stone passage leading to a chamber"],["Potato blight","A plant disease that destroyed potato crops"],["Troubles","The conflict in Northern Ireland from the late 1960s to 1998"],["Gaeltacht","Irish-speaking areas of Ireland"]],
f:["Newgrange is older than the pyramids of Giza.","The Great Famine killed about a million people.","Ireland approved same-sex marriage by popular vote in 2015."],
fun:"The midwinter sun lights up the inside of Newgrange, a tomb built over 5,000 years ago."});

ADDART({id:"austriacountry",t:"Austria",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎻",w:"Austria",r:["alps","mozart","beethoven","germanycountry","danube","eu"],
p:[["Land of music","Austria was home to Mozart, Haydn, Schubert and the Strauss waltzes."],["The Habsburg Empire","For centuries, Vienna was the capital of a great European empire."],["Alpine country","The Alps cover most of Austria."]],
l:"Austria is a landlocked country in central Europe, with about 9.2 million people. Its capital is Vienna. For more than six centuries, Austria was the heart of the Habsburg monarchy, one of Europe’s great powers, and Vienna became a center of music, art, science and philosophy, home to Mozart, Beethoven, Schubert, the Strauss family, Sigmund Freud and Gustav Klimt. After the empire collapsed in 1918, Austria became a republic, was annexed by Nazi Germany in 1938 and regained independence in 1955 as a neutral country. Today it is a prosperous member of the European Union, famous for the Alps, skiing, coffee houses and classical music.",
s:[["Land","About two-thirds of Austria is covered by the Alps, with peaks such as the Grossglockner, glaciers and lakes. The Danube River flows through the north, past Linz and Vienna. Eastern Austria has lowlands, vineyards and the shallow Lake Neusiedl."],["The Habsburgs","The Habsburg family ruled Austria from 1278 until 1918. Through marriages and wars, they built an empire including Hungary, Bohemia, parts of Italy and the Balkans and, for a time, Spain and its colonies, including the Philippines under Spain’s Habsburg kings. Vienna resisted Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683."],["The end of the empire","In 1867 the empire became Austria-Hungary. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the throne, in Sarajevo in 1914 triggered World War I. Defeat in 1918 ended the monarchy, and Austria became a small republic."],["Anschluss and occupation","In March 1938 Nazi Germany annexed Austria in the Anschluss, welcomed by many Austrians but opposed by others. Austrian Jews were persecuted, and many were murdered in the Holocaust. After World War II, Austria was occupied by the Allies until the State Treaty of 1955 restored its independence."]],
k:[["Capital","Vienna"],["Population","About 9.2 million (2024)"],["Area","About 83,900 km²"],["Highest point","Grossglockner, 3,798 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["996","The name Ostarrîchi, an early form of Austria, first appears"],["1278","The Habsburgs begin ruling Austria"],["1683","Vienna resists an Ottoman siege"],["1815","The Congress of Vienna redraws the map of Europe"],["1918","Austria becomes a republic"],["1938","Nazi Germany annexes Austria"],["1955","Austria regains independence and declares neutrality"]],
s2:[["Music","Vienna is often called the city of music. Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven, Schubert, Brahms and Mahler lived and worked there. Johann Strauss II composed waltzes such as The Blue Danube, and the Vienna Philharmonic’s New Year’s Concert is watched by millions around the world. Salzburg, Mozart’s birthplace, hosts a famous music festival."],["Science and ideas","Austria produced influential thinkers and scientists, including the psychoanalyst Sigmund Freud, the physicist Erwin Schrödinger, the geneticist Gregor Mendel, who worked in Brno in the Austrian Empire, and the philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein."],["Neutrality","When Austria regained independence in 1955, it declared permanent neutrality, celebrated on its National Day, 26 October. Vienna hosts many international organizations, including a United Nations headquarters, the International Atomic Energy Agency and OPEC."],["Coffee houses","Viennese coffee houses, with marble tables, newspapers and pastries such as Sachertorte and apple strudel, have been meeting places for writers, artists and thinkers for centuries. UNESCO recognized Viennese coffee house culture as intangible cultural heritage."],["Art and architecture","Vienna has grand palaces such as Schönbrunn, the Habsburg summer residence, and art from many periods, including the golden paintings of Gustav Klimt, such as The Kiss. Its social housing estates are admired around the world."],["The Alps and skiing","Austria is one of the world’s top destinations for skiing and mountain sports, and Austrian skiers are among the most successful in Winter Olympics and World Cup races. Mountain villages and Alpine traditions, such as yodeling, attract visitors year-round."]],
sp:["The Blue Danube","In 1866 Austria suffered a painful defeat in a war with Prussia, and the mood in Vienna was gloomy. The next year, the composer Johann Strauss II wrote a waltz for a choral society, originally with words, called On the Beautiful Blue Danube. At first it was only modestly successful, but in its instrumental version it soon became a worldwide hit and an unofficial anthem of Austria. Every New Year’s Day, the Vienna Philharmonic plays it as an encore at its famous concert. In the 1968 film 2001: A Space Odyssey, it accompanied spaceships gliding through space."],
g:[["Habsburg","The royal family that ruled Austria for centuries"],["Anschluss","The annexation of Austria by Nazi Germany in 1938"],["Neutrality","Not taking sides in wars"],["Waltz","A ballroom dance in three-four time"],["Alps","A mountain range in central Europe"]],
f:["The Habsburgs ruled Austria from 1278 to 1918.","Mozart was born in Salzburg.","Austria declared neutrality in 1955."],
fun:"The Blue Danube waltz accompanied spaceships in the film 2001: A Space Odyssey."});

ADDART({id:"finlandcountry",t:"Finland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌲",w:"Finland",r:["swedencountry","norwaycountry","russiacountry","arctic","education","eu"],
p:[["Land of lakes","Finland has around 188,000 lakes and vast forests."],["Happiest country","Finland has repeatedly been ranked the happiest country in the world."],["Sauna nation","Finland has millions of saunas, a central part of its culture."]],
l:"Finland is a country in northern Europe, between Sweden, Norway and Russia, with about 5.6 million people. Its capital is Helsinki. Known as the land of a thousand lakes, it actually has around 188,000 lakes and is mostly covered by forest, with Lapland in the far north above the Arctic Circle. Finland was part of Sweden for centuries and then of the Russian Empire before becoming independent in 1917. It is famous for its education system, technology, design, saunas and the Sámi culture of Lapland, and it has repeatedly been ranked the happiest country in the world. In 2023 it joined NATO.",
s:[["Land","Finland is mostly low and flat, shaped by Ice Age glaciers that left thousands of lakes, islands and ridges. Forests of pine, spruce and birch cover about three-quarters of the land. In Lapland, the sun does not set for weeks in summer and barely rises in winter, when the northern lights glow."],["Swedish and Russian rule","From the Middle Ages, Finland was part of the Swedish kingdom, and Swedish remains an official language alongside Finnish. In 1809 Russia conquered Finland, which became an autonomous Grand Duchy within the Russian Empire, with its own parliament and laws."],["Independence","After the Russian Revolution, Finland declared independence on 6 December 1917. A brief civil war followed in 1918. During World War II, Finland fought the Soviet Union in the Winter War of 1939 to 1940 and the Continuation War, keeping its independence though losing territory."],["Women’s rights","In 1906 Finland became the first country in Europe to give women the right to vote and the first in the world to allow women to stand for parliament. In 1907, 19 women were elected to its parliament."]],
k:[["Capital","Helsinki"],["Population","About 5.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 338,000 km²"],["Highest point","Halti, 1,324 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["1809","Finland becomes part of the Russian Empire"],["1835","The national epic, the Kalevala, is published"],["1906","Finnish women gain the right to vote and stand for parliament"],["1917","Finland becomes independent"],["1939","The Winter War with the Soviet Union begins"],["1995","Finland joins the European Union"],["2023","Finland joins NATO"]],
s2:[["Education","Finland’s schools are admired worldwide. Children start formal school at seven, have short school days, little homework and few standardized tests, and teachers are highly trained and respected. Finnish students ranked near the top of international tests in the 2000s, attracting visitors from around the world to study its approach."],["Happiness","Finland has topped the World Happiness Report every year since 2018. Researchers point to trust in government and neighbors, low corruption, strong social services, work-life balance and closeness to nature, though Finns themselves often describe their happiness as quiet contentment."],["Saunas","Finland has around three million saunas for its 5.6 million people, in homes, apartment buildings, offices and even a Burger King restaurant. Finns relax in the hot steam and then cool off in a lake or snow. Finnish sauna culture is recognized by UNESCO."],["Technology and design","Finland is home to companies such as Nokia, once the world’s largest mobile phone maker, and gave the world the Linux operating system, created by Linus Torvalds in 1991, and the Angry Birds game. Finnish design, from Marimekko fabrics to Alvar Aalto’s furniture and buildings, is world-renowned."],["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of Lapland and the far north of Norway, Sweden and Russia, known for reindeer herding and joik singing. Finland has a Sámi Parliament, and efforts continue to protect Sámi languages and rights."],["Culture","The Kalevala, a collection of Finnish folk poetry compiled by Elias Lönnrot in 1835, inspired Finnish national identity and the composer Jean Sibelius, whose Finlandia became a patriotic anthem. Tove Jansson’s Moomins, created in 1945, are loved worldwide."]],
sp:["The Winter War","On 30 November 1939, the Soviet Union, with a far larger army, invaded Finland after the Finns refused to give up territory. Many expected Finland to fall within weeks. Instead, Finnish soldiers, dressed in white and moving swiftly on skis through snowy forests in temperatures as low as −40 °C, fought fiercely, defeating Soviet columns many times their size. Although Finland eventually had to give up about a tenth of its territory in the peace treaty of March 1940, it kept its independence. The Winter War became a symbol of Finnish sisu, a word meaning determination and courage in the face of hardship."],
g:[["Lapland","The far northern region of Finland"],["Grand Duchy","A territory ruled by a grand duke"],["Sauna","A small room or building for hot-air or steam bathing"],["Sisu","A Finnish word for determination and resilience"],["Kalevala","Finland’s national epic poem"]],
f:["Finland has around 188,000 lakes.","Finnish women were the first in the world allowed to stand for parliament.","Finland joined NATO in 2023."],
fun:"Finland has around three million saunas for 5.6 million people."});

